{"parentUuid":"70ba44af-8692-44c8-8f3d-546927ce1c14","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZZge7Q8sQ4ZYn2ujbP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P4HP4y7dGXZRxCkaZhqYEr","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && head -c 5000 fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md; ls _tmp/ _renders/ 2>/dev/null | head -30","description":"Read fix log head and list tmp dirs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4183,"cache_read_input_tokens":101242,"output_tokens":183,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":25},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4183},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":183,"cache_read_input_tokens":101242,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4183,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01P4HP4y7dGXZRxCkaZhqYEr":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && head -c 5000 fixes_supertraining_ch4-3-4_applied.md; ls _tmp/ _renders/ 2>/dev/null | head -30","description":"Read fix log head and list tmp dirs"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZZgNVHXgaaspvwGSFm","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d873e4ee-9fbb-4698-be37-de35e2a84916","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:03.527Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d873e4ee-9fbb-4698-be37-de35e2a84916","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P4HP4y7dGXZRxCkaZhqYEr","type":"tool_result","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.3-4.4 (Ch4-3-4), 23 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 8 entry FIX rows plus 1 `_book.md` FIX).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 with PyMuPDF. I rendered page images for PDF 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 to `staging/_renders/fix_p<N>.png` and opened PDF 256 (Fig 4.22 and its passage), PDF 257 (power equations, Eq 4.1) and PDF 264 (Table 4.2). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 23 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`; only the 8 flagged entries and `_book.md` were edited. No new entries were written, so the scope file is unchanged.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | **Title** is now \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\". The slug is unchanged, so the one inbound link (RFD entry) still resolves.<br>**What it is:** the scope line now reads \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\". It says the authors' constant-force statement is imprecise and is not part of that scope. Power is now quoted as \"The rate of doing work at any instant\" (PDF 257 image).<br>**Why it works:** rewritten in full. The old first sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it ... wrong instantaneous picture\") endorsed the constant-force claim in the writer's voice. Now:<br>(a) The authors' two sentences are quoted with the locator (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned\"; \"If the force is not constant ... equation (4.1)\").<br>(b) The scope sentence follows.<br>(c) An Inference gives the physics: dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval. What fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity (the average of a product is not the product of averages, and the peaks need not coincide). The authors' \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\" is quoted as the point the warning targets.<br>**When NOT:** \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" is replaced by \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"<br>**Confidence:** `confidence: proven` kept, scoped to the instantaneous identity only (textbook mechanics, which the brief allows as `proven`). |\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When NOT:** the Inference \"when the criteria conflict (for example ... breaks the regime of work ...)\" is replaced. The new text says the book gives no ranking among the criteria, then quotes three sentences from PDF 256 / p.245: the \"incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence, \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\", and that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\". Then: \"So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | **What it is:** the Fig 4.22 paragraph now quotes the full \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence. It then adds the dropped \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). It also quotes the text's own account of the two partners (\"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\"; \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors\"). The caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | **Why it works:** first sentence replaced. It now quotes the authors' \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity)\" and \"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\", plus the next sentence on the page, \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" (PDF 255 / p.244), so the size-and-speed point is in their words. \"standard fluid mechanics\" is removed. The new Inference reads: \"velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\". It is merged with the existing Inference, so there is one, not two. |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When to apply:** the populations are now labelled: \"(Inference: f_renders/:\nfix_p255.png\nfix_p256.png\nfix_p257.png\nfix_p258.png\nfix_p260.png\nfix_p263.png\nfix_p264.png\nr2_p253.png\nr2_p255.png\nr2_p257.png\nr2_p260.png\nr2_p261.png\nr2_p262.png\nr2_p263.png\nr2_p264.png\nrev_p252.png\nrev_p253.png\nrev_p254.png\nrev_p255.png\nrev_p256.png\nrev_p257.png\nrev_p258.png\nrev_p259.png\nrev_p260.png\nrev_p261.png\nrev_p262.png\nrev_p263.png\nrev_p264.png\nrev_p265.png","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f1c00efe-cc2a-43a5-92a5-cda3594a257f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:03.701Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 4.3-4.4 (Ch4-3-4), 23 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch4-3-4_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 8 entry FIX rows plus 1 `_book.md` FIX).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 254, 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 with PyMuPDF. I rendered page images for PDF 255, 256, 257, 258, 260, 263 and 264 to `staging/_renders/fix_p<N>.png` and opened PDF 256 (Fig 4.22 and its passage), PDF 257 (power equations, Eq 4.1) and PDF 264 (Table 4.2). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 23 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch4-3-4.txt`; only the 8 flagged entries and `_book.md` were edited. No new entries were written, so the scope file is unchanged.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--power-equals-force-times-velocity-only-when-force-is-constant.md | **Title** is now \"The Authors Warn That P = F x V Is Misapplied: Average or Peak Force Times Velocity Is Not Power When Force Varies; Use the Force-Time Curve, a Force Plate or High-Speed Video\". The slug is unchanged, so the one inbound link (RFD entry) still resolves.<br>**What it is:** the scope line now reads \"Scope of `proven`: only the identity P = dW/dt = F·v, which holds instant by instant for any force (Inference, see Why it works)\". It says the authors' constant-force statement is imprecise and is not part of that scope. Power is now quoted as \"The rate of doing work at any instant\" (PDF 257 image).<br>**Why it works:** rewritten in full. The old first sentence (\"This is calculus, and the authors state it ... wrong instantaneous picture\") endorsed the constant-force claim in the writer's voice. Now:<br>(a) The authors' two sentences are quoted with the locator (\"if F is constant over the interval concerned\"; \"If the force is not constant ... equation (4.1)\").<br>(b) The scope sentence follows.<br>(c) An Inference gives the physics: dW/dt = F·ds/dt = F·v at every instant. Constant force is needed only when one force value stands for a whole interval. What fails is multiplying an average or peak force by an average or peak velocity (the average of a product is not the product of averages, and the peaks need not coincide). The authors' \"It is inadequate to refer to average or peak force alone\" is quoted as the point the warning targets.<br>**When NOT:** \"The physics is sound; the wording around it is loose.\" is replaced by \"The authors' statement that P = F.V needs constant force is imprecise (see What it is); their practical warning against single average or peak values stands.\"<br>**Confidence:** `confidence: proven` kept, scoped to the instantaneous identity only (textbook mechanics, which the brief allows as `proven`). |\n| framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When NOT:** the Inference \"when the criteria conflict (for example ... breaks the regime of work ...)\" is replaced. The new text says the book gives no ranking among the criteria, then quotes three sentences from PDF 256 / p.245: the \"incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence, \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\", and that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\". Then: \"So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\" |\n| heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle.md | **What it is:** the Fig 4.22 paragraph now quotes the full \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22)\" sentence. It then adds the dropped \"However, this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria\" (PDF 256 / p.245). It also quotes the text's own account of the two partners (\"simulates the vertical loading which sprinting normally imposes\"; \"intended to strengthen the power of the hip flexors\"). The caption and \"applied carefully\" sentences are kept. |\n| heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release.md | **Why it works:** first sentence replaced. It now quotes the authors' \"increases rapidly with velocity (actually the square of the velocity)\" and \"allows us to use these devices more knowledgeably\", plus the next sentence on the page, \"Naturally, running speed determines the degree of drag resistance\" (PDF 255 / p.244), so the size-and-speed point is in their words. \"standard fluid mechanics\" is removed. The new Inference reads: \"velocity-squared drag is the textbook drag equation, and this relation is the only part of the entry that could rate `proven`\". It is merged with the existing Inference, so there is one, not two. |\n| claim--parachute-resistance-tradeoffs-for-dynamic-correspondence.md | **When to apply:** the populations are now labelled: \"(Inference: f_renders/:\nfix_p255.png\nfix_p256.png\nfix_p257.png\nfix_p258.png\nfix_p260.png\nfix_p263.png\nfix_p264.png\nr2_p253.png\nr2_p255.png\nr2_p257.png\nr2_p260.png\nr2_p261.png\nr2_p262.png\nr2_p263.png\nr2_p264.png\nrev_p252.png\nrev_p253.png\nrev_p254.png\nrev_p255.png\nrev_p256.png\nrev_p257.png\nrev_p258.png\nrev_p259.png\nrev_p260.png\nrev_p261.png\nrev_p262.png\nrev_p263.png\nrev_p264.png\nrev_p265.png","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d873e4ee-9fbb-4698-be37-de35e2a84916","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
