{"parentUuid":"60937713-5e5f-4aaf-b956-1000e1e6579b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZZrCaWLUtSBrahYN6p","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RsdxdWNXDmFVuSwUSKuFo2","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && ls -la _tmp/ ; cd library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && cat claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals.md; echo; echo XXXX; cat framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md","description":"Check lint script and read two style references"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2315,"cache_read_input_tokens":105425,"output_tokens":278,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":72},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2315},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":105425,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2315,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2315,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RsdxdWNXDmFVuSwUSKuFo2":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging && ls -la _tmp/ ; cd library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && cat claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals.md; echo; echo XXXX; cat framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence.md","description":"Check lint script and read two style references"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZZquy9TrRdt83aZr2Y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1a5df967-9c15-4151-8d18-ad7de145c44f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:06.729Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1a5df967-9c15-4151-8d18-ad7de145c44f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RsdxdWNXDmFVuSwUSKuFo2","type":"tool_result","content":"total 256\ndrwxr-xr-x@  8 kristinarash  staff     256 Oct  3 10:45 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 78 kristinarash  staff    2496 Oct  3 10:39 ..\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff  106720 Oct  3 10:45 ch5b_text.txt\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff    3379 Oct  3 10:38 links_mac.py\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff    2264 Oct  3 10:38 lint_mac.py\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff      20 Oct  3 10:41 memsearch-index-2026-10-03.log\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff    3648 Oct  3 10:04 promote_mac.py\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff     935 Oct  3 10:39 sync_mac.py\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.12\"\ntopic: concurrent strength and endurance training\ncategory: [concurrent-training, programming, fiber-type]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Continuous Circuit Training Does Not Simultaneously Build Strength and Endurance; Interval Circuit Training Is a Better Fit\n\n**What it is** — Research does not support the claim (made by some instructors and machine\nmanufacturers) that continuous circuit training (CCT) simultaneously develops\ncardiovascular endurance and strength. Interval circuit training (ICT: high-intensity\nloading with regular rest intervals) is described as more suitable for developing strength\nand strength-endurance together. Supporting evidence cited: prolonged low-intensity\nendurance training performed alongside strength training in the same phase seriously\ncompromises strength/power development, partly because fast-twitch fibres can start\nbehaving like slow-twitch fibres under prolonged low-intensity load; muscle biopsy studies\nof distance runners' gastrocnemius after a marathon show necrosis and inflammation\ndetectable at least 7 days later, an abnormality not seen in comparable biopsies from\nweightlifters, sprinters, or rowers after strenuous sessions. (PDF p. 70, printed p. 59.)\n\n**Why it works** — The author gives one mechanism: fast-twitch fibres can start behaving\nlike slow-twitch fibres under prolonged low-intensity work (printed 59). Inference:\ncontinuous, moderate-intensity circuit work biases fibre behavior toward slow-twitch\ncharacteristics, a different and partly conflicting neuromuscular target from the\nhigh-force, high-velocity, largely fast-twitch demands of strength and power work; ICT's\nrest intervals let each work bout stay in a higher-intensity, more strength/power-specific\nzone instead of drifting toward an endurance stimulus.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** relevant when designing off-season/GPP programming\nthat needs to develop both conditioning and strength: the text's recommendation is to\nprescribe cardiovascular training separately, in limited amounts, early in the off-season,\nwith higher-intensity resistance training emphasized later, and to use interval-style\n(work/rest) circuits rather than continuous circuits when the goal includes\nstrength-endurance. **Eric's baseline:** this converges with the department's own\nconditioning priority stack for football (alactic and aerobic capacity/power first, with\nglycolytic conditioning absorbed incidentally rather than programmed as a primary target;\n`method-baseline.md`, \"Conditioning and energy systems,\" citing `class-4-2026.md pp.34,\n39`), though the baseline material does not itself cite this fibre-type mechanism.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Inference:** this is evidence against combining continuous,\nprolonged low-intensity work with concurrent strength training in the same block, not an\nargument against all conditioning; short, high-intensity interval conditioning is treated\nas more compatible with strength/power goals. The gastrocnemius necrosis finding is\nspecific to marathon-distance running, not necessarily to all forms of aerobic\nconditioning used in team-sport contexts. The authors also state that ICT's own\nsub-maximal nature \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently to meet the specific\nneeds of strength and speed sports, except possibly during the early off-season\" (PDF p.\n91, printed p. 80); ICT is a better fit than CCT for concurrent goals, not a substitute for\ndedicated strength/power training.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** programming continuous, moderate-pace circuit stations as a\n\"two birds, one stone\" strength-and-conditioning solution is not well supported by this\nevidence; separating a short conditioning block from resistance training, or using\ninterval-format circuits with real rest between high-intensity efforts, is the better-\nsupported approach.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--fiber-recruitment-not-simply-graded-by-force]]; [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]] (4.4, the other direction of interaction: the authors claim strength training raises haemoglobin and myoglobin and aids distance sport; rated contested)\n\nXXXX\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3\"\ntopic: dynamic correspondence criteria\ncategory: [specificity, exercise-selection, transfer, special-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence: Five Criteria for Choosing Special Strength Exercises\n\n**What it is** — The authors open section 4.3 by arguing that \"All sporting movement is specific and goal-directed,\" so the strength shown in each movement is also specific, and \"One should not refer to strength in general, but only in the context of the given task.\" The means and methods of strength training should therefore give \"an adequate conditioning regime for the motor system in the special exercises.\" The foundation is the principle of dynamic correspondence: training means for a given sport should be chosen to enhance the required motor qualities in terms of five criteria (PDF p. 252 / printed p. 241):\n1. the amplitude and direction of movement (4.3.1);\n2. the accentuated region of force production (4.3.2);\n3. the dynamics of the effort (4.3.3);\n4. the rate and time of maximum force production (4.3.4);\n5. the regime of muscular work (4.3.5).\n\nSection 4.3.6 closes the set by stating that the character of muscular work is learned from analysis of the athlete's movements, and that \"success in selecting the means of special strength training is determined by a thorough knowledge of the biomechanics of the movements\" (PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the specificity argument above: strength is always expressed in a particular task, so a training means only transfers to the extent that it reproduces the task's conditions on these five dimensions. They do not give a physiological mechanism for transfer in this section; each sub-criterion gives its own reasoning (see Related). Inference: the five criteria read as the exercise-selection version of the broader specificity dimensions the book lists in 1.4, narrowed to features a coach can inspect in a movement.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the five criteria as a checklist when choosing or designing a special strength exercise for a sport skill: check each criterion in turn rather than judging transfer by whether the exercise \"looks like\" the sport. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" Tagged **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103). Inference: this entry is the primary-source version of the list Eric already teaches in HHPR 5355, so it can be cited directly for the criteria's original wording.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The criteria select special strength exercises; the authors say in the opening of the next chapter that this selection is \"only part of the process\" and that methods are a separate task (cross-reference only, PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They are not an argument against general preparation: the text gives no rule here for how much general work an athlete needs. The authors offer the criteria as reasoned principles with illustrative examples, not as the result of controlled transfer studies, so a high correspondence score does not by itself prove that an exercise will transfer. The text gives no ranking among the criteria. The authors say that following the amplitude and direction requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22),\" but that \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria,\" and they add that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples for each criterion: the rower and shot putter whose shoulder movements have similar amplitude but opposite force direction (Fig 4.17, PDF p. 253 / printed p. 242); the standing hip-flexor machine that misses the sprinter's range (PDF p. 253 / printed p. 242); the boxer and shot putter who should do the same pulley thrust with different loads and tempos (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (Ch1.4, the general specificity dimensions these criteria narrow down), [[heuristic--match-amplitude-direction-and-line-of-resistance-to-the-sport]], [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]], [[heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter]], [[heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]] (Evans applies the same five criteria to a wrestling takedown; agreement), [[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--weight-room-transfer-limited-without-dynamic-correspondence]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"364db404-73c2-4bb9-8325-52bd6cf6ec0b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:07.098Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"total 256\ndrwxr-xr-x@  8 kristinarash  staff     256 Oct  3 10:45 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 78 kristinarash  staff    2496 Oct  3 10:39 ..\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff  106720 Oct  3 10:45 ch5b_text.txt\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff    3379 Oct  3 10:38 links_mac.py\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff    2264 Oct  3 10:38 lint_mac.py\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff      20 Oct  3 10:41 memsearch-index-2026-10-03.log\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff    3648 Oct  3 10:04 promote_mac.py\n-rw-r--r--@  1 kristinarash  staff     935 Oct  3 10:39 sync_mac.py\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"1.12\"\ntopic: concurrent strength and endurance training\ncategory: [concurrent-training, programming, fiber-type]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Continuous Circuit Training Does Not Simultaneously Build Strength and Endurance; Interval Circuit Training Is a Better Fit\n\n**What it is** — Research does not support the claim (made by some instructors and machine\nmanufacturers) that continuous circuit training (CCT) simultaneously develops\ncardiovascular endurance and strength. Interval circuit training (ICT: high-intensity\nloading with regular rest intervals) is described as more suitable for developing strength\nand strength-endurance together. Supporting evidence cited: prolonged low-intensity\nendurance training performed alongside strength training in the same phase seriously\ncompromises strength/power development, partly because fast-twitch fibres can start\nbehaving like slow-twitch fibres under prolonged low-intensity load; muscle biopsy studies\nof distance runners' gastrocnemius after a marathon show necrosis and inflammation\ndetectable at least 7 days later, an abnormality not seen in comparable biopsies from\nweightlifters, sprinters, or rowers after strenuous sessions. (PDF p. 70, printed p. 59.)\n\n**Why it works** — The author gives one mechanism: fast-twitch fibres can start behaving\nlike slow-twitch fibres under prolonged low-intensity work (printed 59). Inference:\ncontinuous, moderate-intensity circuit work biases fibre behavior toward slow-twitch\ncharacteristics, a different and partly conflicting neuromuscular target from the\nhigh-force, high-velocity, largely fast-twitch demands of strength and power work; ICT's\nrest intervals let each work bout stay in a higher-intensity, more strength/power-specific\nzone instead of drifting toward an endurance stimulus.\n\n**When to apply** — **Inference:** relevant when designing off-season/GPP programming\nthat needs to develop both conditioning and strength: the text's recommendation is to\nprescribe cardiovascular training separately, in limited amounts, early in the off-season,\nwith higher-intensity resistance training emphasized later, and to use interval-style\n(work/rest) circuits rather than continuous circuits when the goal includes\nstrength-endurance. **Eric's baseline:** this converges with the department's own\nconditioning priority stack for football (alactic and aerobic capacity/power first, with\nglycolytic conditioning absorbed incidentally rather than programmed as a primary target;\n`method-baseline.md`, \"Conditioning and energy systems,\" citing `class-4-2026.md pp.34,\n39`), though the baseline material does not itself cite this fibre-type mechanism.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Inference:** this is evidence against combining continuous,\nprolonged low-intensity work with concurrent strength training in the same block, not an\nargument against all conditioning; short, high-intensity interval conditioning is treated\nas more compatible with strength/power goals. The gastrocnemius necrosis finding is\nspecific to marathon-distance running, not necessarily to all forms of aerobic\nconditioning used in team-sport contexts. The authors also state that ICT's own\nsub-maximal nature \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently to meet the specific\nneeds of strength and speed sports, except possibly during the early off-season\" (PDF p.\n91, printed p. 80); ICT is a better fit than CCT for concurrent goals, not a substitute for\ndedicated strength/power training.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** programming continuous, moderate-pace circuit stations as a\n\"two birds, one stone\" strength-and-conditioning solution is not well supported by this\nevidence; separating a short conditioning block from resistance training, or using\ninterval-format circuits with real rest between high-intensity efforts, is the better-\nsupported approach.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--fiber-recruitment-not-simply-graded-by-force]]; [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]] (4.4, the other direction of interaction: the authors claim strength training raises haemoglobin and myoglobin and aids distance sport; rated contested)\n\nXXXX\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"4.3\"\ntopic: dynamic correspondence criteria\ncategory: [specificity, exercise-selection, transfer, special-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence: Five Criteria for Choosing Special Strength Exercises\n\n**What it is** — The authors open section 4.3 by arguing that \"All sporting movement is specific and goal-directed,\" so the strength shown in each movement is also specific, and \"One should not refer to strength in general, but only in the context of the given task.\" The means and methods of strength training should therefore give \"an adequate conditioning regime for the motor system in the special exercises.\" The foundation is the principle of dynamic correspondence: training means for a given sport should be chosen to enhance the required motor qualities in terms of five criteria (PDF p. 252 / printed p. 241):\n1. the amplitude and direction of movement (4.3.1);\n2. the accentuated region of force production (4.3.2);\n3. the dynamics of the effort (4.3.3);\n4. the rate and time of maximum force production (4.3.4);\n5. the regime of muscular work (4.3.5).\n\nSection 4.3.6 closes the set by stating that the character of muscular work is learned from analysis of the athlete's movements, and that \"success in selecting the means of special strength training is determined by a thorough knowledge of the biomechanics of the movements\" (PDF p. 259 / printed p. 248).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the specificity argument above: strength is always expressed in a particular task, so a training means only transfers to the extent that it reproduces the task's conditions on these five dimensions. They do not give a physiological mechanism for transfer in this section; each sub-criterion gives its own reasoning (see Related). Inference: the five criteria read as the exercise-selection version of the broader specificity dimensions the book lists in 1.4, narrowed to features a coach can inspect in a movement.\n\n**When to apply** — Use the five criteria as a checklist when choosing or designing a special strength exercise for a sport skill: check each criterion in turn rather than judging transfer by whether the exercise \"looks like\" the sport. Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers, rather than surface resemblance to the sport.\" Tagged **Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)** (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103). Inference: this entry is the primary-source version of the list Eric already teaches in HHPR 5355, so it can be cited directly for the criteria's original wording.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The criteria select special strength exercises; the authors say in the opening of the next chapter that this selection is \"only part of the process\" and that methods are a separate task (cross-reference only, PDF p. 266 / printed p. 255). They are not an argument against general preparation: the text gives no rule here for how much general work an athlete needs. The authors offer the criteria as reasoned principles with illustrative examples, not as the result of controlled transfer studies, so a high correspondence score does not by itself prove that an exercise will transfer. The text gives no ranking among the criteria. The authors say that following the amplitude and direction requirements \"can sometimes lead to such initial positions using weights or pulley machines which are incongruous and artificial (Fig 4.22),\" but that \"this occurs only when an exercise is attempted which fails to take into consideration the appropriate criteria,\" and they add that the Fig 4.22 exercise \"may be appropriate if applied carefully\" (PDF p. 256 / printed p. 245). So they do not treat the criteria as conflicting. Inference: in practice, meeting all five at once may still force a compromise; the book does not address that case.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own examples for each criterion: the rower and shot putter whose shoulder movements have similar amplitude but opposite force direction (Fig 4.17, PDF p. 253 / printed p. 242); the standing hip-flexor machine that misses the sprinter's range (PDF p. 253 / printed p. 242); the boxer and shot putter who should do the same pulley thrust with different loads and tempos (PDF p. 258 / printed p. 247).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (Ch1.4, the general specificity dimensions these criteria narrow down), [[heuristic--match-amplitude-direction-and-line-of-resistance-to-the-sport]], [[heuristic--train-force-at-the-accentuated-joint-angle]], [[principle--training-effort-must-match-or-exceed-sport-effort-judged-by-movement-speed]], [[heuristic--rfd-as-correspondence-criterion-and-impulse-cannot-compare-movements]], [[heuristic--same-exercise-different-regime-boxer-vs-shot-putter]], [[heuristic--phasic-biomechanical-analysis-before-selecting-special-strength-means]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/case-study--dynamic-correspondence-applied-to-wrestling-takedown]] (Evans applies the same five criteria to a wrestling takedown; agreement), [[elite-sports-performance--evans/claim--weight-room-transfer-limited-without-dynamic-correspondence]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1a5df967-9c15-4151-8d18-ad7de145c44f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
