{"parentUuid":"99f77029-9d97-492d-a17e-78abbff751f4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: block-periodization--issurin\nchapter: \"1.2.2\"\ntopic: workload concentration principle\ncategory: [periodization, programming, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-09-26\n---\n\n# Concentration of Training Workloads Is the Cornerstone Principle of Block Periodization\n\n**What it is** — Issurin states the Block Periodization Concept (BPC) rests on one\nfoundational principle, concentration of training workloads, from which two others follow:\n(1) a minimal number of targeted abilities per block, because highly concentrated loads\n\"only provide sufficient training stimuli to improve the targeted ability,\" and (2)\nconsecutive (not simultaneous) development of abilities, because \"the number of decisive\nabilities exceeds the number of abilities that can be developed simultaneously\" (PDF p. 37,\nprinted p. 26, Figure 1.7). The rationale is laid out as a table of contradictions in the\ntraditional, simultaneous-development model for high-level athletes (Table 1.4, PDF p. 20,\nprinted p. 9): energy supply gets spread across too many targets so the main one does not\nget the attention it needs, differing physiological-system restoration times leave athletes\nunder-recovered for all targets at once, workloads interact negatively (energy deficits,\ntechnical complexity, neuromuscular fatigue), mental concentration cannot be split across\nmany targets, and the training stimulus needed for a high-level athlete's progress cannot be\nreached when it is divided among many abilities. **Locator note:** Table 1.4's\nContradictions column literally reads \"There is sufficient energy supply for concurrent\nperformance of diversified workloads\" (PDF p. 20, printed p. 9); its Consequences column\nimplies the opposite, \"Energy is directed to many targets while the main target doesn't get\nthe appropriate attention.\" This reads as an apparent wording inconsistency in the source;\nthe table's overall point, that concentration protects the main target's stimulus, still\nstands.\n\n**Why it works** — Issurin's own explanation is that above a threshold of athletic level,\na quality only responds to a training stimulus concentrated enough to exceed that\nthreshold; splitting the same total training time across many qualities keeps each one's\nstimulus below that threshold. He does not give a quantitative mechanism for where that\nthreshold sits or how \"concentrated enough\" is defined for a given ability or athlete; the\nprinciple is argued from coaches' and scientists' collective experience (Table 1.4) rather\nthan derived from a stated dose-response model.\n\n**When to apply** — Designing training blocks for advanced, high-level athletes juggling\nmore qualities than can be trained well at once (the explicit target population, per\nIssurin). **Eric's baseline:** the course teaches this rule from Issurin (week1-lecture.md\nmarker pp.64, 67, which name \"Issurin (Block Training Text)\" as a source for block\nperiodization and file \"you can't ride 2 horses w/one saddle\" under the same section),\nfiled as \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle\": incompatible qualities are not both\ntrained at full intensity in the same block (`method-baseline.md`, \"Periodization and\nprogramming,\" citing `01192022-hp5355-class1-transcript.md` pp.28-29). It also converges with\n[[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], which describes\nconcentrated, saturated loading on one quality with a delayed 4-12 week rebound.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Issurin's own text (PDF p. 44, printed p. 33) states the\ntraditional, complex (simultaneous multi-ability) model \"has visible benefits for the\npreparation of low- and mid-level athletes\": it is \"more diversified, attractive and\nlively,\" and medium-level concentration is \"sufficient stimulation\" at that level: the\nconcentration principle is explicitly scoped to high-performance athletes, not a universal\nprogramming rule. **Contested (Issurin 2008 vs Issurin 2016):** this book limits the\nconcentration principle to high-level athletes, saying medium-level concentration \"still\nprovides sufficient stimulation\" for low- and mid-level athletes (PDF 44, printed p.33).\nIssurin's later review says \"proper analysis gives no evidence that amateur athletes have\nany serious restrictions when implementing highly concentrated workloads structured in\nblocks of optimal duration\" (Issurin, 2016, *Sports Medicine*, \"Benefits and Limitations of\nBlock Periodized Training Approaches to Athletes' Preparation,\" p.11, Zotero item\n`PT9QTQ2I`, full text cached in this library at\n`research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md`).\nThe same review restricts only Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, where many\ninteracting abilities are needed (its pp.6-7, Table 1, \"based on Verkhoshansky\"). It supports\nIssurin's own multi-targeted block model (28 studies, p.11) and repeats the 2008 critique of\ntraditional planning (\"this innovative version has no advantage over traditional planning,\nbecause it does not allow the multi-peak annual design,\" p.6), so it does not dispute \"does\nnot work well for high-performance athletes\" (PDF 19, printed p.8). Its 28-study support is\nan author-curated, non-systematic synthesis, not a pooled comparative estimate (vault brief,\nLimitations). **Inference:** Bompa & Haff's 5th edition, already in this library, may\nalso bear on this claim, but only Bompa Ch1-2 has been ingested so far\n(`periodization--bompa/_book.md`: Ch6 Annual Plan and Ch8 Training Cycles are pending); the\none relevant ingested entry, `principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching`, *converges*\nwith Issurin rather than disputing him, so it cannot be used to ground this contest. Check\nwhether Bompa & Haff Ch6-8 defend period-based annual planning for high-level athletes once\nthose chapters are ingested. Issurin's critique in this chapter also names Bompa's *1984*\nedition specifically (PDF p. 13, printed p. 2) as one of the texts that propagated the\ntraditional model he is arguing against; that is a different, narrower target than the 5th\nedition.\n\n**Example** — **Inference:** a collegiate football strength staff trying to develop max strength,\naerobic capacity, alactic power, and technical skill acquisition all at full intensity in\nthe same 4-week block, per Table 1.4's logic, risks under-stimulating all four rather than\nmeaningfully improving any one; concentrating the block on 1-2 of them (with the rest held\nat a retention dose) is the alternative Issurin argues for.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]], the three-block\nstructure this principle produces. [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\nand [[periodization--bompa/framework--step-loading-paradigm]], converging concentrated-load\nideas already in this library. [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--specificity-of-training-ten-dimensions]] (staging):\ncheck for overlap on why generalized simultaneous training under-delivers for advanced\nathletes.\n\n(eval):1: ========= not found","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UoBREzvBEYaXWXuknLi9sY"}]},"uuid":"c4da63d5-b26d-4c9e-a63a-f0133892b53c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:24.381Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Exit code 1\n---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: block-periodization--issurin\nchapter: \"1.2.2\"\ntopic: workload concentration principle\ncategory: [periodization, programming, exercise-selection]\nconfiden