{"parentUuid":"b4256718-0213-41a1-bd60-e63a983fcb58","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZbNewEuHEm1AkoL9tV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PxV1ZDCkjP3fvw5JKugQRm","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"V=/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library; cd $V/strength-and-conditioning; sed -n 1,60p periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching.md; echo '----'; grep -n -i \"verkhoshansky\\|concentrated\\|unidirectional\\|long-term delayed\\|LDTE\\|residual\" $V/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits*.md | head -40","description":"Read Bompa entry and grep Issurin 2016"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2790,"cache_read_input_tokens":153319,"output_tokens":273,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":34},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2790},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":273,"cache_read_input_tokens":153319,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2790,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2790,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PxV1ZDCkjP3fvw5JKugQRm":{"command":"V=/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library; cd $V/strength-and-conditioning; sed -n 1,60p periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching.md; echo '----'; grep -n -i \"verkhoshansky\\|concentrated\\|unidirectional\\|long-term delayed\\|LDTE\\|residual\" $V/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits*.md | head -40","description":"Read Bompa entry and grep Issurin 2016"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZbNLqPUF6fg8189gtM","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"cb5fc6e8-863e-41db-b19c-93a9d677a9a4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:27.122Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"cb5fc6e8-863e-41db-b19c-93a9d677a9a4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PxV1ZDCkjP3fvw5JKugQRm","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: periodization--bompa\nchapter: \"2\"\ntopic: concentrated loading and overreaching\ncategory: [periodization, load-management, monitoring, advanced]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-06-12\n---\n\n# Concentrated Loading and Planned Overreaching\n\n**What it is** — A short period of deliberately high, \"saturated\" training load on one quality (planned overreaching), followed by restitution. Performance is *suppressed* during the loading block and rebounds — supercompensates — afterward. Siff and Verkhoshansky note gains may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends; the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay before performance lifts. The **conjugated sequence** extends this: concentrate on one emphasis (e.g. strength) while holding maintenance loads elsewhere, then shift emphasis in the unloading block so a different quality rises.\n\n**Why it works** — Saturating a stimulus drives a deeper adaptive signal than steady loading. Monitoring via the **testosterone:cortisol (T:C) ratio** tracks the anabolic–catabolic balance: ≥3 weeks of concentrated load lowers the basal T:C ratio (a catabolic shift, reduced preparedness); returning load to normal lets the T:C ratio and performance supercompensate. T:C indexes *preparedness*, not overtraining per se.\n\n**When to apply** — Advanced athletes who can tolerate high loads; building a potent stimulus before a taper/peak; reducing long-term work volume versus concurrent training; timing a delayed performance rebound to a competition window.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices or athletes without the training capacity to absorb saturating loads — fatigue is substantial during accumulation and mismanagement tips into overtraining. The delayed-rebound timing (4–12 wk) must be planned backward from competition, or the peak lands in the wrong week. Builds on [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] and [[framework--step-loading-paradigm]]; bounded by [[principle--individualization]].\n\n**Example** — A 3-week strength-concentrated block (strength up, speed maintained) is timed so the performance rebound surfaces ~4–6 weeks later at the first key competition; T:C ratio is monitored to confirm the post-block recovery.\n----\n26:This review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\r\n40:- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\r\n46:- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\r\n48:- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\r\n50:- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\r\n51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\r\n70:| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b7713d22-64fb-41fa-9116-61e9bcbfe86e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:27.343Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: periodization--bompa\nchapter: \"2\"\ntopic: concentrated loading and overreaching\ncategory: [periodization, load-management, monitoring, advanced]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-06-12\n---\n\n# Concentrated Loading and Planned Overreaching\n\n**What it is** — A short period of deliberately high, \"saturated\" training load on one quality (planned overreaching), followed by restitution. Performance is *suppressed* during the loading block and rebounds — supercompensates — afterward. Siff and Verkhoshansky note gains may appear **4 to 12 weeks** after the concentrated block ends; the longer/larger the loading, the longer the delay before performance lifts. The **conjugated sequence** extends this: concentrate on one emphasis (e.g. strength) while holding maintenance loads elsewhere, then shift emphasis in the unloading block so a different quality rises.\n\n**Why it works** — Saturating a stimulus drives a deeper adaptive signal than steady loading. Monitoring via the **testosterone:cortisol (T:C) ratio** tracks the anabolic–catabolic balance: ≥3 weeks of concentrated load lowers the basal T:C ratio (a catabolic shift, reduced preparedness); returning load to normal lets the T:C ratio and performance supercompensate. T:C indexes *preparedness*, not overtraining per se.\n\n**When to apply** — Advanced athletes who can tolerate high loads; building a potent stimulus before a taper/peak; reducing long-term work volume versus concurrent training; timing a delayed performance rebound to a competition window.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for novices or athletes without the training capacity to absorb saturating loads — fatigue is substantial during accumulation and mismanagement tips into overtraining. The delayed-rebound timing (4–12 wk) must be planned backward from competition, or the peak lands in the wrong week. Builds on [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] and [[framework--step-loading-paradigm]]; bounded by [[principle--individualization]].\n\n**Example** — A 3-week strength-concentrated block (strength up, speed maintained) is timed so the performance rebound surfaces ~4–6 weeks later at the first key competition; T:C ratio is monitored to confirm the post-block recovery.\n----\n26:This review distinguishes concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block models and provides a useful conceptual vocabulary for discussing concentrated workloads and sequencing.\r\n40:- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\r\n46:- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\r\n48:- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\r\n50:- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\r\n51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\r\n70:| Concentrated-unidirectional evidence and performance gaps | p. 6 | narrative synthesis |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cb5fc6e8-863e-41db-b19c-93a9d677a9a4","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
