{"parentUuid":"cf7c3f79-c629-425e-8dc1-a300f16a12da","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MxPud4sRUPMdmoJbZ8VaHy","type":"tool_result","content":"### framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order\nchapter: \"4.1.2\"\n# Training Is Non-Commutative and Non-Associative: Order and Combination Change the Outcome\n**What it is** — The authors borrow two properties from algebra to describe how combinations\n### heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination\nchapter: \"4.1.2\"\n# A Structured Complex of Means Beats Volume Alone or a Random Combination of the Same Means\n**What it is** — The authors define training load by five characteristics: its resulting\n### claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases\nchapter: \"2.3.1\"\n# Transfer Between Physical Qualities (Strength, Speed, Endurance) Shrinks as an Athlete's Specific Fitness Rises\n**What it is** — Summarizing a body of Soviet and Western literature (Zimkin 1956/1965;\n### framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness\nchapter: \"4.4\"\n# Functional Specialisation in Four Levels: General Abilities, Coordination, Explosive Force and Specific Endurance, Then the Structure of Special Physical Preparedness\n**What it is** — Under \"The Process of Functional Specialisation,\" the authors give \"an ascending flowchart\" of how the body specialises \"over many years of training\" (Fig 4.24, verified against the page image; PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251):\n### heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns\nchapter: \"1\"\n# Restrictive, Repetitive Patterns May Overspecialize the Nervous System\n**What it is** — Drawing on neuroplasticity research (Rosenzweig, Diamond, Greenough,\n### claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance\nchapter: \"3.4.3\"\n# Tensing Additional, Non-Prime-Mover Muscle Groups Can Raise or Lower Working-Limb Force\n**What it is** — In acyclic movements that require a single instantaneous maximal\n### framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines\nchapter: \"4.2.7.1\"\n# Two Classes of Training Machine: Functional Resistance (Sport-Specific Pattern) vs Non-Functional Resistance (General Pattern)\n**What it is** — The authors distinguish two classes of strength machine: functional\n### framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy\nchapter: \"4.1.2\"\n# Acute vs. Delayed, and Particular vs. Cumulative: The Book's Taxonomy of Training Effect\n**What it is** — The authors distinguish the acute (immediate) training effect (ATE), the\n### heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities\nchapter: \"4.2.1.6\"\n# Practical Rules for Integrating Electrostimulation Into a Programme: Sequencing, Electrode Placement, Dose Limits\n**What it is** — \"Several researchers (Purvin, Deniskin, Khodykin)\", cited without dates,\n### claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more\nchapter: \"3.4.6\"\n# Skilled Lifters Spend Less Energy per Lift, and Discrete Singles Cost More Than a Continuous Set\n**What it is** — Based on Vorobyev's (1978) weightlifting data (Tables 3.10-3.12), energy","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9355b551-696a-4af9-a374-88b6b38979ac","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:47.306Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order\nchapter: \"4.1.2\"\n# Training Is Non-Commutative and Non-Associative: Order and Combination Change the Outcome\n**What it is** — The authors borrow two properties from algebra to describe how combinations\n### heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination\nchapter: \"4.1.2\"\n# A Structured Complex of Means Beats Volume Alone or a Random Combination of the Same Means\n**What it is** — The authors define training load by five characteristics: its resulting\n### claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases\nchapter: \"2.3.1\"\n# Transfer Between Physical Qualities (Strength, Speed, Endurance) Shrinks as an Athlete's Specific Fitness Rises\n**What it is** — Summarizing a body of Soviet and Western literature (Zimkin 1956/1965;\n### framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness\nchapter: \"4.4\"\n# Functional Specialisation in Four Levels: General Abilities, Coordination, Explosive Force and Specific Endurance, Then the Structure of Special Physical Preparedness\n**What it is** — Under \"The Process of Functional Specialisation,\" the authors give \"an ascending flowchart\" of how the body specialises \"over many years of training\" (Fig 4.24, verified against the page image; PDF p. 262 / printed p. 251):\n### heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns\nchapter: \"1\"\n# Restrictive, Repetitive Patterns May Overspecialize the Nervous System\n**What it is** — Drawing on neuroplasticity research (Rosenzweig, Diamond, Greenough,\n### claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance\nchapter: \"3.4.3\"\n# Tensing Additional, Non-Prime-Mover Muscle Groups Can Raise or Lower Working-Limb Force\n**What it is** — In acyclic movements that require a single instantaneous maximal\n### framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines\nchapter: \"4.2.7.1\"\n# Two Classes of Training Machine: Functional Resistance (Sport-Specific Pattern) vs Non-Functional Resistance (General Pattern)\n**What it is** — The authors distinguish two classes of strength machine: functional\n### framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy\nchapter: \"4.1.2\"\n# Acute vs. Delayed, and Particular vs. Cumulative: The Book's Taxonomy of Training Effect\n**What it is** — The authors distinguish the acute (immediate) training effect (ATE), the\n### heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities\nchapter: \"4.2.1.6\"\n# Practical Rules for Integrating Electrostimulation Into a Programme: Sequencing, Electrode Placement, Dose Limits\n**What it is** — \"Several researchers (Purvin, Deniskin, Khodykin)\", cited without dates,\n### claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more\nchapter: \"3.4.6\"\n# Skilled Lifters Spend Less Energy per Lift, and Discrete Singles Cost More Than a Continuous Set\n**What it is** — Based on Vorobyev's (1978) weightlifting data (Tables 3.10-3.12), energy","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cf7c3f79-c629-425e-8dc1-a300f16a12da","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
