{"parentUuid":"e8524734-f6b5-4804-b0f9-cd85ae9a3130","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZd4emfcKgcujeHobB6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EzKc5wbTycoeFgZLF8AnUG","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n '/What it is/,/When to apply/p' framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order.md | head -30; echo '----'; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities.md | head -12; echo '----'; grep -n -i \"irradiation\\|cross\" claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance.md | head; echo ---; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy.md","description":"Read key overlapping entries more closely"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2399,"cache_read_input_tokens":164684,"output_tokens":339,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2399},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":339,"cache_read_input_tokens":164684,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2399,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01EzKc5wbTycoeFgZLF8AnUG":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; sed -n '/What it is/,/When to apply/p' framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order.md | head -30; echo '----'; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities.md | head -12; echo '----'; grep -n -i \"irradiation\\|cross\" claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance.md | head; echo ---; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy.md","description":"Read key overlapping entries more closely"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffZd4NQ683SPrGRHEKWG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"74c9ae59-b31b-4c87-910e-0250959201be","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:50.331Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"74c9ae59-b31b-4c87-910e-0250959201be","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EzKc5wbTycoeFgZLF8AnUG","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — The authors borrow two properties from algebra to describe how combinations\nof training means, methods and techniques behave. The Commutative Law (A*B = B*A) and the\nAssociative Law (A*(B*C) = (A*B)*C) are stated to generally NOT hold in sports training.\nTraining is non-commutative: the order in which exercises or exercise types are performed\nchanges the outcome. The book's own examples: a squat-then-clean sequence does not give the\nsame result as clean-then-squat; a distance-running session shortly before a weights session\n\"will invariably decrease one's strength capabilities.\" Training is also non-associative:\nusing means A followed by a complex of means B and C does not give the same result as a\ncomplex of A and B followed by C on its own. The book's own example: a muscle-endurance\nregime (A) performed before a complex of plyometrics (B) and Olympic lifting (C) does not\nproduce the same result as a complex of muscle endurance and plyometrics (A and B together)\nfollowed by Olympic lifting (C) alone. The authors extend non-commutativity beyond a single\nsession: over successive microcycles or mesocycles in a periodised program, the after-effect\nphenomenon means the order of training means affects the outcome, and in complex training\n(combinations of different means in sessions or longer cycles) the order of the components\ndetermines the outcome. The authors note that with hybrid exercises (several\nmovements combined into one repetition, cross-referenced to chapter 7.1, not yet ingested in\nthis pass), the order of the individual movements likewise has a major effect on execution\nand effectiveness. (PDF pp. 216-217, printed pp. 205-206.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no physiological mechanism for non-commutativity or\nnon-associativity as such. They tie it to the general after-effect phenomenon: every\nexercise leaves after-effects whose nature, duration and magnitude depend on the intensity,\nduration and pattern of loading, and these after-effects can make exercises placed in\ndifferent parts of a workout, on different training days, or even weeks apart, interact\npositively or negatively. Inference: this is consistent with acute neuromuscular, metabolic\nand potentiation carryover from one exercise or session component to the next, which changes\nthe stimulus actually delivered by whatever comes after it, though the book does not name a\nspecific physiological carrier for the effect.\n\n----\n**What it is** — \"Several researchers (Purvin, Deniskin, Khodykin)\", cited without dates,\nstudied how electrostimulation (ES) combines with other training means. They found that ES\nfirst, followed by plyometrics, produces a greater training effect than the reverse order;\n\"a still larger effect was obtained by using ES and plyometrics concurrently with heavy\nresistance exercise\", while the ES and plyometric combination had its smallest training\neffect during periods of complete or partial rest (PDF pp. 225-226, printed pp. 214-215).\nElectrode placement matters: adjacent (close) attachment localises the contraction and keeps\nit fairly superficial, while distant attachment (one electrode at a remote or fairly neutral\nsite) produces a more extensive, deeper contraction; electrode gel or weak saline solution is\nneeded for comfortable conduction, and skin irritation or prickliness \"invariably\" results\nfrom the gel drying out. Electrodes may be placed directly over important nerves, but \"the\nintensity of the ensuing contraction can be harmful\" and this method \"should be restricted to\n----\n7:topic: cross-limb irradiation effects on prime mover force\n8:category: [neuromuscular, coordination, cross-education]\n21:attribute this to \"overflow\" or \"irradiation\" from stronger to weaker or more fatigued\n36:unexercised-limb \"cross-training\" phenomenon (training one side of the body increases\n68:already committed to the effort, is consistent with the irradiation effect raising\n72:cross-limb neural phenomenon: bilateral force deficit vs this irradiation/cross-education\n---\n**What it is** — The authors distinguish the acute (immediate) training effect (ATE), the\nbody's short-term improvement or deterioration in functional state right after a strength\nmeans is applied, from the delayed training effect (DTE), the more stable adaptation that\nfollows once the volume of training influence is sufficient (cross-referenced forward to\nchapter 6.7.1, not yet ingested in this pass). Whether the immediate reaction is\ndeterioration or improvement is said to depend on the exercise's energy cost, which\ndetermines the rest interval needed before the next exercise: the interval can be lengthened\nif more rest is needed, or optimally shortened to exploit a beneficial \"superimposed\nafter-effect phenomenon.\" Separately, the authors distinguish the particular effect (from\none or more unidirectional means, reflecting the specificity of that means and regime) from\nthe cumulative effect (from a complex of multi-directional means), stating explicitly that\nthe cumulative effect is not simply the sum of the body's individual functional changes, but\n\"a complex of new forms of its motor potential\" that still contains the specific\ncharacteristics of the particular means used. The authors then state five generalizations\nabout the training effect of a complex of means: (1) the training effect of any means\ndiminishes as the athlete's special physical fitness increases; (2) the means used should\nproduce the optimal training effect relative to the athlete's current functional state; (3)\nthe after-effect of previous work alters the training effect of any subsequent methods; (4) the\ntraining effect of a complex of means depends not only on the sum of the individual stimuli\nbut also on their combination, order of succession and intervals of separation; (5) the\ncontent of special strength training should be a complex of specific stimuli that produces\nthe strength fitness required for the given sport, matched to the athlete's level of sports\nmastery. (PDF pp. 215-216, printed pp. 204-205.)\n\n**Why it works** — No mechanism is given for the ATE/DTE split beyond the energy-cost-driven","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b0f84db2-a222-49f0-abb1-de0039e16fee","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:45:50.559Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — The authors borrow two properties from algebra to describe how combinations\nof training means, methods and techniques behave. The Commutative Law (A*B = B*A) and the\nAssociative Law (A*(B*C) = (A*B)*C) are stated to generally NOT hold in sports training.\nTraining is non-commutative: the order in which exercises or exercise types are performed\nchanges the outcome. The book's own examples: a squat-then-clean sequence does not give the\nsame result as clean-then-squat; a distance-running session shortly before a weights session\n\"will invariably decrease one's strength capabilities.\" Training is also non-associative:\nusing means A followed by a complex of means B and C does not give the same result as a\ncomplex of A and B followed by C on its own. The book's own example: a muscle-endurance\nregime (A) performed before a complex of plyometrics (B) and Olympic lifting (C) does not\nproduce the same result as a complex of muscle endurance and plyometrics (A and B together)\nfollowed by Olympic lifting (C) alone. The authors extend non-commutativity beyond a single\nsession: over successive microcycles or mesocycles in a periodised program, the after-effect\nphenomenon means the order of training means affects the outcome, and in complex training\n(combinations of different means in sessions or longer cycles) the order of the components\ndetermines the outcome. The authors note that with hybrid exercises (several\nmovements combined into one repetition, cross-referenced to chapter 7.1, not yet ingested in\nthis pass), the order of the individual movements likewise has a major effect on execution\nand effectiveness. (PDF pp. 216-217, printed pp. 205-206.)\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no physiological mechanism for non-commutativity or\nnon-associativity as such. They tie it to the general after-effect phenomenon: every\nexercise leaves after-effects whose nature, duration and magnitude depend on the intensity,\nduration and pattern of loading, and these after-effects can make exercises placed in\ndifferent parts of a workout, on different training days, or even weeks apart, interact\npositively or negatively. Inference: this is consistent with acute neuromuscular, metabolic\nand potentiation carryover from one exercise or session component to the next, which changes\nthe stimulus actually delivered by whatever comes after it, though the book does not name a\nspecific physiological carrier for the effect.\n\n----\n**What it is** — \"Several researchers (Purvin, Deniskin, Khodykin)\", cited without dates,\nstudied how electrostimulation (ES) combines with other training means. They found that ES\nfirst, followed by plyometrics, produces a greater training effect than the reverse order;\n\"a still larger effect was obtained by using ES and plyometrics concurrently with heavy\nresistance exercise\", while the ES and plyometric combination had its smallest training\neffect during periods of complete or partial rest (PDF pp. 225-226, printed pp. 214-215).\nElectrode placement matters: adjacent (close) attachment localises the contraction and keeps\nit fairly superficial, while distant attachment (one electrode at a remote or fairly neutral\nsite) produces a more extensive, deeper contraction; electrode gel or weak saline solution is\nneeded for comfortable conduction, and skin irritation or prickliness \"invariably\" results\nfrom the gel drying out. Electrodes may be placed directly over important nerves, but \"the\nintensity of the ensuing contraction can be harmful\" and this method \"should be restricted to\n----\n7:topic: cross-limb irradiation effects on prime mover force\n8:category: [neuromuscular, coordination, cross-education]\n21:attribute this to \"overflow\" or \"irradiation\" from stronger to weaker or more fatigued\n36:unexercised-limb \"cross-training\" phenomenon (training one side of the body increases\n68:already committed to the effort, is consistent with the irradiation effect raising\n72:cross-limb neural phenomenon: bilateral force deficit vs this irradiation/cross-education\n---\n**What it is** — The authors distinguish the acute (immediate) training effect (ATE), the\nbody's short-term improvement or deterioration in functional state right after a strength\nmeans is applied, from the delayed training effect (DTE), the more stable adaptation that\nfollows once the volume of training influence is sufficient (cross-referenced forward to\nchapter 6.7.1, not yet ingested in this pass). Whether the immediate reaction is\ndeterioration or improvement is said to depend on the exercise's energy cost, which\ndetermines the rest interval needed before the next exercise: the interval can be lengthened\nif more rest is needed, or optimally shortened to exploit a beneficial \"superimposed\nafter-effect phenomenon.\" Separately, the authors distinguish the particular effect (from\none or more unidirectional means, reflecting the specificity of that means and regime) from\nthe cumulative effect (from a complex of multi-directional means), stating explicitly that\nthe cumulative effect is not simply the sum of the body's individual functional changes, but\n\"a complex of new forms of its motor potential\" that still contains the specific\ncharacteristics of the particular means used. The authors then state five generalizations\nabout the training effect of a complex of means: (1) the training effect of any means\ndiminishes as the athlete's special physical fitness increases; (2) the means used should\nproduce the optimal training effect relative to the athlete's current functional state; (3)\nthe after-effect of previous work alters the training effect of any subsequent methods; (4) the\ntraining effect of a complex of means depends not only on the sum of the individual stimuli\nbut also on their combination, order of succession and intervals of separation; (5) the\ncontent of special strength training should be a complex of specific stimuli that produces\nthe strength fitness required for the given sport, matched to the athlete's level of sports\nmastery. (PDF pp. 215-216, printed pp. 204-205.)\n\n**Why it works** — No mechanism is given for the ATE/DTE split beyond the energy-cost-driven","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"74c9ae59-b31b-4c87-910e-0250959201be","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
