mework--workout-load-classification-and-rpe-mapping.md\nheuristic--dual-approach-initial-level-and-improvement-rate-giftedness.md\nheuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state.md\nheuristic--microcycle-construction-key-workout-rules.md\nheuristic--nine-step-annual-program-construction-sequence.md\nheuristic--older-vs-younger-athlete-quadrennial-workload-differentiation.md\nheuristic--sensitive-periods-by-motor-ability-and-sex.md\nheuristic--two-a-day-workout-sequencing-by-dominant-ability.md\nprinciple--concentration-of-training-workloads.md\nprinciple--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout.md\n**What it is** — The residual training effect is defined as \"the retention of changes\ninduced by systematic workloads beyond a certain time period after the cessation of\ntraining,\" a term Issurin attributes to Brian and James Counsilman (1991) (PDF p. 33,\nprinted p. 22). Issurin & Lustig (2004) is cited as the source of Table 1.6 (PDF p. 35,\nprinted p. 24), giving residual duration ± SD in days after cessation of training for each\nmotor ability: **aerobic endurance 30 ± 5 days**, **maximum strength 30 ± 5 days**,\n**anaerobic glycolytic endurance 18 ± 4 days**, **strength endurance 15 ± 5 days**,\n**maximum speed (alactic) 5 ± 3 days**. Table 1.6's own physiological-background column\nlists aerobic endurance's residual as resting on increased aerobic enzymes, mitochondria,\nmuscle capillaries, hemoglobin capacity, glycogen storage, and fat-metabolism rate, and\nmaximum strength's on improved neural mechanism and muscle hypertrophy from fiber\nenlargement, with no percentages attached in the table itself. Later in the text (PDF p. 35,\nprinted p. 24), Issurin gives a specific figure for one of those table items: aerobic\nenzymes increase by 40-90% with training (Volkov, 1986; Fox et al., 1993; Wilmore & Costill,\n1993). He contrasts this with much smaller local adaptations from sprint training:\nphosphocreatine storage (+2-5%), peak lactate accumulation (+10-20%), and anaerobic enzymes\n(+2-20%), given without a citation on that page. On the following page (PDF p. 36, printed\np. 25), Thorstensson (1988) is cited specifically for \"relatively small increases\" in\nquickly available energy sources, ATP and phosphocreatine, and enzymes such as creatine\nkinase, from highly concentrated sprint training.\n\n**Why it works** — Issurin states the mechanism directly (PDF pp. 35-36, printed pp. 24-25):\naerobic ability, supported by pronounced morphological and biochemical changes, is retained\n\"for a number of weeks at near-peak level,\" while anaerobic abilities, particularly maximum\nsprint speed, \"retain near-peak levels for much shorter periods\" because peak speed's gains\n----\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:40:theory, block-sequencing prescriptions, and the numeric tables he or his co-authors derived\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:53:p.11). Its concentrated-unidirectional critique (pp.6-7) targets Verkhoshansky's model, not\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:69:Table 1.6 residual-effect durations); 1.3 Consequences of the modern approach (printed\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:76:cool-down) (printed 46-59, PDF 57-70); 2.3 Guidelines for constructing a workout — sequencing,\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:131:sequence, Figures 4.1-4.4); 4.1.3 General trends in workload planning (printed 130-132, PDF\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:146:125-140): 1 heuristic on the nine-step annual-program sequence, 1 framework on the\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/_book.md:163:Supertraining/Verkhoshansky Table 1.2 entry; both numbers are kept, not averaged. The\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay.md:66:cases as \"concurrent interference\" versus \"sequential decay\" is this entry's own\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay.md:70:motivates the consecutive block sequencing in\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay.md:98:failure mode: without deliberate sequencing or volume management, the concurrent aerobic\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay.md:104:[[framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]]; [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-training-raises-haem\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles.md:24:be shortened to two weeks.\" Sequencing depends on the competition schedule and individual\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles.md:38:logic (concentrated, sequenced blocks beat simultaneous multi-ability training) applied\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin/case-study--sedykh-hammer-throw-explosive-strength-microcycle.md:50:[[heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]: CNS-dependent, high-precision qualities\n/Users/kris