{"parentUuid":"71ef1869-8b63-4ddf-babc-603e5352a592","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZduvgBCr5tZHy13UBo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013Fb5TPELbt6KZ2kYGQ6ui5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"V=/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library; cd $V/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin; for f in principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay; do echo \"### $f\"; grep -m1 '^# ' $f.md; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' $f.md | head -14 | cut -c1-600; done; echo '----'; sed -n 36,60p $V/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits*.md; echo ----; grep -m1 '^# ' $V/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model.md","description":"Read Issurin sequencing and compatibility entries"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4736,"cache_read_input_tokens":172717,"output_tokens":1266,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":915},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4736},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1266,"cache_read_input_tokens":172717,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4736,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4736,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013Fb5TPELbt6KZ2kYGQ6ui5":{"command":"V=/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library; cd $V/strength-and-conditioning/block-periodization--issurin; for f in principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay; do echo \"### $f\"; grep -m1 '^# ' $f.md; sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' $f.md | head -14 | cut -c1-600; done; echo '----'; sed -n 36,60p $V/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits*.md; echo ----; grep -m1 '^# ' $V/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model.md","description":"Read Issurin sequencing and compatibility entries"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffZdufZMuJKBKVwjSqc4","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9d2ceb1d-3ef0-47c7-84d8-81564a63efa5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:46:10.009Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9d2ceb1d-3ef0-47c7-84d8-81564a63efa5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Fb5TPELbt6KZ2kYGQ6ui5","type":"tool_result","content":"### principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout\n# No More Than Three Training Modalities Per Workout, With 65-70% of Time on One or Two\n**What it is** — Issurin extends the concentration principle to single-session design\n(Figure 2.4, PDF p. 74, printed p. 63; Table 2.12, PDF p. 76, printed p. 65) with five\nstated rules (PDF p. 75, printed p. 64): (1) a workout should contain **no more than three\ntraining modalities**, typically one dominant, one compatible with the main purpose, and\none for technique/tactics improvement or restoration; (2) **65-70% of total training time**\nin a development workout should go to one or two selected modalities, to preserve workload\nconcentration; (3) the typical frequency of workouts in high-performance training (6-12 a\nweek) dictates certain conditions for the session after a key-workout: it should show a\nsignificant load reduction, and running two consecutive key-workouts, while possible, risks\nexcessive load concentration; (4) hypertrophy-focused sessions require workload to be\n\"substantially reduced\" for **at least 20 hours** afterward to protect the anabolic phase of\nmuscle restoration; (5) limiting modality count matters most in high-performance sport; the\ndaily program for juniors, for example, may be more diverse, multilateral, and therefore\nmore attractive. Figure 2.4 maps which modalities are compatible as secondary/tertiary\n### heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state\n# Which Training Modalities Tolerate Fatigue, and Which Need a Fresh Athlete\n**What it is** — Figure 2.3 (PDF p. 71, printed p. 60) ranks twelve training modalities by\nhow much athlete fatigue they can tolerate before the acute training effect is compromised,\nfrom \"well rested\" through \"slightly fatigued,\" \"moderately fatigued,\" to \"fatigued\":\n**well rested only**: maximal speed (least fatigue-tolerant of all); **well rested to\nslightly fatigued**: maximal strength (neural mechanism, e.g. 1-3RM), explosive strength,\nacquisition of new technical skills, anaerobic glycolytic power; **up to moderately\nfatigued**: anaerobic glycolytic capacity, aerobic power, maximal strength (hypertrophy,\ne.g. 8-10RM); **through to fatigued, end of session (most fatigue-tolerant)**: aerobic\nendurance (long duration), strength endurance, technique perfection, flexibility. Issurin's\nstated reasoning (PDF pp. 71-73, printed pp. 60-62): exercises needing precise excitatory\nneural output (max speed, explosive strength, technical skill acquisition, neural-mechanism\nstrength) are blocked by inhibitory CNS output in fatigued athletes, so they belong early;\nanaerobic glycolytic power needs available energy reserves that are depleted in fatigued\nathletes; aerobic power's acute effect depends on the total duration of exercise performed\n### case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay\n# Two Studies Issurin Cites Against Simultaneous Development: Swimmer Interference and Kayaker Decay\n**What it is** — Issurin presents two data-backed examples as motivation for abandoning\nsimultaneous multi-ability training (traditional periodization) in favor of consecutive,\nconcentrated blocks.\n\n**(1) Swimmer concurrent-training interference (PDF pp. 21-22, printed pp. 10-11, Figure\n1.3).** High-level male swimmers were studied over an 8-week early-season program combining\nextensive swimming with resistive/power dry-land work aimed at swim-specific strength\n(9-11 workouts/week; sample size not stated in this text). Measured: maximal force of\ntethered swimming (Ftsw), dry-land explosive strength (Fexp), and dry-land strength\nendurance (SE). Result: strength endurance improved markedly; swim-specific strength did not\nimprove; explosive strength *decreased*, despite about 30% of dry-land program time devoted\nto maximal/explosive strength work. Issurin's mechanistic explanation has two separate\nparts: extensive endurance work captures the metabolic energy needed for post-exercise\nanabolism, which suppresses muscle hypertrophy; separately, coaches' and athletes'\n----\n- It is a training-theory review, not evidence specific to collegiate American football.\r\n\r\n## Study design and methods\r\n\r\n- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\r\n- The article summarizes prior studies in tables but does not report a systematic search, eligibility process, formal risk-of-bias appraisal, or meta-analysis.\r\n- Consequently, the stated number and direction of supportive studies should be interpreted as an author-curated synthesis rather than a pooled comparative estimate.\r\n\r\n## Key findings\r\n\r\n- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\r\n- In an elite-swimmer example, specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes. [p. 6]\r\n- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\r\n- The review reports support from 28 studies for multi-targeted block programs, but the designs, populations, outcomes, and comparator quality varied. [p. 11]\r\n- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\r\n- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\r\n\r\n## Limitations and applicability\r\n\r\n- Non-systematic study selection and absent risk-of-bias assessment create a substantial risk of selective emphasis.\r\n- Fitness improvements are not interchangeable with sport-specific performance, availability, or competitive outcomes. [p. 6]\r\n- Training models were not consistently isolated from total volume, intensity, exercise choice, coaching, or athlete history.\r\n- Evidence spans dissimilar sports and training levels; it does not supply validated block durations or effect estimates for collegiate American football.\r\n- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\r\n\r\n----\n# The Long-Term Sequential Development Model","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"da20c0dc-cc05-41a0-8ac8-e9f6cd4c71da","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:46:10.258Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"### principle--training-modality-compatibility-in-a-workout\n# No More Than Three Training Modalities Per Workout, With 65-70% of Time on One or Two\n**What it is** — Issurin extends the concentration principle to single-session design\n(Figure 2.4, PDF p. 74, printed p. 63; Table 2.12, PDF p. 76, printed p. 65) with five\nstated rules (PDF p. 75, printed p. 64): (1) a workout should contain **no more than three\ntraining modalities**, typically one dominant, one compatible with the main purpose, and\none for technique/tactics improvement or restoration; (2) **65-70% of total training time**\nin a development workout should go to one or two selected modalities, to preserve workload\nconcentration; (3) the typical frequency of workouts in high-performance training (6-12 a\nweek) dictates certain conditions for the session after a key-workout: it should show a\nsignificant load reduction, and running two consecutive key-workouts, while possible, risks\nexcessive load concentration; (4) hypertrophy-focused sessions require workload to be\n\"substantially reduced\" for **at least 20 hours** afterward to protect the anabolic phase of\nmuscle restoration; (5) limiting modality count matters most in high-performance sport; the\ndaily program for juniors, for example, may be more diverse, multilateral, and therefore\nmore attractive. Figure 2.4 maps which modalities are compatible as secondary/tertiary\n### heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state\n# Which Training Modalities Tolerate Fatigue, and Which Need a Fresh Athlete\n**What it is** — Figure 2.3 (PDF p. 71, printed p. 60) ranks twelve training modalities by\nhow much athlete fatigue they can tolerate before the acute training effect is compromised,\nfrom \"well rested\" through \"slightly fatigued,\" \"moderately fatigued,\" to \"fatigued\":\n**well rested only**: maximal speed (least fatigue-tolerant of all); **well rested to\nslightly fatigued**: maximal strength (neural mechanism, e.g. 1-3RM), explosive strength,\nacquisition of new technical skills, anaerobic glycolytic power; **up to moderately\nfatigued**: anaerobic glycolytic capacity, aerobic power, maximal strength (hypertrophy,\ne.g. 8-10RM); **through to fatigued, end of session (most fatigue-tolerant)**: aerobic\nendurance (long duration), strength endurance, technique perfection, flexibility. Issurin's\nstated reasoning (PDF pp. 71-73, printed pp. 60-62): exercises needing precise excitatory\nneural output (max speed, explosive strength, technical skill acquisition, neural-mechanism\nstrength) are blocked by inhibitory CNS output in fatigued athletes, so they belong early;\nanaerobic glycolytic power needs available energy reserves that are depleted in fatigued\nathletes; aerobic power's acute effect depends on the total duration of exercise performed\n### case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay\n# Two Studies Issurin Cites Against Simultaneous Development: Swimmer Interference and Kayaker Decay\n**What it is** — Issurin presents two data-backed examples as motivation for abandoning\nsimultaneous multi-ability training (traditional periodization) in favor of consecutive,\nconcentrated blocks.\n\n**(1) Swimmer concurrent-training interference (PDF pp. 21-22, printed pp. 10-11, Figure\n1.3).** High-level male swimmers were studied over an 8-week early-season program combining\nextensive swimming with resistive/power dry-land work aimed at swim-specific strength\n(9-11 workouts/week; sample size not stated in this text). Measured: maximal force of\ntethered swimming (Ftsw), dry-land explosive strength (Fexp), and dry-land strength\nendurance (SE). Result: strength endurance improved markedly; swim-specific strength did not\nimprove; explosive strength *decreased*, despite about 30% of dry-land program time devoted\nto maximal/explosive strength work. Issurin's mechanistic explanation has two separate\nparts: extensive endurance work captures the metabolic energy needed for post-exercise\nanabolism, which suppresses muscle hypertrophy; separately, coaches' and athletes'\n----\n- It is a training-theory review, not evidence specific to collegiate American football.\r\n\r\n## Study design and methods\r\n\r\n- Narrative review contrasting concentrated-unidirectional and multi-targeted block-periodization models.\r\n- The article summarizes prior studies in tables but does not report a systematic search, eligibility process, formal risk-of-bias appraisal, or meta-analysis.\r\n- Consequently, the stated number and direction of supportive studies should be interpreted as an author-curated synthesis rather than a pooled comparative estimate.\r\n\r\n## Key findings\r\n\r\n- Concentrated-unidirectional programs in combat and team sports improved some fitness measures, but the reviewed studies did not report effects on team practice or competitive performance. [p. 6]\r\n- In an elite-swimmer example, specific strength and anaerobic capacity improved but swimming performance improved in only two of three athletes. [p. 6]\r\n- The author judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation. [p. 6]\r\n- The review reports support from 28 studies for multi-targeted block programs, but the designs, populations, outcomes, and comparator quality varied. [p. 11]\r\n- The concentrated-unidirectional model was presented as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and as restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities. [p. 11]\r\n- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\r\n\r\n## Limitations and applicability\r\n\r\n- Non-systematic study selection and absent risk-of-bias assessment create a substantial risk of selective emphasis.\r\n- Fitness improvements are not interchangeable with sport-specific performance, availability, or competitive outcomes. [p. 6]\r\n- Training models were not consistently isolated from total volume, intensity, exercise choice, coaching, or athlete history.\r\n- Evidence spans dissimilar sports and training levels; it does not supply validated block durations or effect estimates for collegiate American football.\r\n- The strong conclusion favoring multi-targeted block periodization is the author's synthesis and should not be treated as settled comparative evidence. [p. 11]\r\n\r\n----\n# The Long-Term Sequential Development Model","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9d2ceb1d-3ef0-47c7-84d8-81564a63efa5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
