{"parentUuid":"53ee8005-8c26-4799-8c03-ac6566a22e77","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012TcGf7x9uhBhHsjEp3mUQF","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"aa4a0cff-a4f1-4888-8db7-f2184ea5edb4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:02.390Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means.md","oldString":"(PDF pp. 298-299 / printed pp. 287-288; the text layer reads \"amply a summation\", the page reads as \"simply\" in context).","newString":"(PDF pp. 298-299 / printed pp. 287-288; \"simply\" checked against the page image of PDF p. 298, where the text layer reads \"amply\").","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: sequential vs concurrent means\ncategory: [periodization, programming, sequencing, special-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Ways to Apply Special Strength Means Systematically: Sequential and Concurrent\n\n**What it is** — The authors state that \"The systematic use of means of special strength training over a given period produces a cumulative effect which is significantly greater than the separate non-sequential application of these same means,\" and that there are \"two possible variants for the systematic application of means: sequential and concurrent\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n1. **Sequential means:** \"the strictly determined, successive introduction of different means, each with a higher training effect, into the annual or multi-annual cycle.\" Its basis is two observations: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" and \"the training effect must be maintained by using a complex of means to steadily increase the body's special work capacity.\" Functional changes from earlier means \"should create favourable conditions for achieving the training effect of subsequent means.\" Building such a sequence requires one to \"assess the quantitative and qualitative components of the training effect\" and \"categorise the means relative to the level of the athlete's special fitness\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n2. **Concurrent means:** \"the parallel use of means (over an extended period) that are close to the body's work regime, but which produce a largely different training effect.\" Its basis is evidence that adaptation to a complex of work \"is not simply a summation of the separate effects, but the synergistic result of the interaction of the effects at each stage of training,\" producing \"a new physical state with a significantly greater work potential than would have resulted from the separate non-systematic use of these same means, even at increased volume\" (PDF pp. 298-299 / printed pp. 287-288; the text layer reads \"amply a summation\", the page reads as \"simply\" in context).\n\nThe authors' example of a sequential scheme: endurance development \"begins by training aerobic (cardiorespiratory) processes, then non-oxidative glycolysis and finally high energy phosphagen processes\" (Ozolin 1959, 1970; Volkov 1963; Yakovlev 1957; Borisov 1979; PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the two observations and the synergy claim quoted above. They add that \"the training regime designed to develop a particular motor ability is determined largely by the degree of continuity in sequentially changing means with different training effects\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). They give no physiological mechanism for the synergy. They also say plainly that \"research in the area of the systematic use of means is limited and no definitive practical recommendations have yet emerged,\" and that \"Experimental assessment of the cumulative effects of different variations of the combination of means is necessary\" (PDF pp. 298-299 / printed pp. 287-288).\n\n**When to apply** — Use the two variants as the first design question for any block: are the means introduced one after another, each stronger than the last, or run in parallel because they suit the same work regime but train different things? Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming: incompatible qualities (e.g., maximal strength work and glycolytic-capacity work) are not both targeted at full intensity in the same block ... Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred, transcript and slides). Inference: the authors' \"concurrent\" variant matches the baseline's \"compatible qualities are trained together\"; their \"sequential\" variant is the case where qualities are not trained at full intensity together.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors give this as a framework with \"no definitive practical recommendations\" yet (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287), so neither variant is shown to be better in general. Their own example sequence for endurance (aerobic, then glycolytic, then phosphagen) differs from the speed-endurance sequence they give later in 5.3.3 (aerobic, mixed anaerobic, phosphagen, then glycolysis; PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293); the later scheme is for a different target (speed-endurance), so the two are not a contradiction, but neither is a fixed rule. The synergy claim is stated without data on this page. The section's later experiments (see Related) were on novices and athletes of average qualification.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own sequential example is the endurance order above: aerobic processes first, then non-oxidative glycolysis, then high-energy phosphagens, with the \"sequential introduction of 'anaerobic' work\" creating \"favourable conditions for the training effect of 'aerobic' work\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (4.1.2, why order matters), [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, particular vs cumulative effect), [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]] (4.1.2, structured complex beats volume), [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]] (Issurin's consecutive-development principle)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — The authors state that \"The systematic use of means of special strength training over a given period produces a cumulative effect which is significantly greater than the separate non-sequential application of these same means,\" and that there are \"two possible variants for the systematic application of means: sequential and concurrent\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287)."," 1. **Sequential means:** \"the strictly determined, successive introduction of different means, each with a higher training effect, into the annual or multi-annual cycle.\" Its basis is two observations: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" and \"the training effect must be maintained by using a complex of means to steadily increase the body's special work capacity.\" Functional changes from earlier means \"should create favourable conditions for achieving the training effect of subsequent means.\" Building such a sequence requires one to \"assess the quantitative and qualitative components of the training effect\" and \"categorise the means relative to the level of the athlete's special fitness\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287).","-2. **Concurrent means:** \"the parallel use of means (over an extended period) that are close to the body's work regime, but which produce a largely different training effect.\" Its basis is evidence that adaptation to a complex of work \"is not simply a summation of the separate effects, but the synergistic result of the interaction of the effects at each stage of training,\" producing \"a new physical state with a significantly greater work potential than would have resulted from the separate non-systematic use of these same means, even at increased volume\" (PDF pp. 298-299 / printed pp. 287-288; the text layer reads \"amply a summation\", the page reads as \"simply\" in context).","+2. **Concurrent means:** \"the parallel use of means (over an extended period) that are close to the body's work regime, but which produce a largely different training effect.\" Its basis is evidence that adaptation to a complex of work \"is not simply a summation of the separate effects, but the synergistic result of the interaction of the effects at each stage of training,\" producing \"a new physical state with a significantly greater work potential than would have resulted from the separate non-systematic use of these same means, even at increased volume\" (PDF pp. 298-299 / printed pp. 287-288; \"simply\" checked against the page image of PDF p. 298, where the text layer reads \"amply\")."," "," The authors' example of a sequential scheme: endurance development \"begins by training aerobic (cardiorespiratory) processes, then non-oxidative glycolysis and finally high energy phosphagen processes\" (Ozolin 1959, 1970; Volkov 1963; Yakovlev 1957; Borisov 1979; PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"53ee8005-8c26-4799-8c03-ac6566a22e77","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
