{"parentUuid":"b817c7c6-f40b-426e-96ea-f3dddfbb28b5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa2YxkPCjbvYtVXaMP8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":409,"cache_read_input_tokens":264729,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":409,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":498,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa2J2pgEYQzBLYfJigq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"980de704-b433-47b7-aaf4-b70e27a3944f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:07.731Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"980de704-b433-47b7-aaf4-b70e27a3944f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa2YxkPCjbvYtVXaMP8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mcjgh2Hkfy1nw8g4RDe1oc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: sequencing strength and plyometrics in novices\ncategory: [sequencing, plyometrics, novices, special-strength, post-activation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Novice Sequencing Experiments: Order of Strength and Speed-Strength Means Mattered Little; Depth Jumps Beat Both\n\n**What it is** — The authors report a set of experiments on novices in speed-strength sports (researchers named as \"Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian and Chernousov\", names checked against the page image; no dates, sample sizes or statistics are given) (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288):\n1. **Order across the preparatory period.** \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices. Consequently, the same effect can be obtained by using these means in any order.\"\n2. **Mixed sessions.** \"A relatively larger effect than produced by the above-mentioned variants (with the same volume of training load) occurred in groups who used strength and speed-strength means concurrently by alternating them in mixed training sessions.\" The authors add that \"the contrasting of stimuli in mixed workouts ... undoubtedly has a positive effect.\"\n3. **Weights then depth jumps.** One novice group did weights in stage 1 and depth jumps in stage 2, a second the reverse, a third both in both stages (not in the same workout). \"the first variant was more effective for developing speed-strength.\" The concurrent group improved in stage 1 but slowed in stage 2 \"due to monotonous repetition\"; the reversed group improved most after stage 1 but its speed-strength \"were lowest at the end of the second stage.\"\n4. **Within one workout.** Strength-then-speed-strength vs the reverse, with pauses \"increased slightly to minimise the influence of the positive after-effect\", plus a depth-jump group: \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups. The largest increase in all the parameters of the control movement was discovered in the third group.\" Depth jumps gave \"significantly larger improvement in speed-strength fitness for a smaller volume of training.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for experiment 4: \"any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person. The body is unable to react selectively to a chiefly strength or speed-strength means in one workout. In addition, the period over which the traces of the training stimulus are maintained is apparently longer than the duration of the after-effect of prior tonic work. Within one workout, the traces of all the training tasks are summed\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288). For experiment 3, the reversed group's early gain is put down to \"the larger training effect of the plyometric method.\" No mechanism is given for the \"monotonous repetition\" slowdown.\n\n**When to apply** — With novices, do not spend planning effort on whether strength or speed-strength work comes first in a session; the authors found no significant difference. Across stages, their data favour heavy resistance first and depth jumps after. The authors limit the within-workout finding to novices: \"it is very likely that their lower level of special fitness excluded the possibility for exploiting this phenomenon adequately. A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect ... However, the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288). Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred). Inference: the weights-then-depth-jumps result fits that order for a first-year athlete, though the authors tested speed-strength, not skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The authors' own summary on the next page reads: \"A slightly smaller effect is obtained by the concurrent use of strength and speed-strength means in one training session or their sequential use in successive sessions\" and \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). The first sentence ranks mixed sessions below the best variant, while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave \"A relatively larger effect\"; the second sentence ranks strength-then-speed-strength in one session last, while PDF p. 299 found \"no statistically significant difference\" between that order and its reverse. The PDF p. 299 statement that order \"has no real significance for novices\" also sits uneasily with experiment 3 on the same page, where weights-then-depth-jumps beat the reverse order in novices. These results do not support claims about trained athletes; the authors say so. Their hypothesis that trained athletes benefit from a strength after-effect is untested in this text. Issurin, also in this library, sequences by fatigue tolerance: explosive strength needs a well-rested to slightly fatigued athlete ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]), which is a different rule from either order tested here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' third experiment: novices doing weights in stage 1 and depth jumps in stage 2 developed speed-strength best; the reverse order gained fastest at first but ended lowest (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the conclusions the authors draw), [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (the after-effect within a session), [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":409,"cache_read_input_tokens":264729,"output_tokens":2218,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":46},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":409},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2218,"cache_read_input_tokens":264729,"cache_creation_input_tokens":409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":409,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Mcjgh2Hkfy1nw8g4RDe1oc":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: sequencing strength and plyometrics in novices\ncategory: [sequencing, plyometrics, novices, special-strength, post-activation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Novice Sequencing Experiments: Order of Strength and Speed-Strength Means Mattered Little; Depth Jumps Beat Both\n\n**What it is** — The authors report a set of experiments on novices in speed-strength sports (researchers named as \"Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian and Chernousov\", names checked against the page image; no dates, sample sizes or statistics are given) (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288):\n1. **Order across the preparatory period.** \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices. Consequently, the same effect can be obtained by using these means in any order.\"\n2. **Mixed sessions.** \"A relatively larger effect than produced by the above-mentioned variants (with the same volume of training load) occurred in groups who used strength and speed-strength means concurrently by alternating them in mixed training sessions.\" The authors add that \"the contrasting of stimuli in mixed workouts ... undoubtedly has a positive effect.\"\n3. **Weights then depth jumps.** One novice group did weights in stage 1 and depth jumps in stage 2, a second the reverse, a third both in both stages (not in the same workout). \"the first variant was more effective for developing speed-strength.\" The concurrent group improved in stage 1 but slowed in stage 2 \"due to monotonous repetition\"; the reversed group improved most after stage 1 but its speed-strength \"were lowest at the end of the second stage.\"\n4. **Within one workout.** Strength-then-speed-strength vs the reverse, with pauses \"increased slightly to minimise the influence of the positive after-effect\", plus a depth-jump group: \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups. The largest increase in all the parameters of the control movement was discovered in the third group.\" Depth jumps gave \"significantly larger improvement in speed-strength fitness for a smaller volume of training.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for experiment 4: \"any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person. The body is unable to react selectively to a chiefly strength or speed-strength means in one workout. In addition, the period over which the traces of the training stimulus are maintained is apparently longer than the duration of the after-effect of prior tonic work. Within one workout, the traces of all the training tasks are summed\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288). For experiment 3, the reversed group's early gain is put down to \"the larger training effect of the plyometric method.\" No mechanism is given for the \"monotonous repetition\" slowdown.\n\n**When to apply** — With novices, do not spend planning effort on whether strength or speed-strength work comes first in a session; the authors found no significant difference. Across stages, their data favour heavy resistance first and depth jumps after. The authors limit the within-workout finding to novices: \"it is very likely that their lower level of special fitness excluded the possibility for exploiting this phenomenon adequately. A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect ... However, the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288). Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred). Inference: the weights-then-depth-jumps result fits that order for a first-year athlete, though the authors tested speed-strength, not skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The authors' own summary on the next page reads: \"A slightly smaller effect is obtained by the concurrent use of strength and speed-strength means in one training session or their sequential use in successive sessions\" and \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). The first sentence ranks mixed sessions below the best variant, while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave \"A relatively larger effect\"; the second sentence ranks strength-then-speed-strength in one session last, while PDF p. 299 found \"no statistically significant difference\" between that order and its reverse. The PDF p. 299 statement that order \"has no real significance for novices\" also sits uneasily with experiment 3 on the same page, where weights-then-depth-jumps beat the reverse order in novices. These results do not support claims about trained athletes; the authors say so. Their hypothesis that trained athletes benefit from a strength after-effect is untested in this text. Issurin, also in this library, sequences by fatigue tolerance: explosive strength needs a well-rested to slightly fatigued athlete ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]), which is a different rule from either order tested here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' third experiment: novices doing weights in stage 1 and depth jumps in stage 2 developed speed-strength best; the reverse order gained fastest at first but ended lowest (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the conclusions the authors draw), [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (the after-effect within a session), [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa2J2pgEYQzBLYfJigq","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1ccbb119-db80-4cc8-89c8-820db031ba5a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:24.599Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1ccbb119-db80-4cc8-89c8-820db031ba5a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mcjgh2Hkfy1nw8g4RDe1oc","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"bf278919-f565-43bc-afa6-546551a91b8e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:24.723Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: sequencing strength and plyometrics in novices\ncategory: [sequencing, plyometrics, novices, special-strength, post-activation]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Novice Sequencing Experiments: Order of Strength and Speed-Strength Means Mattered Little; Depth Jumps Beat Both\n\n**What it is** — The authors report a set of experiments on novices in speed-strength sports (researchers named as \"Purvin, Khodykin, Deniskin, Tatian and Chernousov\", names checked against the page image; no dates, sample sizes or statistics are given) (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288):\n1. **Order across the preparatory period.** \"the sequence of means in the preparatory period (beginning with strength, then speed-strength means, or vice-versa) has no real significance for novices. Consequently, the same effect can be obtained by using these means in any order.\"\n2. **Mixed sessions.** \"A relatively larger effect than produced by the above-mentioned variants (with the same volume of training load) occurred in groups who used strength and speed-strength means concurrently by alternating them in mixed training sessions.\" The authors add that \"the contrasting of stimuli in mixed workouts ... undoubtedly has a positive effect.\"\n3. **Weights then depth jumps.** One novice group did weights in stage 1 and depth jumps in stage 2, a second the reverse, a third both in both stages (not in the same workout). \"the first variant was more effective for developing speed-strength.\" The concurrent group improved in stage 1 but slowed in stage 2 \"due to monotonous repetition\"; the reversed group improved most after stage 1 but its speed-strength \"were lowest at the end of the second stage.\"\n4. **Within one workout.** Strength-then-speed-strength vs the reverse, with pauses \"increased slightly to minimise the influence of the positive after-effect\", plus a depth-jump group: \"There was no statistically significant difference in speed-strength of the subjects in the first two groups. The largest increase in all the parameters of the control movement was discovered in the third group.\" Depth jumps gave \"significantly larger improvement in speed-strength fitness for a smaller volume of training.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for experiment 4: \"any training influence will leave significant traces in an untrained person. The body is unable to react selectively to a chiefly strength or speed-strength means in one workout. In addition, the period over which the traces of the training stimulus are maintained is apparently longer than the duration of the after-effect of prior tonic work. Within one workout, the traces of all the training tasks are summed\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288). For experiment 3, the reversed group's early gain is put down to \"the larger training effect of the plyometric method.\" No mechanism is given for the \"monotonous repetition\" slowdown.\n\n**When to apply** — With novices, do not spend planning effort on whether strength or speed-strength work comes first in a session; the authors found no significant difference. Across stages, their data favour heavy resistance first and depth jumps after. The authors limit the within-workout finding to novices: \"it is very likely that their lower level of special fitness excluded the possibility for exploiting this phenomenon adequately. A well-trained athlete can expect the preceding strength work to have a positive effect ... However, the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288). Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred). Inference: the weights-then-depth-jumps result fits that order for a first-year athlete, though the authors tested speed-strength, not skill.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The authors' own summary on the next page reads: \"A slightly smaller effect is obtained by the concurrent use of strength and speed-strength means in one training session or their sequential use in successive sessions\" and \"Least effective is the sequence of strength followed by speed-strength means during the same session\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). The first sentence ranks mixed sessions below the best variant, while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave \"A relatively larger effect\"; the second sentence ranks strength-then-speed-strength in one session last, while PDF p. 299 found \"no statistically significant difference\" between that order and its reverse. The PDF p. 299 statement that order \"has no real significance for novices\" also sits uneasily with experiment 3 on the same page, where weights-then-depth-jumps beat the reverse order in novices. These results do not support claims about trained athletes; the authors say so. Their hypothesis that trained athletes benefit from a strength after-effect is untested in this text. Issurin, also in this library, sequences by fatigue tolerance: explosive strength needs a well-rested to slightly fatigued athlete ([[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]), which is a different rule from either order tested here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' third experiment: novices doing weights in stage 1 and depth jumps in stage 2 developed speed-strength best; the reverse order gained fastest at first but ended lowest (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the conclusions the authors draw), [[framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing]] (the after-effect within a session), [[claim--pap-findings-contested-western-vs-russian-literature]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1ccbb119-db80-4cc8-89c8-820db031ba5a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"bf278919-f565-43bc-afa6-546551a91b8e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14732642 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"efac4efb-e45f-4abe-83b4-06bfdf5efdaf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:24.730Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14732642 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"efac4efb-e45f-4abe-83b4-06bfdf5efdaf","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa4JYdtDhnqTbF23tsD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2345,"cache_read_input_tokens":265138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2345,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":467,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffa3wUsNDQmMEiTDu1YY","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8a1cae7c-3c10-43d6-aeae-d47bb646922b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:31.469Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8a1cae7c-3c10-43d6-aeae-d47bb646922b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa4JYdtDhnqTbF23tsD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mk9Qm7vG3berAWsgGz2taC","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: stage order of jump and barbell means\ncategory: [sequencing, plyometrics, periodization, jumpers, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Across Stages: Jumps, Then Barbell Work, Then Depth Jumps; Staying on One Means Stalls or Reverses Progress\n\n**What it is** — Three \"equivalent groups of jumpers of average qualification\" trained \"Under natural training conditions\" (Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970; Fig 5.13, verified against the page image; PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). All used special jumping exercises in stage 1. Group 1 stayed on jumps for all three stages. Group 2 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and stayed there. Group 3 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and to depth jumps in stage 3. \"The special strength training indicators displayed a clear tendency to stabilise in groups 1 and 2 after the first and second stages respectively, and in group 1, even to decrease during the third stage.\" The figure shows group 3's line rising through all three stages, group 2's flat in stage 3, and group 1's flattening in stage 2 and dipping in stage 3. The authors conclude: \"the sequential use of means with higher, specific training effects clearly produces a positive result. At the same time, the use of the same means, even with an increase in volume, not only does not produce a positive change, but decreases the existing level of special strength fitness.\" From this and the novice work they draw two rules \"for the sequential use of training means over a given period\" (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290):\n- \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"\n- \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the sequential-means logic of 5.3: each means loses effect as the body adapts, so a stronger means must follow (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). Fig 5.13 shows only the direction of the control indicators; it has no numerical axis. No physiological mechanism is given for why depth jumps must follow, not precede, strength work. Inference: the deniskin laboratory study on the next page (heavy resistance, then two depth-jump stages) is the authors' supporting case for the second rule; see Related.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering the means for jumpers or other speed-strength athletes over an off-season: general jumping first, barbell strength next, intense depth jumps last. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred). Inference: the authors' order also runs from lower to higher training effect, which fits that rule; for a football off-season this would read as general jumps and med-ball work, then a barbell-strength block, then depth jumps closer to the period where speed-strength is needed.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The same summary list ranks variants differently from the experiments reported a page earlier: it calls strength followed by speed-strength in the same session \"Least effective\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289), while PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288 found \"no statistically significant difference\" between that order and its reverse in novices, and it puts mixed sessions below the best variant while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave \"A relatively larger effect\" (see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The rules come from novices and \"athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 300); the authors say so in the lead-in to the list. No group sizes, statistics or units are given for Fig 5.13. The first rule is about order over a period (stages), not within one session; it should not be read as \"lift after you jump\" in a single workout, which is a separate question. Issurin 2016 questions the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional sequencing in team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: group 3 (jumps, then barbell, then depth jumps) kept improving through stage 3, while group 1 (jumps throughout) levelled off and then declined (Fig 5.13, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]] (Fig 5.18 gives the same order with \"jumps with weights\" added), [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2345,"cache_read_input_tokens":265138,"output_tokens":1944,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":74},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2345},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1944,"cache_read_input_tokens":265138,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2345,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2345,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Mk9Qm7vG3berAWsgGz2taC":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: stage order of jump and barbell means\ncategory: [sequencing, plyometrics, periodization, jumpers, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Across Stages: Jumps, Then Barbell Work, Then Depth Jumps; Staying on One Means Stalls or Reverses Progress\n\n**What it is** — Three \"equivalent groups of jumpers of average qualification\" trained \"Under natural training conditions\" (Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970; Fig 5.13, verified against the page image; PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). All used special jumping exercises in stage 1. Group 1 stayed on jumps for all three stages. Group 2 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and stayed there. Group 3 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and to depth jumps in stage 3. \"The special strength training indicators displayed a clear tendency to stabilise in groups 1 and 2 after the first and second stages respectively, and in group 1, even to decrease during the third stage.\" The figure shows group 3's line rising through all three stages, group 2's flat in stage 3, and group 1's flattening in stage 2 and dipping in stage 3. The authors conclude: \"the sequential use of means with higher, specific training effects clearly produces a positive result. At the same time, the use of the same means, even with an increase in volume, not only does not produce a positive change, but decreases the existing level of special strength fitness.\" From this and the novice work they draw two rules \"for the sequential use of training means over a given period\" (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290):\n- \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"\n- \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the sequential-means logic of 5.3: each means loses effect as the body adapts, so a stronger means must follow (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). Fig 5.13 shows only the direction of the control indicators; it has no numerical axis. No physiological mechanism is given for why depth jumps must follow, not precede, strength work. Inference: the deniskin laboratory study on the next page (heavy resistance, then two depth-jump stages) is the authors' supporting case for the second rule; see Related.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering the means for jumpers or other speed-strength athletes over an off-season: general jumping first, barbell strength next, intense depth jumps last. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred). Inference: the authors' order also runs from lower to higher training effect, which fits that rule; for a football off-season this would read as general jumps and med-ball work, then a barbell-strength block, then depth jumps closer to the period where speed-strength is needed.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The same summary list ranks variants differently from the experiments reported a page earlier: it calls strength followed by speed-strength in the same session \"Least effective\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289), while PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288 found \"no statistically significant difference\" between that order and its reverse in novices, and it puts mixed sessions below the best variant while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave \"A relatively larger effect\" (see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The rules come from novices and \"athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 300); the authors say so in the lead-in to the list. No group sizes, statistics or units are given for Fig 5.13. The first rule is about order over a period (stages), not within one session; it should not be read as \"lift after you jump\" in a single workout, which is a separate question. Issurin 2016 questions the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional sequencing in team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: group 3 (jumps, then barbell, then depth jumps) kept improving through stage 3, while group 1 (jumps throughout) levelled off and then declined (Fig 5.13, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]] (Fig 5.18 gives the same order with \"jumps with weights\" added), [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa3wUsNDQmMEiTDu1YY","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"06cd6871-d2df-4b00-b6b7-2b0ef6674c55","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:47.097Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
