{"parentUuid":"e2846eb7-83f0-4017-9ad4-fc05db359183","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa5v5684eWkXPGA5LxN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PrxmCGqmcTkeecXW6TT8xA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md","old_string":"Inference: the deniskin laboratory study","new_string":"Inference: the Deniskin (1976) laboratory study"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2077,"cache_read_input_tokens":267483,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2077,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PrxmCGqmcTkeecXW6TT8xA":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md","old_string":"Inference: the deniskin laboratory study","new_string":"Inference: the Deniskin (1976) laboratory study"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa5bjmXQ8VqNDvNvsTu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c4594691-c9e2-4f47-af33-f8a090bd51dd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:54.505Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c4594691-c9e2-4f47-af33-f8a090bd51dd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PrxmCGqmcTkeecXW6TT8xA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"fc0801b8-11f6-4c95-885b-340f21d75a9b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:51:54.622Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md","oldString":"Inference: the deniskin laboratory study","newString":"Inference: the Deniskin (1976) laboratory study","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: stage order of jump and barbell means\ncategory: [sequencing, plyometrics, periodization, jumpers, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Across Stages: Jumps, Then Barbell Work, Then Depth Jumps; Staying on One Means Stalls or Reverses Progress\n\n**What it is** — Three \"equivalent groups of jumpers of average qualification\" trained \"Under natural training conditions\" (Verkhoshansky & Aganin 1970; Fig 5.13, verified against the page image; PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). All used special jumping exercises in stage 1. Group 1 stayed on jumps for all three stages. Group 2 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and stayed there. Group 3 switched to barbell exercises in stage 2 and to depth jumps in stage 3. \"The special strength training indicators displayed a clear tendency to stabilise in groups 1 and 2 after the first and second stages respectively, and in group 1, even to decrease during the third stage.\" The figure shows group 3's line rising through all three stages, group 2's flat in stage 3, and group 1's flattening in stage 2 and dipping in stage 3. The authors conclude: \"the sequential use of means with higher, specific training effects clearly produces a positive result. At the same time, the use of the same means, even with an increase in volume, not only does not produce a positive change, but decreases the existing level of special strength fitness.\" From this and the novice work they draw two rules \"for the sequential use of training means over a given period\" (PDF pp. 300-301 / printed pp. 289-290):\n- \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\"\n- \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the sequential-means logic of 5.3: each means loses effect as the body adapts, so a stronger means must follow (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). Fig 5.13 shows only the direction of the control indicators; it has no numerical axis. No physiological mechanism is given for why depth jumps must follow, not precede, strength work. Inference: the deniskin laboratory study on the next page (heavy resistance, then two depth-jump stages) is the authors' supporting case for the second rule; see Related.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering the means for jumpers or other speed-strength athletes over an off-season: general jumping first, barbell strength next, intense depth jumps last. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred). Inference: the authors' order also runs from lower to higher training effect, which fits that rule; for a football off-season this would read as general jumps and med-ball work, then a barbell-strength block, then depth jumps closer to the period where speed-strength is needed.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The same summary list ranks variants differently from the experiments reported a page earlier: it calls strength followed by speed-strength in the same session \"Least effective\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289), while PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288 found \"no statistically significant difference\" between that order and its reverse in novices, and it puts mixed sessions below the best variant while PDF p. 299 says mixed sessions gave \"A relatively larger effect\" (see [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]). The rules come from novices and \"athletes of average qualification\" (PDF p. 300); the authors say so in the lead-in to the list. No group sizes, statistics or units are given for Fig 5.13. The first rule is about order over a period (stages), not within one session; it should not be read as \"lift after you jump\" in a single workout, which is a separate question. Issurin 2016 questions the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional sequencing in team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: group 3 (jumps, then barbell, then depth jumps) kept improving through stage 3, while group 1 (jumps throughout) levelled off and then declined (Fig 5.13, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]], [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]] (Fig 5.18 gives the same order with \"jumps with weights\" added), [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--optimal-depth-jump-drop-height-for-power-vs-force]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - \"Strength exercises should be done after speed-strength exercises in training\""," - \"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before.\""," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the sequential-means logic of 5.3: each means loses effect as the body adapts, so a stronger means must follow (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). Fig 5.13 shows only the direction of the control indicators; it has no numerical axis. No physiological mechanism is given for why depth jumps must follow, not precede, strength work. Inference: the deniskin laboratory study on the next page (heavy resistance, then two depth-jump stages) is the authors' supporting case for the second rule; see Related.","+**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the sequential-means logic of 5.3: each means loses effect as the body adapts, so a stronger means must follow (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). Fig 5.13 shows only the direction of the control indicators; it has no numerical axis. No physiological mechanism is given for why depth jumps must follow, not precede, strength work. Inference: the Deniskin (1976) laboratory study on the next page (heavy resistance, then two depth-jump stages) is the authors' supporting case for the second rule; see Related."," "," **When to apply** — Ordering the means for jumpers or other speed-strength athletes over an off-season: general jumping first, barbell strength next, intense depth jumps last. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred). Inference: the authors' order also runs from lower to higher training effect, which fits that rule; for a football off-season this would read as general jumps and med-ball work, then a barbell-strength block, then depth jumps closer to the period where speed-strength is needed."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c4594691-c9e2-4f47-af33-f8a090bd51dd","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fc0801b8-11f6-4c95-885b-340f21d75a9b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa5v5684eWkXPGA5LxN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015s1i7N4JADvAqKk7EgViPc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: jump training for junior sprinters\ncategory: [sprinting, plyometrics, jump-training, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Junior Sprinters: Short Jumps Trained Stride, Long Jumps Trained Top Speed, and the Combination Was Called a Summation\n\n**What it is** — \"In an experiment with junior class sprinters\" three groups trained \"for a period of eight months\": one did mainly short jumps (repeated jumps on one and two legs, triple and quintuple standing broad jumps), one mainly long jumping exercises (\"multiple jumps on one leg or from leg to leg over distances of 30-100m\"), and one did both (PDF pp. 299-300 / printed pp. 288-289). The authors report: \"The short jumping exercises developed primarily length and speed of the running stride, whereas the long jumping exercises largely increased maximum running speed and speed-strength endurance. The combination of short and long jumping exercises led to a summation of their separate training effects and the development of speed-strength abilities which are specific to sprinters\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). Fig 5.12 (verified against the page image) plots the decrease in sprint time in seconds for each group at 30 m and 60 m from a low start (LS) and a standing start (SS), and at 100 m from a low start. Approximate bar heights read from the image (short / long / combined): 30 m LS about 0.40 / 0.08 / 0.29 s; 30 m SS about 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20 s; 60 m LS about 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33 s; 60 m SS about 0.08 / 0.37 / 0.43 s; 100 m LS about 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0 s (the 100 m panel uses a 0 to 1.0 s scale). No group sizes or statistics are given.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the outcome labels: short jumps act on stride length and stride speed, long jumps on maximum speed and speed-strength endurance, and the two \"summate.\" Inference: the split matches the jump durations (brief standing multi-jumps vs bounding over 30-100 m), but the text does not draw that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing jump means for sprint development: the authors' data suggest short, standing multi-jumps and long bounding series train different parts of the sprint, so a sprint-focused off-season can include both. Inference: for football, short jumps map to the start and early acceleration distances and long bounds to the longer runs, but the authors tested junior track sprinters, not football players.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect \"a summation of their separate training effects,\" but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: over 100 m from a low start, the combined jump group improved by about 1.0 s, against about 0.27 s for short jumps alone and about 0.50 s for long jumps alone (Fig 5.12, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] (the concurrent variant this experiment illustrates), [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2077,"cache_read_input_tokens":267483,"output_tokens":1876,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":41},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2077},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1876,"cache_read_input_tokens":267483,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2077,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2077,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015s1i7N4JADvAqKk7EgViPc":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: jump training for junior sprinters\ncategory: [sprinting, plyometrics, jump-training, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Junior Sprinters: Short Jumps Trained Stride, Long Jumps Trained Top Speed, and the Combination Was Called a Summation\n\n**What it is** — \"In an experiment with junior class sprinters\" three groups trained \"for a period of eight months\": one did mainly short jumps (repeated jumps on one and two legs, triple and quintuple standing broad jumps), one mainly long jumping exercises (\"multiple jumps on one leg or from leg to leg over distances of 30-100m\"), and one did both (PDF pp. 299-300 / printed pp. 288-289). The authors report: \"The short jumping exercises developed primarily length and speed of the running stride, whereas the long jumping exercises largely increased maximum running speed and speed-strength endurance. The combination of short and long jumping exercises led to a summation of their separate training effects and the development of speed-strength abilities which are specific to sprinters\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). Fig 5.12 (verified against the page image) plots the decrease in sprint time in seconds for each group at 30 m and 60 m from a low start (LS) and a standing start (SS), and at 100 m from a low start. Approximate bar heights read from the image (short / long / combined): 30 m LS about 0.40 / 0.08 / 0.29 s; 30 m SS about 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20 s; 60 m LS about 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33 s; 60 m SS about 0.08 / 0.37 / 0.43 s; 100 m LS about 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0 s (the 100 m panel uses a 0 to 1.0 s scale). No group sizes or statistics are given.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the outcome labels: short jumps act on stride length and stride speed, long jumps on maximum speed and speed-strength endurance, and the two \"summate.\" Inference: the split matches the jump durations (brief standing multi-jumps vs bounding over 30-100 m), but the text does not draw that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing jump means for sprint development: the authors' data suggest short, standing multi-jumps and long bounding series train different parts of the sprint, so a sprint-focused off-season can include both. Inference: for football, short jumps map to the start and early acceleration distances and long bounds to the longer runs, but the authors tested junior track sprinters, not football players.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect \"a summation of their separate training effects,\" but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: over 100 m from a low start, the combined jump group improved by about 1.0 s, against about 0.27 s for short jumps alone and about 0.50 s for long jumps alone (Fig 5.12, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] (the concurrent variant this experiment illustrates), [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffa5bjmXQ8VqNDvNvsTu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b8985339-3d97-4a2f-8389-3749639594c4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:08.420Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b8985339-3d97-4a2f-8389-3749639594c4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015s1i7N4JADvAqKk7EgViPc","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"88e2c1f1-2222-408f-ba24-9a9be66191f1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:08.526Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: jump training for junior sprinters\ncategory: [sprinting, plyometrics, jump-training, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Junior Sprinters: Short Jumps Trained Stride, Long Jumps Trained Top Speed, and the Combination Was Called a Summation\n\n**What it is** — \"In an experiment with junior class sprinters\" three groups trained \"for a period of eight months\": one did mainly short jumps (repeated jumps on one and two legs, triple and quintuple standing broad jumps), one mainly long jumping exercises (\"multiple jumps on one leg or from leg to leg over distances of 30-100m\"), and one did both (PDF pp. 299-300 / printed pp. 288-289). The authors report: \"The short jumping exercises developed primarily length and speed of the running stride, whereas the long jumping exercises largely increased maximum running speed and speed-strength endurance. The combination of short and long jumping exercises led to a summation of their separate training effects and the development of speed-strength abilities which are specific to sprinters\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). Fig 5.12 (verified against the page image) plots the decrease in sprint time in seconds for each group at 30 m and 60 m from a low start (LS) and a standing start (SS), and at 100 m from a low start. Approximate bar heights read from the image (short / long / combined): 30 m LS about 0.40 / 0.08 / 0.29 s; 30 m SS about 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20 s; 60 m LS about 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33 s; 60 m SS about 0.08 / 0.37 / 0.43 s; 100 m LS about 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0 s (the 100 m panel uses a 0 to 1.0 s scale). No group sizes or statistics are given.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the outcome labels: short jumps act on stride length and stride speed, long jumps on maximum speed and speed-strength endurance, and the two \"summate.\" Inference: the split matches the jump durations (brief standing multi-jumps vs bounding over 30-100 m), but the text does not draw that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing jump means for sprint development: the authors' data suggest short, standing multi-jumps and long bounding series train different parts of the sprint, so a sprint-focused off-season can include both. Inference: for football, short jumps map to the start and early acceleration distances and long bounds to the longer runs, but the authors tested junior track sprinters, not football players.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect \"a summation of their separate training effects,\" but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: over 100 m from a low start, the combined jump group improved by about 1.0 s, against about 0.27 s for short jumps alone and about 0.50 s for long jumps alone (Fig 5.12, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] (the concurrent variant this experiment illustrates), [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b8985339-3d97-4a2f-8389-3749639594c4","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
