{"parentUuid":"a079b1ed-5a7f-4bf1-9e79-38588bbbac6f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa7QHzNfCL2mZXFE9X2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VBe2A1ZT8tempV4869BVC4","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md","old_string":"The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others.","new_string":"The combined group is largest at the other four distances. Read against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is about equal to it at 30 m SS (0.20 vs 0.07 + 0.13) and 60 m SS (0.43 vs 0.08 + 0.37), above it at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.27 + 0.50), and below it at 60 m LS (0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_read_input_tokens":269560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2159,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VBe2A1ZT8tempV4869BVC4":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md","old_string":"The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others.","new_string":"The combined group is largest at the other four distances. Read against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is about equal to it at 30 m SS (0.20 vs 0.07 + 0.13) and 60 m SS (0.43 vs 0.08 + 0.37), above it at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.27 + 0.50), and below it at 60 m LS (0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffa7AuLShhngXNrUAYqd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9dac79fe-828f-4f0a-b1dc-acef5237d6bf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:17.167Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9dac79fe-828f-4f0a-b1dc-acef5237d6bf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VBe2A1ZT8tempV4869BVC4","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"c352ee11-6314-4642-9bee-6e64388dc622","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:17.269Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--junior-sprinters-short-and-long-jump-training-effects.md","oldString":"The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others.","newString":"The combined group is largest at the other four distances. Read against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is about equal to it at 30 m SS (0.20 vs 0.07 + 0.13) and 60 m SS (0.43 vs 0.08 + 0.37), above it at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.27 + 0.50), and below it at 60 m LS (0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: jump training for junior sprinters\ncategory: [sprinting, plyometrics, jump-training, special-strength]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Junior Sprinters: Short Jumps Trained Stride, Long Jumps Trained Top Speed, and the Combination Was Called a Summation\n\n**What it is** — \"In an experiment with junior class sprinters\" three groups trained \"for a period of eight months\": one did mainly short jumps (repeated jumps on one and two legs, triple and quintuple standing broad jumps), one mainly long jumping exercises (\"multiple jumps on one leg or from leg to leg over distances of 30-100m\"), and one did both (PDF pp. 299-300 / printed pp. 288-289). The authors report: \"The short jumping exercises developed primarily length and speed of the running stride, whereas the long jumping exercises largely increased maximum running speed and speed-strength endurance. The combination of short and long jumping exercises led to a summation of their separate training effects and the development of speed-strength abilities which are specific to sprinters\" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). Fig 5.12 (verified against the page image) plots the decrease in sprint time in seconds for each group at 30 m and 60 m from a low start (LS) and a standing start (SS), and at 100 m from a low start. Approximate bar heights read from the image (short / long / combined): 30 m LS about 0.40 / 0.08 / 0.29 s; 30 m SS about 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20 s; 60 m LS about 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33 s; 60 m SS about 0.08 / 0.37 / 0.43 s; 100 m LS about 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0 s (the 100 m panel uses a 0 to 1.0 s scale). No group sizes or statistics are given.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the outcome labels: short jumps act on stride length and stride speed, long jumps on maximum speed and speed-strength endurance, and the two \"summate.\" Inference: the split matches the jump durations (brief standing multi-jumps vs bounding over 30-100 m), but the text does not draw that link.\n\n**When to apply** — Choosing jump means for sprint development: the authors' data suggest short, standing multi-jumps and long bounding series train different parts of the sprint, so a sprint-focused off-season can include both. Inference: for football, short jumps map to the start and early acceleration distances and long bounds to the longer runs, but the authors tested junior track sprinters, not football players.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect \"a summation of their separate training effects,\" but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: over 100 m from a low start, the combined jump group improved by about 1.0 s, against about 0.27 s for short jumps alone and about 0.50 s for long jumps alone (Fig 5.12, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] (the concurrent variant this experiment illustrates), [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Choosing jump means for sprint development: the authors' data suggest short, standing multi-jumps and long bounding series train different parts of the sprint, so a sprint-focused off-season can include both. Inference: for football, short jumps map to the start and early acceleration distances and long bounds to the longer runs, but the authors tested junior track sprinters, not football players."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect \"a summation of their separate training effects,\" but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at 30 m SS, 60 m LS, 60 m SS and 100 m LS. At 60 m SS and 100 m LS the combined bar is larger than either single group, but it is not the sum of the two at 60 m LS (about 0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect \"a summation of their separate training effects,\" but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at the other four distances. Read against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is about equal to it at 30 m SS (0.20 vs 0.07 + 0.13) and 60 m SS (0.43 vs 0.08 + 0.37), above it at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.27 + 0.50), and below it at 60 m LS (0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So \"summation\" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: over 100 m from a low start, the combined jump group improved by about 1.0 s, against about 0.27 s for short jumps alone and about 0.50 s for long jumps alone (Fig 5.12, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9dac79fe-828f-4f0a-b1dc-acef5237d6bf","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c352ee11-6314-4642-9bee-6e64388dc622","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffa7QHzNfCL2mZXFE9X2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VWskZooM2p9xXAMqj7dnGn","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: depth jumps after resistance block\ncategory: [plyometrics, sequencing, recovery, explosive-strength, weightlifting]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Deniskin (1976): A Resistance Stage, Then Two Depth-Jump Stages, With Complete Rest Between Them\n\n**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were given a complete rest between stages to enhance supercompensation\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Fig 5.14 (verified against the page image) lays the stages out by week: resistance weeks 1-7, rest week 8, depth jumps weeks 9-15, rest week 16, depth jumps weeks 17-21, rest week 22, testing weeks 23-24. It plots Fmax (kgf), tmax (s, time to Fmax), starting strength (Ss) and explosive strength (Se). Read by eye from the image: Fmax rises from about 103 to about 110 kgf in stage 1 and reaches about 125 kgf by stage 4; tmax falls from about 0.42 s to about 0.27-0.32 s; Ss and Se both rise through stages 1-3, and Ss falls back from its stage-3 high at the stage-4 test.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' conclusion: \"the plyometric method produces an additional, significant increase in the level of explosive strength that was first attained with resistance training,\" which shows \"the use of these means in two appropriate stages: the first stage, which improves explosive strength, and the second stage, which stabilises the level of special fitness.\" On rest: \"There was a significant increase in all the control indicators after a period of complete rest following the first and second stages. This emphasizes that rest may be used as a training means. This is not a paradox, because growth and maintenance processes take place predominantly during sleep and other forms of passive or active rest\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). That sentence is the authors' mechanism; they cite nothing for it.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after 7 weeks of resistance work, a rest week, then 7 and 5 weeks of depth jumps with a rest week after each, Fmax in the leg extension had risen from about 103 to about 125 kgf at the stage-4 test (Fig 5.14, PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290; values read from the image).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (ES, plyometrics and heavy resistance, same research group), [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_read_input_tokens":269560,"output_tokens":2503,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":205},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2159},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2503,"cache_read_input_tokens":269560,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2159,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2159,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VWskZooM2p9xXAMqj7dnGn":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: depth jumps after resistance block\ncategory: [plyometrics, sequencing, recovery, explosive-strength, weightlifting]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Deniskin (1976): A Resistance Stage, Then Two Depth-Jump Stages, With Complete Rest Between Them\n\n**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were given a complete rest between stages to enhance supercompensation\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Fig 5.14 (verified against the page image) lays the stages out by week: resistance weeks 1-7, rest week 8, depth jumps weeks 9-15, rest week 16, depth jumps weeks 17-21, rest week 22, testing weeks 23-24. It plots Fmax (kgf), tmax (s, time to Fmax), starting strength (Ss) and explosive strength (Se). Read by eye from the image: Fmax rises from about 103 to about 110 kgf in stage 1 and reaches about 125 kgf by stage 4; tmax falls from about 0.42 s to about 0.27-0.32 s; Ss and Se both rise through stages 1-3, and Ss falls back from its stage-3 high at the stage-4 test.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' conclusion: \"the plyometric method produces an additional, significant increase in the level of explosive strength that was first attained with resistance training,\" which shows \"the use of these means in two appropriate stages: the first stage, which improves explosive strength, and the second stage, which stabilises the level of special fitness.\" On rest: \"There was a significant increase in all the control indicators after a period of complete rest following the first and second stages. This emphasizes that rest may be used as a training means. This is not a paradox, because growth and maintenance processes take place predominantly during sleep and other forms of passive or active rest\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). That sentence is the authors' mechanism; they cite nothing for it.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after 7 weeks of resistance work, a rest week, then 7 and 5 weeks of depth jumps with a rest week after each, Fmax in the leg extension had risen from about 103 to about 125 kgf at the stage-4 test (Fig 5.14, PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290; values read from the image).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (ES, plyometrics and heavy resistance, same research group), [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffa7AuLShhngXNrUAYqd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"32ee60f1-653e-4972-a001-07af02744d0e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:32.032Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"32ee60f1-653e-4972-a001-07af02744d0e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VWskZooM2p9xXAMqj7dnGn","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"2688e2cb-2b3d-4196-aa3c-886133a1b3a7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:52:32.148Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: depth jumps after resistance block\ncategory: [plyometrics, sequencing, recovery, explosive-strength, weightlifting]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Deniskin (1976): A Resistance Stage, Then Two Depth-Jump Stages, With Complete Rest Between Them\n\n**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test