eline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after 7 weeks of resistance work, a rest week, then 7 and 5 weeks of depth jumps with a rest week after each, Fmax in the leg extension had risen from about 103 to about 125 kgf at the stage-4 test (Fig 5.14, PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290; values read from the image).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (ES, plyometrics and heavy resistance, same research group), [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"32ee60f1-653e-4972-a001-07af02744d0e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint",