Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after 7 weeks of resistance work, a rest week, then 7 and 5 weeks of depth jumps with a rest week after each, Fmax in the leg extension had risen from about 103 to about 125 kgf at the stage-4 test (Fig 5.14, PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290; values read from the image).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (ES, plyometrics and heavy resistance, same research group), [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' conclusion: \"the plyometric method produces an additional, significant increase in the level of explosive strength that was first attained with resistance training,\" which shows \"the use of these means in two appropriate stages: the first stage, which improves explosive strength, and the second stage, which stabilises the level of special fitness.\" On rest: \"There was a significant increase in all the control indicators after a period of complete rest following the first and second stages. This emphasizes that rest may be used as a training means. This is not a paradox, because growth and maintenance processes take place predominantly during sleep and other forms of passive or active rest\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). That sentence is the authors' mechanism; they cite nothing for it."," ","-**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery capacity must be 'earned' by training hard enough first\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.","+**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript): Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first. Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f9d499b9-e54c-4f22-b1f9-889258f95970","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
