action speed of trained muscles\"; Filinov, \"excessively heavy strength loads diminish the force and speed of boxers' punches\"; Deniskin (1976), \"prolonged use of high volume strength loads diminishes the power and speed of weightlifters, high jumpers and triple jumpers.\" On dose: \"speed-strength and strength-speed cease to increase after a certain volume of heavy strength training has been executed. Deniskin observed this phenomenon after 18 weeks (54 workouts) of strength training. Other studies have also noted the same phenomenon after 50-60 training sessions or approximately 12 weeks of training.\" On technique, Siff writes (the passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person): \"research by Verkhoshansky and colleagues has established that excessive maximum strength training can impair speed-strength and technical skill in boxers (Verkhoshansky, 1977). It can also lead to a deterioration of several months' duration in the technique of weightlifters and javelin throwers\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for the technique loss: \"The negative influence of heavy strength training on sports technique may be due substantially to deterioration of the speed-strength qualities of the neuromuscular system,\" and \"heavy training loads reduce one's ability to control movements, primarily of their most complex technical phases\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). For the plateau they give a dose argument only: \"there are certain volumes and durations of strength training that are essential for achieving an optimum training effect. Exceeding these optimal values renders any further increase in training volume not only unproductive, but detrimental.\" No cellular mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small. Cap a heavy-strength emphasis well before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, and change the means. The authors also warn against prescribing heavy physical and technical work together for highly qualified athletes: a \"literal interpretation of this concept of unified training by the concurrent prescription of physical and technical training leads to serious error,\" because it \"contradicts the well-known need for the athlete to practise technical skills when he is fresh\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred). Inference: the authors' warning is about volume, not intensity, which fits that rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 30% figure is \"as much as,\" with no study named for it. The Kotz and Filinov reports carry no dates or data here. The 12-18 week figures are for \"heavy strength training\" in speed-strength athletes; the authors do not say the same plateau applies to novices or to lower-volume strength work. The section does not argue against heavy strength work in general: it calls it necessary for absolute strength and general conditioning, and criticises only extensive use during specialised phases. The authors' wider complaint (PDF p. 302) that training means are applied in a \"rubber-stamp way\" in which \"Virtually the only factors that change are the load and overall volume\" is an opinion, not a finding.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Deniskin saw speed-strength and strength-speed stop rising after 18 weeks (54 workouts) of strength training; other studies saw it after 50-60 sessions, about 12 weeks (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).\n\n**Related** — [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]] (swimmers' explosive strength fell under concurrent endurance and strength work; agreement on interference, different cause), [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-a