{"parentUuid":"aadb9aa7-56ea-43af-9f06-30a033639a49","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FeHyeGfx14qj9jEZw4vv4A","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"de6c883e-85b6-45ac-84d0-d9ab31a9c922","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:53:26.415Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect.md","oldString":"Inference: this is the same logic as the 4.1.2 generalization that \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the athlete's special physical fitness increases.\"","newString":"Inference: this is the same logic as the first 4.1.2 generalization (the training effect of any means diminishes as special physical fitness increases; see the taxonomy entry in Related).","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.2\"\ntopic: sequential system of means\ncategory: [periodization, exercise-selection, progression, special-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Introduce Means of Gradually Greater Training Effect, Matched to the Athlete's Current Special Fitness\n\n**What it is** — In 5.3.2, \"A Sequential System of Training Means,\" the authors argue that the development of a specific motor ability \"occurs in a specific pattern,\" so \"it is necessary to sequentially improve the functioning of those systems and physiological mechanisms which are crucial for developing a specific motor ability\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). \"Progressive functional changes in some systems act as prerequisites for improving the performance of other systems.\" The rule: \"the means used should elicit a definite training effect, but its magnitude should correspond to the body's current level of specific fitness and increase in conjunction with the latter.\" Hence \"the necessity ... for devising a scheme for sequentially introducing means of gradually greater training effect into training.\" The new means \"should be novel, so as to promote a greater training effect by compelling the adaptive processes to transcend any performance plateau\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason is that earlier functional changes are prerequisites for later ones, and that \"Specificity becomes more relevant to one's state of fitness as sports proficiency increases. At the same time, the role of the means used to develop trainability grows\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). Novelty is given as the way past a plateau; no physiological mechanism is named. Inference: this is the same logic as the 4.1.2 generalization that \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the athlete's special physical fitness increases.\"\n\n**When to apply** — When selecting means for a block, check that each one is strong enough for the athlete's current level and no stronger than needed, and plan the next, stronger means before the current one stops working. The authors name two common errors to avoid: means \"selected on the spur of the moment without considering their precise training effect and often on the basis of what is currently fashionable or subjectively preferred,\" and \"means which are highly effective during more advanced stages of training ... used inappropriately during the early stages of the yearly cycle or in the beginning stages of multi-year training.\" They also criticise a repertoire that \"is usually extremely limited and is repeated in much the same way from year to year\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102, Inferred). Inference: the authors' \"gradually greater training effect\" is a second axis on that spectrum: within a category, means can be ranked by strength of effect.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors note a limit of the evidence: \"Research has investigated changing the special training means only once or twice during the period of study,\" while practical use needs \"multiple changes\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292), so multi-change sequences are argued, not tested, here. The principle needs a ranking of means by training effect, which the authors say requires \"experimental assessment\" (PDF p. 299 / printed p. 288); without it the ranking is a guess. \"Novel\" means are not the same as random variety: the authors elsewhere call random combination less effective (see Related).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own error case: highly effective advanced means (their example category, not a named exercise) used at the start of a yearly cycle or of a young athlete's career (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). Inference: depth jumps from high boxes in a freshman's first summer would be that error; the 5.3 jumper study placed them in the third stage.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the five generalizations), [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]] (5.4.4, the same principle applied), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--plan-variation-to-avoid-restrictive-neural-patterns]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — In 5.3.2, \"A Sequential System of Training Means,\" the authors argue that the development of a specific motor ability \"occurs in a specific pattern,\" so \"it is necessary to sequentially improve the functioning of those systems and physiological mechanisms which are crucial for developing a specific motor ability\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). \"Progressive functional changes in some systems act as prerequisites for improving the performance of other systems.\" The rule: \"the means used should elicit a definite training effect, but its magnitude should correspond to the body's current level of specific fitness and increase in conjunction with the latter.\" Hence \"the necessity ... for devising a scheme for sequentially introducing means of gradually greater training effect into training.\" The new means \"should be novel, so as to promote a greater training effect by compelling the adaptive processes to transcend any performance plateau\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason is that earlier functional changes are prerequisites for later ones, and that \"Specificity becomes more relevant to one's state of fitness as sports proficiency increases. At the same time, the role of the means used to develop trainability grows\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). Novelty is given as the way past a plateau; no physiological mechanism is named. Inference: this is the same logic as the 4.1.2 generalization that \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the athlete's special physical fitness increases.\"","+**Why it works** — The authors' stated reason is that earlier functional changes are prerequisites for later ones, and that \"Specificity becomes more relevant to one's state of fitness as sports proficiency increases. At the same time, the role of t