{"parentUuid":"16b77a8c-94a5-4ef2-9abe-e16180f7ba32","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaF1rLnfkqNdVLiF62R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XzuojCdRWxPZBCB9bb8EvY","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md","old_string":"Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" and says the model is \"restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (vault summary of Issurin 2016, *Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11; see","new_string":"The library's summary of Issurin 2016 (*Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11) records that Issurin judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation (the reviewed studies improved some fitness measures but did not report team practice or competition outcomes), and presented the model as suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2755,"cache_read_input_tokens":279772,"output_tokens":699,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":243},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2755},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":699,"cache_read_input_tokens":279772,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2755,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XzuojCdRWxPZBCB9bb8EvY":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md","old_string":"Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" and says the model is \"restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (vault summary of Issurin 2016, *Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11; see","new_string":"The library's summary of Issurin 2016 (*Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11) records that Issurin judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation (the reviewed studies improved some fitness measures but did not report team practice or competition outcomes), and presented the model as suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaEhJszYZNtCT8ViSAi","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"149bc774-fde3-4ad0-9e61-568ba37479e8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:01.195Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"149bc774-fde3-4ad0-9e61-568ba37479e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XzuojCdRWxPZBCB9bb8EvY","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"5cc44497-738f-4e45-ba40-d485eea90dc3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:01.308Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md","oldString":"Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" and says the model is \"restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (vault summary of Issurin 2016, *Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11; see","newString":"The library's summary of Issurin 2016 (*Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11) records that Issurin judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation (the reviewed studies improved some fitness measures but did not report team practice or competition outcomes), and presented the model as suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: conjugate sequence system\ncategory: [periodization, block-periodization, concentrated-loading, sequencing, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Conjugate Sequence System: Successive Blocks of Unidirectional Loading, Each Built on the Delayed Effect of the Last\n\n**What it is** — The authors name \"two main systems of organising long term training: the concurrent system and the conjugate sequence system\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). The concurrent system is \"the parallel training of several motor abilities, such as strength, speed and endurance, over the same period.\" The conjugate sequence system (\"sometimes translated from the Russian as the coupled successive system\") \"involves successively introducing into the training programme separate, specific means, each of which has a progressively stronger training effect, and coupling them sequentially to create favourable conditions for eliciting the cumulative effect of all the training loads.\" It usually trains \"a carefully chosen sequence of specific motor abilities, each of which is confined largely to a given period,\" and \"It is appropriate to use concentrated loading primarily for enhancing the effectiveness of the SPP (Special Physical Preparation) phase\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292; Verkhoshansky 1978 cited). Two sample sequences (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293):\n- **Speed-endurance** (Fig 5.15, verified against the page image): 1. Aerobic; 2. Mixed anaerobic (ATP-CP and anaerobic glycolysis); 3. High energy phosphagens (ATP-CP); 4. Anaerobic glycolysis. \"The block of specialised strength work is concentrated in stage 2, speed work is concentrated mainly in stage 3 and is executed against a background of the long-term delayed training effect of the strength block, and stage 4 is devoted to improving speed-endurance while the general volume of loading is decreased significantly.\" The figure labels the four stages cardiovascular, strength, speed and speed-endurance, draws them as overlapping humps under a rising \"Training Potential\" line, and its caption says it is \"solely a descriptive scheme, which does not give quantitative details of intensity, volume or timing of loading\" and that \"there may be a certain amount of overlap of all training stages 1-4.\"\n- **Speed-strength and complex-technique sports:** \"1. General development work, 2. Concentrated strength loading, 3. In-depth perfecting of technique against a background of the delayed training effect of the strength block 4. Further perfecting of technique under competitive conditions.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: for elite athletes the concurrent system \"produces only average results,\" so \"To evoke a more powerful training effect in athletes who have already accommodated to high levels of stimulation, it becomes necessary to impose intense phases of uni-directional loading on the body\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). \"the 'degree of contrast' in training (i.e. the alternation of means with different beneficial training effects over certain optimal periods) is a factor which enhances the functional responsiveness of the body\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). Each stage is run on \"the long-term delayed training effect\" of the one before. No physiological mechanism for the delayed effect is given in this section; Chapter 6 treats it (see Related).\n\n**When to apply** — Advanced athletes \"who are at a high level of special strength fitness,\" for whom the authors recommend \"primarily a complex system of means for: producing the specific training effect of the complex of means; preserving the magnitude of the training effect,\" the latter \"realised by the sequential replacement of one complex combination of means with another\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming ... \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred). Inference: the speed-strength sequence (general work, concentrated strength, technique on the strength block's delayed effect) is the closest of the two to a football off-season, but the authors give it for \"speed-strength and complex-technique types of sport,\" not team sports.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Not for lower-level athletes on the authors' own account: research supporting the concurrent system was done on \"athletes of lower qualification,\" in whom \"the negative influence of the complex system is not apparent\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). **Contested by Issurin.** Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" and says the model is \"restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (vault summary of Issurin 2016, *Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11; see [[issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors that concentration is for high-level athletes ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], PDF 44 / printed 33), so the dispute is over which sports the unidirectional version suits, not over concentration as such. Note also a naming overlap in the text: \"complex system\" is used for both the concurrent system the authors reject for elites (PDF p. 303) and the \"complex system of means\" they recommend for qualified athletes (PDF p. 304). The second means a complex of means with one primary emphasis, replaced as a whole over time.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own speed-strength sequence: general development work, then concentrated strength loading, then in-depth technique work \"against a background of the delayed training effect of the strength block,\" then technique under competitive conditions (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin's multi-targeted alternative), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Advanced athletes \"who are at a high level of special strength fitness,\" for whom the authors recommend \"primarily a complex system of means for: producing the specific training effect of the complex of means; preserving the magnitude of the training effect,\" the latter \"realised by the sequential replacement of one complex combination of means with another\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming ... \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred). Inference: the speed-strength sequence (general work, concentrated strength, technique on the strength block's delayed effect) is the closest of the two to a football off-season, but the authors give it for \"speed-strength and complex-technique types of sport,\" not team sports."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Not for lower-level athletes on the authors' own account: research supporting the concurrent system was done on \"athletes of lower qualification,\" in whom \"the negative influence of the complex system is not apparent\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). **Contested by Issurin.** Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model \"insufficient for broad implementation in high-performance combat, team-sport, and swimming preparation\" and says the model is \"restricted when peak performance requires many interacting abilities\" (vault summary of Issurin 2016, *Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11; see [[issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors that concentration is for high-level athletes ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], PDF 44 / printed 33), so the dispute is over which sports the unidirectional version suits, not over concentration as such. Note also a naming overlap in the text: \"complex system\" is used for both the concurrent system the authors reject for elites (PDF p. 303) and the \"complex system of means\" they recommend for qualified athletes (PDF p. 304). The second means a complex of means with one primary emphasis, replaced as a whole over time.","+**When NOT to apply** — Not for lower-level athletes on the authors' own account: research supporting the concurrent system was done on \"athletes of lower qualification,\" in whom \"the negative influence of the complex system is not apparent\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). **Contested by Issurin.** The library's summary of Issurin 2016 (*Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11) records that Issurin judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation (the reviewed studies improved some fitness measures but did not report team practice or competition outcomes), and presented the model as suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see [[issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors that concentration is for high-level athletes ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], PDF 44 / printed 33), so the dispute is over which sports the unidirectional version suits, not over concentration as such. Note also a naming overlap in the text: \"complex system\" is used for both the concurrent system the authors reject for elites (PDF p. 303) and the \"complex system of means\" they recommend for qualified athletes (PDF p. 304). The second means a complex of means with one primary emphasis, replaced as a whole over time."," "," **Example** — The authors' own speed-strength sequence: general development work, then concentrated strength loading, then in-depth technique work \"against a background of the delayed training effect of the strength block,\" then technique under competitive conditions (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"149bc774-fde3-4ad0-9e61-568ba37479e8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
