{"parentUuid":"410ffbb5-de6a-4aeb-8b3f-570b26de45de","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_01XzuojCdRWxPZBCB9bb8EvY","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2ae54ea5-e70b-42b2-a842-3ea0306a3217","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:07.090Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2ae54ea5-e70b-42b2-a842-3ea0306a3217","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaFkWdspYXM1gqin38V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":822,"cache_read_input_tokens":282527,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":822,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":814,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaFVG7zvcLApMzAWsxC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7bff96fc-fea7-4a41-aa92-94ebc9ce7fef","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:07.089Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7bff96fc-fea7-4a41-aa92-94ebc9ce7fef","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaFkWdspYXM1gqin38V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016WKPqSLsRuF9uogTU6Awx9","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: concentrated loading conditions\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, volume, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# When Concentrated Unidirectional Loading Works: A Diverse Complex of Means, Low-Intensity Means, and 23-25% of the Year's Volume in One Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one uses a diverse complex of means with a single primary emphasis, together with a variety of training methods (Fedorova et al, 1975; Platonov & Monograov, 1977; Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n3. **Low intensity of the means.** \"an important condition for implementing concentrated loading is the relatively low intensity of the means, since the frequent use of these means already intensifies the training.\"\n4. **Definition.** \"the loading may be considered concentrated if its volume in the month in which it is applied constitutes 23-25% of the general volume for the year.\"\n5. **Payoff claimed.** \"the effectiveness of the conjugate sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' one stated reason is condition 3: concentration \"already intensifies the training,\" so intense means on top of it would overload. Chapter 6 repeats this in the same words (\"Concentration of unidirectional loading already intensifies the training process, and one should not intensify it further with high intensity means\"; PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). No reason is given for the 23-25% threshold beyond the definition itself, and no source is named for the 20% volume saving.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors warn on the same page that \"concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength\" (see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). The 23-25% figure is a definition, not a target derived from data. The \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" is defined here as a volume saving of up to 20%, but 5.4.2 defines it differently, as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296); keep both meanings in view. **Contested by Issurin** for team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]). Issurin's own block model also concentrates workloads but on a minimal number of targets per block, not a single one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 4-week concentrated strength block in which squats, pulls, presses and loaded jumps all serve the strength emphasis, each at moderate loads, with that month carrying close to a quarter of the year's strength volume. The authors give the rule, not a worked plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":822,"cache_read_input_tokens":282527,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":822,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016WKPqSLsRuF9uogTU6Awx9":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: concentrated loading conditions\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, volume, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# When Concentrated Unidirectional Loading Works: A Diverse Complex of Means, Low-Intensity Means, and 23-25% of the Year's Volume in One Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one uses a diverse complex of means with a single primary emphasis, together with a variety of training methods (Fedorova et al, 1975; Platonov & Monograov, 1977; Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n3. **Low intensity of the means.** \"an important condition for implementing concentrated loading is the relatively low intensity of the means, since the frequent use of these means already intensifies the training.\"\n4. **Definition.** \"the loading may be considered concentrated if its volume in the month in which it is applied constitutes 23-25% of the general volume for the year.\"\n5. **Payoff claimed.** \"the effectiveness of the conjugate sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' one stated reason is condition 3: concentration \"already intensifies the training,\" so intense means on top of it would overload. Chapter 6 repeats this in the same words (\"Concentration of unidirectional loading already intensifies the training process, and one should not intensify it further with high intensity means\"; PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). No reason is given for the 23-25% threshold beyond the definition itself, and no source is named for the 20% volume saving.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors warn on the same page that \"concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength\" (see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). The 23-25% figure is a definition, not a target derived from data. The \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" is defined here as a volume saving of up to 20%, but 5.4.2 defines it differently, as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296); keep both meanings in view. **Contested by Issurin** for team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]). Issurin's own block model also concentrates workloads but on a minimal number of targets per block, not a single one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 4-week concentrated strength block in which squats, pulls, presses and loaded jumps all serve the strength emphasis, each at moderate loads, with that month carrying close to a quarter of the year's strength volume. The authors give the rule, not a worked plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaFVG7zvcLApMzAWsxC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"0fa5587c-7404-4ef2-b37f-e2ebd2f6c037","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:21.625Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0fa5587c-7404-4ef2-b37f-e2ebd2f6c037","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016WKPqSLsRuF9uogTU6Awx9","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"73e6efbe-7364-4839-bbbe-9b3165e9f379","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:21.743Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: concentrated loading conditions\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, block-periodization, volume, programming, advanced]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# When Concentrated Unidirectional Loading Works: A Diverse Complex of Means, Low-Intensity Means, and 23-25% of the Year's Volume in One Month\n\n**What it is** — The authors set out the working conditions for the concentrated phases of the conjugate sequence system (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image):\n1. **Scale and duration.** \"Unidirectional emphasis on qualities such as strength or endurance may be used not only in individual workouts but also during each microcycle (Kudelin, 1980; Budokho et al, 1978; Mironenko, 1980). Research has shown that unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective, especially if the intensity is gradually increased for each form of loading in the given cycle.\"\n2. **Diversity within one emphasis.** \"unidirectional loading is effective only if one uses a diverse complex of means with a single primary emphasis, together with a variety of training methods (Fedorova et al, 1975; Platonov & Monograov, 1977; Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n3. **Low intensity of the means.** \"an important condition for implementing concentrated loading is the relatively low intensity of the means, since the frequent use of these means already intensifies the training.\"\n4. **Definition.** \"the loading may be considered concentrated if its volume in the month in which it is applied constitutes 23-25% of the general volume for the year.\"\n5. **Payoff claimed.** \"the effectiveness of the conjugate sequence system with advanced athletes is such that the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2).\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' one stated reason is condition 3: concentration \"already intensifies the training,\" so intense means on top of it would overload. Chapter 6 repeats this in the same words (\"Concentration of unidirectional loading already intensifies the training process, and one should not intensify it further with high intensity means\"; PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). No reason is given for the 23-25% threshold beyond the definition itself, and no source is named for the 20% volume saving.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a concentrated block for an advanced athlete: fill it with several different exercises that share one emphasis, keep each one moderate in intensity, and expect the month's volume to be about a quarter of the year's. Eric's baseline: \"Training residual effects set the retraining cadence: aerobic capacity and max strength both need re-exposure roughly every 30 days; speed needs re-exposure roughly every 5 days\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 184, Inferred). Inference: a 4-12 week unidirectional block runs past those windows for the qualities it does not train, so the baseline's retention dose would still be needed alongside it; the authors do not address retention of other qualities here.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors warn on the same page that \"concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength\" (see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). The 23-25% figure is a definition, not a target derived from data. The \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" is defined here as a volume saving of up to 20%, but 5.4.2 defines it differently, as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296); keep both meanings in view. **Contested by Issurin** for team and combat sports (see [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]). Issurin's own block model also concentrates workloads but on a minimal number of targets per block, not a single one ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]]).\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 4-week concentrated strength block in which squats, pulls, presses and loaded jumps all serve the strength emphasis, each at moderate loads, with that month carrying close to a quarter of the year's strength volume. The authors give the rule, not a worked plan.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/claim--residual-training-effect-durations-by-motor-ability]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0fa5587c-7404-4ef2-b37f-e2ebd2f6c037","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"73e6efbe-7364-4839-bbbe-9b3165e9f379","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaFkWdspYXM1gqin38V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y3AP42rwY55s8uYAwjC7VW","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: restoration after concentrated loading\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, delayed-training-effect, recovery, block-periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks, so it is essential for the athlete not to lose patience and hasten into the next concentrated phase without adequate restoration. Not only does premature imposition of concentrated loading suppress the delayed training effect of the previous stage, but it also lowers the effectiveness of future training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the conjugate sequence system is based on exploitation of the delayed training effect\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), so the next block must start once that effect has appeared, not before. No physiological mechanism is given in this section. Inference: the authors frame the restorative period as supercompensation; the two-factor (fitness-fatigue) reading in this library would describe the same pattern as fatigue clearing to reveal fitness, and the book does not choose between them here.\n\n**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":822,"cache_read_input_tokens":282527,"output_tokens":3774,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":139},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":822},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3774,"cache_read_input_tokens":282527,"cache_creation_input_tokens":822,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":822,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Y3AP42rwY55s8uYAwjC7VW":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: restoration after concentrated loading\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, delayed-training-effect, recovery, block-periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks, so it is essential for the athlete not to lose patience and hasten into the next concentrated phase without adequate restoration. Not only does premature imposition of concentrated loading suppress the delayed training effect of the previous stage, but it also lowers the effectiveness of future training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the conjugate sequence system is based on exploitation of the delayed training effect\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), so the next block must start once that effect has appeared, not before. No physiological mechanism is given in this section. Inference: the authors frame the restorative period as supercompensation; the two-factor (fitness-fatigue) reading in this library would describe the same pattern as fatigue clearing to reveal fitness, and the book does not choose between them here.\n\n**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaFVG7zvcLApMzAWsxC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a1b02bdd-fc29-4376-a65e-28c9dea35617","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:35.715Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a1b02bdd-fc29-4376-a65e-28c9dea35617","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y3AP42rwY55s8uYAwjC7VW","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d3f1a512-cd5b-4a01-9eaa-872bbadb4c6b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:35.821Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: restoration after concentrated loading\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, delayed-training-effect, recovery, block-periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks, so it is essential for the athlete not to lose patience and hasten into the next concentrated phase without adequate restoration. Not only does premature imposition of concentrated loading suppress the delayed training effect of the previous stage, but it also lowers the effectiveness of future training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the conjugate sequence system is based on exploitation of the delayed training effect\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), so the next block must start once that effect has appeared, not before. No physiological mechanism is given in this section. Inference: the authors frame the restorative period as supercompensation; the two-factor (fitness-fatigue) reading in this library would describe the same pattern as fatigue clearing to reveal fitness, and the book does not choose between them here.\n\n**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a1b02bdd-fc29-4376-a65e-28c9dea35617","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
