{"parentUuid":"0594f3a0-12f5-4510-a502-35e676de5c86","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaJGWjhhz5zzJF1zLQx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4046,"cache_read_input_tokens":283349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4046,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1653,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaJ2XMg8o6eTbDGzCAW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"eec98681-336c-4faf-afea-d204c26267c5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:42.072Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"eec98681-336c-4faf-afea-d204c26267c5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaJGWjhhz5zzJF1zLQx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now section 5.4."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4046,"cache_read_input_tokens":283349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4046,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaJ2XMg8o6eTbDGzCAW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6ace09d1-3ae2-4083-9cb0-a77a76c9b76a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:42.073Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"6ace09d1-3ae2-4083-9cb0-a77a76c9b76a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaJGWjhhz5zzJF1zLQx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EuC3AvSw69752CECWMrHrv","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4\"\ntopic: principles of special strength training\ncategory: [programming, long-term-development, special-strength, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Four Organising Principles of Special Strength Training: Converge, Accelerate, Correspond, Maintain\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is determined entirely by the specific components of the training stimulus\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). On that basis the authors give \"a set of general principles for organising special strength training\" which \"form the foundation of a new system of sport training, although they do not exclude all of the traditional principles and rules\":\n1. **Converging the partial effects of strength training means** (5.4.1): the cumulative effect of all means is steered toward the specific characteristics the sport needs.\n2. **Accelerating specific adaptation** (5.4.2): bring forward the stage at which peak proficiency is reached by modelling the sport and sequencing its demands.\n3. **Specific correspondence of the training effect** (5.4.3): match the general effect of strength work to the sport's motor regime, stage by stage.\n4. **Maintaining the strength training effect** (5.4.4): keep each load productive by timely replacement of means with more effective ones.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the premise above: once adaptation becomes selective, \"the regime of the means used should contain an optimal combination of specific training means\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). The principles are stated as ways of \"exploiting these characteristics\"; no further mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — As a checklist for a long-term strength plan for an advanced athlete: does the plan converge on the sport's needs, does it bring the peak forward rather than leave it to chance, does each stage's effect match the sport's regime, and is each block's means replaced before it stops working? Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit, interview Q1). Inference: principle 3 is the programme-level counterpart of the exercise-level dynamic-correspondence criteria Eric already teaches.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The premise is the authors' summary; the selective-adaptation idea is already in this library with its sources ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], 2.3.1). For novices, the authors' own premise says general adaptation dominates, so these principles, built for \"the high levels of sports proficiency,\" apply less. The principles are stated as a framework, not tested as a set. The term \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" carries two definitions in the chapter (see [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own illustrations are in the four section entries: Fig 5.16 (convergence), the children's sport-school stages (early specialisation by motor regime), Fig 5.17 (functional specialisation) and Figs 5.18-5.19 (replacing means). See Related.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4046,"cache_read_input_tokens":283349,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4046,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01EuC3AvSw69752CECWMrHrv":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4\"\ntopic: principles of special strength training\ncategory: [programming, long-term-development, special-strength, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Four Organising Principles of Special Strength Training: Converge, Accelerate, Correspond, Maintain\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is determined entirely by the specific components of the training stimulus\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). On that basis the authors give \"a set of general principles for organising special strength training\" which \"form the foundation of a new system of sport training, although they do not exclude all of the traditional principles and rules\":\n1. **Converging the partial effects of strength training means** (5.4.1): the cumulative effect of all means is steered toward the specific characteristics the sport needs.\n2. **Accelerating specific adaptation** (5.4.2): bring forward the stage at which peak proficiency is reached by modelling the sport and sequencing its demands.\n3. **Specific correspondence of the training effect** (5.4.3): match the general effect of strength work to the sport's motor regime, stage by stage.\n4. **Maintaining the strength training effect** (5.4.4): keep each load productive by timely replacement of means with more effective ones.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the premise above: once adaptation becomes selective, \"the regime of the means used should contain an optimal combination of specific training means\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). The principles are stated as ways of \"exploiting these characteristics\"; no further mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — As a checklist for a long-term strength plan for an advanced athlete: does the plan converge on the sport's needs, does it bring the peak forward rather than leave it to chance, does each stage's effect match the sport's regime, and is each block's means replaced before it stops working? Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit, interview Q1). Inference: principle 3 is the programme-level counterpart of the exercise-level dynamic-correspondence criteria Eric already teaches.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The premise is the authors' summary; the selective-adaptation idea is already in this library with its sources ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], 2.3.1). For novices, the authors' own premise says general adaptation dominates, so these principles, built for \"the high levels of sports proficiency,\" apply less. The principles are stated as a framework, not tested as a set. The term \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" carries two definitions in the chapter (see [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own illustrations are in the four section entries: Fig 5.16 (convergence), the children's sport-school stages (early specialisation by motor regime), Fig 5.17 (functional specialisation) and Figs 5.18-5.19 (replacing means). See Related.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaJ2XMg8o6eTbDGzCAW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1f8fb18a-0f5a-4ad7-b84b-73413e8765bd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:55.264Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1f8fb18a-0f5a-4ad7-b84b-73413e8765bd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EuC3AvSw69752CECWMrHrv","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"dc5d24a5-9dab-4374-a02f-f3d27ba1c6f1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:54:55.376Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4\"\ntopic: principles of special strength training\ncategory: [programming, long-term-development, special-strength, periodization]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Four Organising Principles of Special Strength Training: Converge, Accelerate, Correspond, Maintain\n\n**What it is** — Section 5.4 opens with the premise that adaptation changes character with level. \"During the initial stages of training the body reacts to any stimulus with all of its survival mechanisms and motor abilities,\" there is \"a relatively high correlation between the various motor abilities,\" and \"The transfer of fitness is very distinct here.\" \"As sporting proficiency grows, so the adaptation response becomes more selective,\" the link between abilities weakens, and \"the direction of progress is determined entirely by the specific components of the training stimulus\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). On that basis the authors give \"a set of general principles for organising special strength training\" which \"form the foundation of a new system of sport training, although they do not exclude all of the traditional principles and rules\":\n1. **Converging the partial effects of strength training means** (5.4.1): the cumulative effect of all means is steered toward the specific characteristics the sport needs.\n2. **Accelerating specific adaptation** (5.4.2): bring forward the stage at which peak proficiency is reached by modelling the sport and sequencing its demands.\n3. **Specific correspondence of the training effect** (5.4.3): match the general effect of strength work to the sport's motor regime, stage by stage.\n4. **Maintaining the strength training effect** (5.4.4): keep each load productive by timely replacement of means with more effective ones.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the premise above: once adaptation becomes selective, \"the regime of the means used should contain an optimal combination of specific training means\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). The principles are stated as ways of \"exploiting these characteristics\"; no further mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — As a checklist for a long-term strength plan for an advanced athlete: does the plan converge on the sport's needs, does it bring the peak forward rather than leave it to chance, does each stage's effect match the sport's regime, and is each block's means replaced before it stops working? Eric's baseline: \"The Principle of Dynamic Correspondence (Verkhoshansky), matching amplitude/direction of movement, region of force production, dynamics of effort, rate/timing of force production, and regime of muscular work to the sport skill, governs whether an exercise actually transfers\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 103, Explicit, interview Q1). Inference: principle 3 is the programme-level counterpart of the exercise-level dynamic-correspondence criteria Eric already teaches.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The premise is the authors' summary; the selective-adaptation idea is already in this library with its sources ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], 2.3.1). For novices, the authors' own premise says general adaptation dominates, so these principles, built for \"the high levels of sports proficiency,\" apply less. The principles are stated as a framework, not tested as a set. The term \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" carries two definitions in the chapter (see [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own illustrations are in the four section entries: Fig 5.16 (convergence), the children's sport-school stages (early specialisation by motor regime), Fig 5.17 (functional specialisation) and Figs 5.18-5.19 (replacing means). See Related.\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1f8fb18a-0f5a-4ad7-b84b-73413e8765bd","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"dc5d24a5-9dab-4374-a02f-f3d27ba1c6f1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaJGWjhhz5zzJF1zLQx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PUv7uCEgmjRrUqezi74F21","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: convergence of training effects\ncategory: [long-term-development, specificity, special-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Converging the Partial Effects: Over Years, Every Means Should Steer Toward the Sport's Key Motor Ability\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" linked \"only by the long-term plan.\" Specialisation then \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern (see 2.2.3).\" \"As the STP advances, the training effects of the means begin to converge according to their specificity,\" until \"at the high level of sports mastery the special strength training converges towards the specific character of the motor regime of the given exercise and its specific motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Fig 5.16 (verified against the page image) plots special fitness against STP time: the key motor ability M and its contributing abilities M1, M2 and M3 start at different, lower levels and curve upward to meet the \"Sport A\" line at the right of the chart.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' criterion: \"the criterion of successful convergence is the closeness of correspondence of the training effect to the performance characteristics of the given sport.\" Their account of the late stage: \"the effect of the specific training means exceeds the required degree of closeness, while the general training means more strongly reflect the specifics of the special exercise,\" and \"there is a close interrelation between all components which constitute the STP\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no physiological mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-term planning: early years can use widely varied means, but each year the means, general ones included, should look more like the sport's motor regime. The authors' role split is useful for planning reviews: \"the functional characteristics demanded by the sport determine the requirements of the special strength training, while the level of special fitness determines the progress of the STP as a whole\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102, Inferred). Inference: the convergence principle says where an athlete should sit on that spectrum over a career: further toward the specific end each year, with even \"general\" work chosen to resemble the sport.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Fig 5.16 is a schematic with no units or data. The principle describes direction over years, not a rule for a single season. The authors say the convergence of general means toward the special exercise happens \"As training progresses,\" so applying it to a beginner would remove the early diversity the same section endorses. Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.16, in which the abilities M1, M2 and M3 that determine the key ability M rise at different rates and converge with M on the profile of sport A by the end of the STP (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport]] (the early-stage half of this principle), [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4046,"cache_read_input_tokens":283349,"output_tokens":3385,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":246},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4046},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3385,"cache_read_input_tokens":283349,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4046,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4046,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01PUv7uCEgmjRrUqezi74F21":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: convergence of training effects\ncategory: [long-term-development, specificity, special-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Converging the Partial Effects: Over Years, Every Means Should Steer Toward the Sport's Key Motor Ability\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" linked \"only by the long-term plan.\" Specialisation then \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern (see 2.2.3).\" \"As the STP advances, the training effects of the means begin to converge according to their specificity,\" until \"at the high level of sports mastery the special strength training converges towards the specific character of the motor regime of the given exercise and its specific motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Fig 5.16 (verified against the page image) plots special fitness against STP time: the key motor ability M and its contributing abilities M1, M2 and M3 start at different, lower levels and curve upward to meet the \"Sport A\" line at the right of the chart.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' criterion: \"the criterion of successful convergence is the closeness of correspondence of the training effect to the performance characteristics of the given sport.\" Their account of the late stage: \"the effect of the specific training means exceeds the required degree of closeness, while the general training means more strongly reflect the specifics of the special exercise,\" and \"there is a close interrelation between all components which constitute the STP\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no physiological mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-term planning: early years can use widely varied means, but each year the means, general ones included, should look more like the sport's motor regime. The authors' role split is useful for planning reviews: \"the functional characteristics demanded by the sport determine the requirements of the special strength training, while the level of special fitness determines the progress of the STP as a whole\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102, Inferred). Inference: the convergence principle says where an athlete should sit on that spectrum over a career: further toward the specific end each year, with even \"general\" work chosen to resemble the sport.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Fig 5.16 is a schematic with no units or data. The principle describes direction over years, not a rule for a single season. The authors say the convergence of general means toward the special exercise happens \"As training progresses,\" so applying it to a beginner would remove the early diversity the same section endorses. Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.16, in which the abilities M1, M2 and M3 that determine the key ability M rise at different rates and converge with M on the profile of sport A by the end of the STP (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport]] (the early-stage half of this principle), [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffaJ2XMg8o6eTbDGzCAW","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1c176945-e72e-4666-a1d6-6fae0587de68","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:08.074Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1c176945-e72e-4666-a1d6-6fae0587de68","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PUv7uCEgmjRrUqezi74F21","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d63bb6f5-1c33-4ad4-af31-9c82288f9048","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:08.189Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: convergence of training effects\ncategory: [long-term-development, specificity, special-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Converging the Partial Effects: Over Years, Every Means Should Steer Toward the Sport's Key Motor Ability\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" linked \"only by the long-term plan.\" Specialisation then \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern (see 2.2.3).\" \"As the STP advances, the training effects of the means begin to converge according to their specificity,\" until \"at the high level of sports mastery the special strength training converges towards the specific character of the motor regime of the given exercise and its specific motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Fig 5.16 (verified against the page image) plots special fitness against STP time: the key motor ability M and its contributing abilities M1, M2 and M3 start at different, lower levels and curve upward to meet the \"Sport A\" line at the right of the chart.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' criterion: \"the criterion of successful convergence is the closeness of correspondence of the training effect to the performance characteristics of the given sport.\" Their account of the late stage: \"the effect of the specific training means exceeds the required degree of closeness, while the general training means more strongly reflect the specifics of the special exercise,\" and \"there is a close interrelation between all components which constitute the STP\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no physiological mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-term planning: early years can use widely varied means, but each year the means, general ones included, should look more like the sport's motor regime. The authors' role split is useful for planning reviews: \"the functional characteristics demanded by the sport determine the requirements of the special strength training, while the level of special fitness determines the progress of the STP as a whole\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102, Inferred). Inference: the convergence principle says where an athlete should sit on that spectrum over a career: further toward the specific end each year, with even \"general\" work chosen to resemble the sport.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Fig 5.16 is a schematic with no units or data. The principle describes direction over years, not a rule for a single season. The authors say the convergence of general means toward the special exercise happens \"As training progresses,\" so applying it to a beginner would remove the early diversity the same section endorses. Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.16, in which the abilities M1, M2 and M3 that determine the key ability M rise at different rates and converge with M on the profile of sport A by the end of the STP (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport]] (the early-stage half of this principle), [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1c176945-e72e-4666-a1d6-6fae0587de68","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
