{"parentUuid":"e6fe752e-01fb-45d9-9c09-8293ee8e56a3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaMekJ6mhRhiAnsbqBz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AhhuX41ZGrxBgG1gULARLs","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md","old_string":"Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.","new_string":"Inference: Bondarchuk's correlation tables in this library find that the barbell snatch and half squat transfer to sprinting only within narrow performance bands in most events ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-strength-exercise-transfer-ceiling]]), which suggests a general means may stop helping at some levels rather than simply converging; the authors here do not discuss that case."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":499,"cache_read_input_tokens":292634,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":499,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AhhuX41ZGrxBgG1gULARLs":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md","old_string":"Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.","new_string":"Inference: Bondarchuk's correlation tables in this library find that the barbell snatch and half squat transfer to sprinting only within narrow performance bands in most events ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-strength-exercise-transfer-ceiling]]), which suggests a general means may stop helping at some levels rather than simply converging; the authors here do not discuss that case."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaMMkbS1s7wYY8P9vfV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5efa0b7d-e468-4922-9d1f-958cd86f3310","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:29.823Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5efa0b7d-e468-4922-9d1f-958cd86f3310","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AhhuX41ZGrxBgG1gULARLs","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"e40da7d6-3e92-4315-a7ed-fe8067e38176","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:29.932Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means.md","oldString":"Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.","newString":"Inference: Bondarchuk's correlation tables in this library find that the barbell snatch and half squat transfer to sprinting only within narrow performance bands in most events ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-strength-exercise-transfer-ceiling]]), which suggests a general means may stop helping at some levels rather than simply converging; the authors here do not discuss that case.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: convergence of training effects\ncategory: [long-term-development, specificity, special-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Converging the Partial Effects: Over Years, Every Means Should Steer Toward the Sport's Key Motor Ability\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of converging the partial effects of strength training means is fundamental to the long-term organisation of strength training. It is achieved by ... the gradual convergence of their cumulative training effect to those important specific characteristics which are essential to the functioning of the body in a particular sport\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Early in the sports training process (STP), \"the tasks which determine the diversity of the training means are far apart,\" linked \"only by the long-term plan.\" Specialisation then \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern (see 2.2.3).\" \"As the STP advances, the training effects of the means begin to converge according to their specificity,\" until \"at the high level of sports mastery the special strength training converges towards the specific character of the motor regime of the given exercise and its specific motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Fig 5.16 (verified against the page image) plots special fitness against STP time: the key motor ability M and its contributing abilities M1, M2 and M3 start at different, lower levels and curve upward to meet the \"Sport A\" line at the right of the chart.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' criterion: \"the criterion of successful convergence is the closeness of correspondence of the training effect to the performance characteristics of the given sport.\" Their account of the late stage: \"the effect of the specific training means exceeds the required degree of closeness, while the general training means more strongly reflect the specifics of the special exercise,\" and \"there is a close interrelation between all components which constitute the STP\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no physiological mechanism.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-term planning: early years can use widely varied means, but each year the means, general ones included, should look more like the sport's motor regime. The authors' role split is useful for planning reviews: \"the functional characteristics demanded by the sport determine the requirements of the special strength training, while the level of special fitness determines the progress of the STP as a whole\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102, Inferred). Inference: the convergence principle says where an athlete should sit on that spectrum over a career: further toward the specific end each year, with even \"general\" work chosen to resemble the sport.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Fig 5.16 is a schematic with no units or data. The principle describes direction over years, not a rule for a single season. The authors say the convergence of general means toward the special exercise happens \"As training progresses,\" so applying it to a beginner would remove the early diversity the same section endorses. Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.16, in which the abilities M1, M2 and M3 that determine the key ability M rise at different rates and converge with M on the profile of sport A by the end of the STP (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport]] (the early-stage half of this principle), [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--key-specific-nonspecific-levelling-motor-ability-model]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/framework--three-viewpoints-on-training-transfer]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Long-term planning: early years can use widely varied means, but each year the means, general ones included, should look more like the sport's motor regime. The authors' role split is useful for planning reviews: \"the functional characteristics demanded by the sport determine the requirements of the special strength training, while the level of special fitness determines the progress of the STP as a whole\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). Eric's baseline: \"Exercise selection is organized on a spectrum from general preparation through specific preparation, specific development, to competitive exercises (Bondarchuk)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 102, Inferred). Inference: the convergence principle says where an athlete should sit on that spectrum over a career: further toward the specific end each year, with even \"general\" work chosen to resemble the sport."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Fig 5.16 is a schematic with no units or data. The principle describes direction over years, not a rule for a single season. The authors say the convergence of general means toward the special exercise happens \"As training progresses,\" so applying it to a beginner would remove the early diversity the same section endorses. Inference: Bondarchuk, also in this library, holds that general exercises can lose transfer as specific fitness rises, which agrees on direction but adds that some means stop helping at all.","+**When NOT to apply** — Fig 5.16 is a schematic with no units or data. The principle describes direction over years, not a rule for a single season. The authors say the convergence of general means toward the special exercise happens \"As training progresses,\" so applying it to a beginner would remove the early diversity the same section endorses. Inference: Bondarchuk's correlation tables in this library find that the barbell snatch and half squat transfer to sprinting only within narrow performance bands in most events ([[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--sprint-strength-exercise-transfer-ceiling]]), which suggests a general means may stop helping at some levels rather than simply converging; the authors here do not discuss that case."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.16, in which the abilities M1, M2 and M3 that determine the key ability M rise at different rates and converge with M on the profile of sport A by the end of the STP (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5efa0b7d-e468-4922-9d1f-958cd86f3310","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e40da7d6-3e92-4315-a7ed-fe8067e38176","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaMekJ6mhRhiAnsbqBz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012uMS6js1proQ9pwWgTc4MV","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: early specialisation by motor regime\ncategory: [youth, long-term-development, talent, specialisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specialise Children Early by Motor Regime, Not by Sport: A Two-Stage Sport-School Model\n\n**What it is** — The authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic management of the functional perfecting of the body\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295, checked against the page image). The model has two stages:\n1. A child who shows \"a predisposition to a particular form of athletic activity (e.g. quick reactions, speed of movement, a capacity for speed-strength activities or motor dexterity)\" trains \"in a group specialising in the appropriate motor regime (the first stage of a children's sport school in Russia),\" using \"a diversity of elementary motor forms which do not involve specialisation in some specific sport.\" \"Competitions, primarily in team sports, are indispensable elements of such training because they involve those same elementary motor forms.\"\n2. As demands rise, \"the teacher will determine the child's predisposition to a specific sport and transfer him to the appropriate sport school for specialised training (the second stage)\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\nThe authors add that schools without this system can adopt it \"if these two stages of early sporting preparation are applied systematically in a single school or club\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: specialising in a sport too early \"can easily lead to forced training,\" and early training should raise \"the body's functional abilities\" through broad \"elementary motor forms.\" In the convergence model, early specialisation \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no data for the model on these pages.\n\n**When to apply** — Youth programmes and first-year college athletes with a narrow sport background: group by motor regime (speed, speed-strength, dexterity) and use varied elementary movements and team games before sport-specific loading. Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the authors' model is the upstream fix for the problem the baseline describes; at college age it can only be applied as remediation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The basis is the authors' reference to Verkhoshansky's own 1963 and 1972 work and the Russian sport-school structure; no outcome data, ages or durations are given, hence `untested`. The model assumes a teacher can judge \"predisposition\" reliably, which the text does not test. Inference: Bompa's long-term sequential model in this library ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]) also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence. It does not address athletes who already specialised.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a child showing quick reactions or a capacity for speed-strength activities joins a speed-strength motor-regime group at the first stage of a Russian children's sport school, then is transferred to a specific sport school at the second stage (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--long-term-preparation-four-stage-model-and-directions]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--sensitive-periods-by-motor-ability-and-sex]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":499,"cache_read_input_tokens":292634,"output_tokens":2126,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":152},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":499},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2126,"cache_read_input_tokens":292634,"cache_creation_input_tokens":499,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":499,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_012uMS6js1proQ9pwWgTc4MV":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: early specialisation by motor regime\ncategory: [youth, long-term-development, talent, specialisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specialise Children Early by Motor Regime, Not by Sport: A Two-Stage Sport-School Model\n\n**What it is** — The authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic management of the functional perfecting of the body\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295, checked against the page image). The model has two stages:\n1. A child who shows \"a predisposition to a particular form of athletic activity (e.g. quick reactions, speed of movement, a capacity for speed-strength activities or motor dexterity)\" trains \"in a group specialising in the appropriate motor regime (the first stage of a children's sport school in Russia),\" using \"a diversity of elementary motor forms which do not involve specialisation in some specific sport.\" \"Competitions, primarily in team sports, are indispensable elements of such training because they involve those same elementary motor forms.\"\n2. As demands rise, \"the teacher will determine the child's predisposition to a specific sport and transfer him to the appropriate sport school for specialised training (the second stage)\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\nThe authors add that schools without this system can adopt it \"if these two stages of early sporting preparation are applied systematically in a single school or club\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: specialising in a sport too early \"can easily lead to forced training,\" and early training should raise \"the body's functional abilities\" through broad \"elementary motor forms.\" In the convergence model, early specialisation \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no data for the model on these pages.\n\n**When to apply** — Youth programmes and first-year college athletes with a narrow sport background: group by motor regime (speed, speed-strength, dexterity) and use varied elementary movements and team games before sport-specific loading. Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the authors' model is the upstream fix for the problem the baseline describes; at college age it can only be applied as remediation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The basis is the authors' reference to Verkhoshansky's own 1963 and 1972 work and the Russian sport-school structure; no outcome data, ages or durations are given, hence `untested`. The model assumes a teacher can judge \"predisposition\" reliably, which the text does not test. Inference: Bompa's long-term sequential model in this library ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]) also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence. It does not address athletes who already specialised.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a child showing quick reactions or a capacity for speed-strength activities joins a speed-strength motor-regime group at the first stage of a Russian children's sport school, then is transferred to a specific sport school at the second stage (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--long-term-preparation-four-stage-model-and-directions]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--sensitive-periods-by-motor-ability-and-sex]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaMMkbS1s7wYY8P9vfV","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c462cc97-0a9d-4aa1-87f1-087aaf41481c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:42.859Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c462cc97-0a9d-4aa1-87f1-087aaf41481c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012uMS6js1proQ9pwWgTc4MV","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"23e6ab28-6193-48d8-8334-0cd1f8eb9f98","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:42.975Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: early specialisation by motor regime\ncategory: [youth, long-term-development, talent, specialisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specialise Children Early by Motor Regime, Not by Sport: A Two-Stage Sport-School Model\n\n**What it is** — The authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic management of the functional perfecting of the body\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295, checked against the page image). The model has two stages:\n1. A child who shows \"a predisposition to a particular form of athletic activity (e.g. quick reactions, speed of movement, a capacity for speed-strength activities or motor dexterity)\" trains \"in a group specialising in the appropriate motor regime (the first stage of a children's sport school in Russia),\" using \"a diversity of elementary motor forms which do not involve specialisation in some specific sport.\" \"Competitions, primarily in team sports, are indispensable elements of such training because they involve those same elementary motor forms.\"\n2. As demands rise, \"the teacher will determine the child's predisposition to a specific sport and transfer him to the appropriate sport school for specialised training (the second stage)\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\nThe authors add that schools without this system can adopt it \"if these two stages of early sporting preparation are applied systematically in a single school or club\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: specialising in a sport too early \"can easily lead to forced training,\" and early training should raise \"the body's functional abilities\" through broad \"elementary motor forms.\" In the convergence model, early specialisation \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no data for the model on these pages.\n\n**When to apply** — Youth programmes and first-year college athletes with a narrow sport background: group by motor regime (speed, speed-strength, dexterity) and use varied elementary movements and team games before sport-specific loading. Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the authors' model is the upstream fix for the problem the baseline describes; at college age it can only be applied as remediation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The basis is the authors' reference to Verkhoshansky's own 1963 and 1972 work and the Russian sport-school structure; no outcome data, ages or durations are given, hence `untested`. The model assumes a teacher can judge \"predisposition\" reliably, which the text does not test. Inference: Bompa's long-term sequential model in this library ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]) also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence. It does not address athletes who already specialised.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a child showing quick reactions or a capacity for speed-strength activities joins a speed-strength motor-regime group at the first stage of a Russian children's sport school, then is transferred to a specific sport school at the second stage (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--long-term-preparation-four-stage-model-and-directions]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--sensitive-periods-by-motor-ability-and-sex]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c462cc97-0a9d-4aa1-87f1-087aaf41481c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
