{"parentUuid":"9232c601-ff31-4373-a1d0-7d26a2bf1fab","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaPPwMUBP7zFHea4qns","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WVQVdnn93ZSprQRbs4uV27","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model.md | cut -c1-900; sed -n 1,30p /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/../library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table.md | grep -m2 \"^# \\|chapter\"","description":"Verify Bompa LTAD entry content"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2398,"cache_read_input_tokens":293133,"output_tokens":293,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":30},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2398},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":293,"cache_read_input_tokens":293133,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2398,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2398,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WVQVdnn93ZSprQRbs4uV27":{"command":"sed -n '/What it is/,/Why it works/p' /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model.md | cut -c1-900; sed -n 1,30p /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/../library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table.md | grep -m2 \"^# \\|chapter\"","description":"Verify Bompa LTAD entry content"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaNynZGerhi2Gbq3rtr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"86392c79-97ec-4e62-86dd-70a7b779944e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:51.844Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"86392c79-97ec-4e62-86dd-70a7b779944e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WVQVdnn93ZSprQRbs4uV27","type":"tool_result","content":"**What it is** — A pyramid model for an athlete's career: a broad base of **multilateral development** (childhood) → increasing **specialized training** (junior years) → **high performance** (maturity). The proportion of sport-specific work rises as the athlete matures — very small during the multilateral phase, then growing; for advanced athletes, ~60–80% of training time is sport-specific, the rest developing biomotor abilities (the exact ratio varies by sport: ~90% specific in long-distance running, ~40% in high jump). Bompa gives per-sport guideline ages for *starting training*, *starting specialization*, and *reaching peak performance* (e.g. specialization generally not before ~14–15 in most sports; Russian longitudinal data suggested not before age 15).\n\n**Why it works** — Three longitudinal studies (East German 14-year, Russian survey, and a tennis study) found athletes with a strong multilateral base reached higher ultimate performance, with fewer overuse injuries and less burnout, than early specializers — who progressed faster initially but plateaued, lost confidence, and faced more pressure. The base builds the physiological and psychological capacity that specialization later draws on.\nchapter: \"1.3\"\n# Soviet Sport-School Guideline Ages by Sport (Start, Specialize, Peak)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"bd7c5e94-0263-4465-9d78-754313fa3b86","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:55:52.142Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"**What it is** — A pyramid model for an athlete's career: a broad base of **multilateral development** (childhood) → increasing **specialized training** (junior years) → **high performance** (maturity). The proportion of sport-specific work rises as the athlete matures — very small during the multilateral phase, then growing; for advanced athletes, ~60–80% of training time is sport-specific, the rest developing biomotor abilities (the exact ratio varies by sport: ~90% specific in long-distance running, ~40% in high jump). Bompa gives per-sport guideline ages for *starting training*, *starting specialization*, and *reaching peak performance* (e.g. specialization generally not before ~14–15 in most sports; Russian longitudinal data suggested not before age 15).\n\n**Why it works** — Three longitudinal studies (East German 14-year, Russian survey, and a tennis study) found athletes with a strong multilateral base reached higher ultimate performance, with fewer overuse injuries and less burnout, than early specializers — who progressed faster initially but plateaued, lost confidence, and faced more pressure. The base builds the physiological and psychological capacity that specialization later draws on.\nchapter: \"1.3\"\n# Soviet Sport-School Guideline Ages by Sport (Start, Specialize, Peak)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"86392c79-97ec-4e62-86dd-70a7b779944e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
