{"parentUuid":"697535d3-430f-496f-99bc-e84fdd1cfc3e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaPwqFpGWjeXP8xWKD8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QMDMcDcMGMujV3sutirQgZ","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md","old_string":"also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence.","new_string":"also delays sport specialisation (generally not before about 14-15 in most sports) and cites three longitudinal studies favouring a multilateral base; the two agree on direction. Bompa's model is multilateral rather than grouped by motor regime, so the authors' first-stage grouping by predisposition is their own addition."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":754,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QMDMcDcMGMujV3sutirQgZ":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md","old_string":"also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence.","new_string":"also delays sport specialisation (generally not before about 14-15 in most sports) and cites three longitudinal studies favouring a multilateral base; the two agree on direction. Bompa's model is multilateral rather than grouped by motor regime, so the authors' first-stage grouping by predisposition is their own addition."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaPfG6sZK8vz5QayKzN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"72b09007-a72a-4116-9ebb-4815c448d80b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:00.262Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"72b09007-a72a-4116-9ebb-4815c448d80b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QMDMcDcMGMujV3sutirQgZ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"0600b20d-8e67-4516-8019-af1d67bb4e81","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:00.363Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport.md","oldString":"also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence.","newString":"also delays sport specialisation (generally not before about 14-15 in most sports) and cites three longitudinal studies favouring a multilateral base; the two agree on direction. Bompa's model is multilateral rather than grouped by motor regime, so the authors' first-stage grouping by predisposition is their own addition.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.1\"\ntopic: early specialisation by motor regime\ncategory: [youth, long-term-development, talent, specialisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specialise Children Early by Motor Regime, Not by Sport: A Two-Stage Sport-School Model\n\n**What it is** — The authors apply the convergence principle to youth: \"particular consideration was given to the advisability of early specialisation, not in the specific type of sport, but in the specific motor regime involved\" (Verkhoshansky 1963, 1972 cited). \"orientation of the child to the sport and physical training with the sports means does not begin with specialised exercise to achieve competitive performance standards (which can easily lead to forced training). Instead, it begins with systematic management of the functional perfecting of the body\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295, checked against the page image). The model has two stages:\n1. A child who shows \"a predisposition to a particular form of athletic activity (e.g. quick reactions, speed of movement, a capacity for speed-strength activities or motor dexterity)\" trains \"in a group specialising in the appropriate motor regime (the first stage of a children's sport school in Russia),\" using \"a diversity of elementary motor forms which do not involve specialisation in some specific sport.\" \"Competitions, primarily in team sports, are indispensable elements of such training because they involve those same elementary motor forms.\"\n2. As demands rise, \"the teacher will determine the child's predisposition to a specific sport and transfer him to the appropriate sport school for specialised training (the second stage)\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\nThe authors add that schools without this system can adopt it \"if these two stages of early sporting preparation are applied systematically in a single school or club\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons: specialising in a sport too early \"can easily lead to forced training,\" and early training should raise \"the body's functional abilities\" through broad \"elementary motor forms.\" In the convergence model, early specialisation \"is firstly according to ability (but not to the physiological system) and is not related to the motor pattern\" (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295). They give no data for the model on these pages.\n\n**When to apply** — Youth programmes and first-year college athletes with a narrow sport background: group by motor regime (speed, speed-strength, dexterity) and use varied elementary movements and team games before sport-specific loading. Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the authors' model is the upstream fix for the problem the baseline describes; at college age it can only be applied as remediation.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The basis is the authors' reference to Verkhoshansky's own 1963 and 1972 work and the Russian sport-school structure; no outcome data, ages or durations are given, hence `untested`. The model assumes a teacher can judge \"predisposition\" reliably, which the text does not test. Inference: Bompa's long-term sequential model in this library ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]) also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence. It does not address athletes who already specialised.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a child showing quick reactions or a capacity for speed-strength activities joins a speed-strength motor-regime group at the first stage of a Russian children's sport school, then is transferred to a specific sport school at the second stage (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--long-term-preparation-four-stage-model-and-directions]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--sensitive-periods-by-motor-ability-and-sex]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Youth programmes and first-year college athletes with a narrow sport background: group by motor regime (speed, speed-strength, dexterity) and use varied elementary movements and team games before sport-specific loading. Eric's baseline: \"Basic movement skills and basic lifts are taught before anything advanced, on the observed premise that many incoming athletes have high sport-specific skill from early specialization but poor general movement competency.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 173, Inferred, transcript). Inference: the authors' model is the upstream fix for the problem the baseline describes; at college age it can only be applied as remediation."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The basis is the authors' reference to Verkhoshansky's own 1963 and 1972 work and the Russian sport-school structure; no outcome data, ages or durations are given, hence `untested`. The model assumes a teacher can judge \"predisposition\" reliably, which the text does not test. Inference: Bompa's long-term sequential model in this library ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]) also delays sport specialisation; the two agree on direction, but neither entry supplies controlled evidence. It does not address athletes who already specialised.","+**When NOT to apply** — The basis is the authors' reference to Verkhoshansky's own 1963 and 1972 work and the Russian sport-school structure; no outcome data, ages or durations are given, hence `untested`. The model assumes a teacher can judge \"predisposition\" reliably, which the text does not test. Inference: Bompa's long-term sequential model in this library ([[periodization--bompa/framework--ltad-sequential-model]]) also delays sport specialisation (generally not before about 14-15 in most sports) and cites three longitudinal studies favouring a multilateral base; the two agree on direction. Bompa's model is multilateral rather than grouped by motor regime, so the authors' first-stage grouping by predisposition is their own addition. It does not address athletes who already specialised."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: a child showing quick reactions or a capacity for speed-strength activities joins a speed-strength motor-regime group at the first stage of a Russian children's sport school, then is transferred to a specific sport school at the second stage (PDF p. 306 / printed p. 295)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"72b09007-a72a-4116-9ebb-4815c448d80b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"0600b20d-8e67-4516-8019-af1d67bb4e81","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaPwqFpGWjeXP8xWKD8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017YhvsfeB5f7TN8eXvrNocj","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.2\"\ntopic: accelerating specific adaptation\ncategory: [long-term-development, planning, special-strength, modelling]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Accelerating Specific Adaptation: Model the Sport, Sequence Its Demands, and Plan for Delays\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of accelerating specific adaptation emphasizes the key role of special strength training as the foundation for developing sports proficiency ... The idea is that specific adaptation should be accelerated by advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime.\" In practice, \"the task of functional specialisation should be based on accurate modelling of all aspects of the motor activity,\" whose requirements \"are then presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The authors list four means of achieving it: \"controlling the post-training supercompensatory phases by using the conjugate sequence method of training, by the heterochronicity principle of superimposed cycles of training the different components of fitness, by converging the partial effects of the training means, or by correctly periodised full or partial restoration after training (see 8.5).\" It also \"necessitates using statistical data which reflect the basic trends in the dynamics of the athlete's specific motor abilities in the given sport (see 2.2.3)\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' claim of effect: \"Specialised means of strength training selected to take into account the specific movements of the given sport and the participant's existing level of fitness produce a timely and substantial increase in relative and starting strength, significantly exceeding the average indices typical of the training of that type of athlete,\" and \"the use of complex, novel means of strength training enable highly qualified athletes to avoid the typically slow rate of increase in starting strength\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). No data or study is given for either statement on this page.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-range planning for an athlete with a target date: model the sport's demands, sequence them, track the athlete's abilities against norms for the sport, and keep a backup plan. The authors' contingency rule is the most directly usable part: success \"depends on anticipating any conditions which may delay the subsequent course of the STP,\" and when fitness lags \"due to unforeseen circumstances such as adaptational idiosyncrasies, illness or injury ... a contingency plan should always be available to maintain the fitness level of those systems which can still be exercised\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). Eric's baseline: \"Applied Performance's stated role in return-to-performance is to supply information that supports Athletics Performance and Athletic Medicine's decisions on training and RTP periodization\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 129, Explicit). Inference: the contingency rule (train what can still be trained during injury) is the authors' version of keeping the uninjured systems loaded during RTP.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (two definitions).** In 5.3.3 the same term names a volume saving: \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2)\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Section 5.4.2, which that sentence points to, defines it as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected\" and does not mention the 20% figure (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume), but the book never says so; quote whichever definition is meant. The effect claims are given without data. The \"statistical data\" on ability trends that the principle requires (2.2.3) may not exist for a given sport or population.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: highly qualified athletes using \"complex, novel means of strength training\" to avoid \"the typically slow rate of increase in starting strength\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). Inference: an injured lineman who cannot squat keeps upper-body and single-leg work going under the contingency rule, so the systems that can still be trained do not detrain.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]] (the 20% definition), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[framework--dynamometric-and-functional-profile-for-weak-link-identification]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"output_tokens":2150,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":104},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":754},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2150,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"cache_creation_input_tokens":754,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":754,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_017YhvsfeB5f7TN8eXvrNocj":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.2\"\ntopic: accelerating specific adaptation\ncategory: [long-term-development, planning, special-strength, modelling]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Accelerating Specific Adaptation: Model the Sport, Sequence Its Demands, and Plan for Delays\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of accelerating specific adaptation emphasizes the key role of special strength training as the foundation for developing sports proficiency ... The idea is that specific adaptation should be accelerated by advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime.\" In practice, \"the task of functional specialisation should be based on accurate modelling of all aspects of the motor activity,\" whose requirements \"are then presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The authors list four means of achieving it: \"controlling the post-training supercompensatory phases by using the conjugate sequence method of training, by the heterochronicity principle of superimposed cycles of training the different components of fitness, by converging the partial effects of the training means, or by correctly periodised full or partial restoration after training (see 8.5).\" It also \"necessitates using statistical data which reflect the basic trends in the dynamics of the athlete's specific motor abilities in the given sport (see 2.2.3)\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' claim of effect: \"Specialised means of strength training selected to take into account the specific movements of the given sport and the participant's existing level of fitness produce a timely and substantial increase in relative and starting strength, significantly exceeding the average indices typical of the training of that type of athlete,\" and \"the use of complex, novel means of strength training enable highly qualified athletes to avoid the typically slow rate of increase in starting strength\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). No data or study is given for either statement on this page.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-range planning for an athlete with a target date: model the sport's demands, sequence them, track the athlete's abilities against norms for the sport, and keep a backup plan. The authors' contingency rule is the most directly usable part: success \"depends on anticipating any conditions which may delay the subsequent course of the STP,\" and when fitness lags \"due to unforeseen circumstances such as adaptational idiosyncrasies, illness or injury ... a contingency plan should always be available to maintain the fitness level of those systems which can still be exercised\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). Eric's baseline: \"Applied Performance's stated role in return-to-performance is to supply information that supports Athletics Performance and Athletic Medicine's decisions on training and RTP periodization\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 129, Explicit). Inference: the contingency rule (train what can still be trained during injury) is the authors' version of keeping the uninjured systems loaded during RTP.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (two definitions).** In 5.3.3 the same term names a volume saving: \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2)\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Section 5.4.2, which that sentence points to, defines it as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected\" and does not mention the 20% figure (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume), but the book never says so; quote whichever definition is meant. The effect claims are given without data. The \"statistical data\" on ability trends that the principle requires (2.2.3) may not exist for a given sport or population.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: highly qualified athletes using \"complex, novel means of strength training\" to avoid \"the typically slow rate of increase in starting strength\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). Inference: an injured lineman who cannot squat keeps upper-body and single-leg work going under the contingency rule, so the systems that can still be trained do not detrain.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]] (the 20% definition), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[framework--dynamometric-and-functional-profile-for-weak-link-identification]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaPfG6sZK8vz5QayKzN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e6d1643e-0f9d-4bd0-88e2-097377067bef","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:14.047Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e6d1643e-0f9d-4bd0-88e2-097377067bef","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017YhvsfeB5f7TN8eXvrNocj","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"0659e78e-553c-494b-ac9c-5fd3065e120d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:14.147Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.2\"\ntopic: accelerating specific adaptation\ncategory: [long-term-development, planning, special-strength, modelling]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Accelerating Specific Adaptation: Model the Sport, Sequence Its Demands, and Plan for Delays\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of accelerating specific adaptation emphasizes the key role of special strength training as the foundation for developing sports proficiency ... The idea is that specific adaptation should be accelerated by advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected to be produced by the current training regime.\" In practice, \"the task of functional specialisation should be based on accurate modelling of all aspects of the motor activity,\" whose requirements \"are then presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The authors list four means of achieving it: \"controlling the post-training supercompensatory phases by using the conjugate sequence method of training, by the heterochronicity principle of superimposed cycles of training the different components of fitness, by converging the partial effects of the training means, or by correctly periodised full or partial restoration after training (see 8.5).\" It also \"necessitates using statistical data which reflect the basic trends in the dynamics of the athlete's specific motor abilities in the given sport (see 2.2.3)\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' claim of effect: \"Specialised means of strength training selected to take into account the specific movements of the given sport and the participant's existing level of fitness produce a timely and substantial increase in relative and starting strength, significantly exceeding the average indices typical of the training of that type of athlete,\" and \"the use of complex, novel means of strength training enable highly qualified athletes to avoid the typically slow rate of increase in starting strength\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). No data or study is given for either statement on this page.\n\n**When to apply** — Long-range planning for an athlete with a target date: model the sport's demands, sequence them, track the athlete's abilities against norms for the sport, and keep a backup plan. The authors' contingency rule is the most directly usable part: success \"depends on anticipating any conditions which may delay the subsequent course of the STP,\" and when fitness lags \"due to unforeseen circumstances such as adaptational idiosyncrasies, illness or injury ... a contingency plan should always be available to maintain the fitness level of those systems which can still be exercised\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). Eric's baseline: \"Applied Performance's stated role in return-to-performance is to supply information that supports Athletics Performance and Athletic Medicine's decisions on training and RTP periodization\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 129, Explicit). Inference: the contingency rule (train what can still be trained during injury) is the authors' version of keeping the uninjured systems loaded during RTP.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (two definitions).** In 5.3.3 the same term names a volume saving: \"the training volume over a macrocycle can be reduced by as much as 20%, a phenomenon referred to as the acceleration of specific adaptation (see 5.4.2)\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Section 5.4.2, which that sentence points to, defines it as \"advancing the stage when maximum sports proficiency is expected\" and does not mention the 20% figure (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The two can coexist (reach the peak sooner with less volume), but the book never says so; quote whichever definition is meant. The effect claims are given without data. The \"statistical data\" on ability trends that the principle requires (2.2.3) may not exist for a given sport or population.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: highly qualified athletes using \"complex, novel means of strength training\" to avoid \"the typically slow rate of increase in starting strength\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). Inference: an injured lineman who cannot squat keeps upper-body and single-leg work going under the contingency rule, so the systems that can still be trained do not detrain.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]] (the 20% definition), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[framework--dynamometric-and-functional-profile-for-weak-link-identification]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e6d1643e-0f9d-4bd0-88e2-097377067bef","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
