{"parentUuid":"7f354664-e00c-4e38-a5c3-bded4fd13fdd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TS4rya575PQE3yBNC7NDQP","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"1a327ae9-dcb2-405b-b9b4-8c6dfebce7ee","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:42.584Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks.md","oldString":"it omits, for example, flexibility-strength, which they list elsewhere).","newString":"it omits, for example, \"flexibility-strength,\" which they list in 5.6, PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312).","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.3\"\ntopic: specific correspondence of training effect\ncategory: [specificity, functional-specialisation, special-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specific Correspondence of the Training Effect: Specialisation by System and by Ability, and Four Programming Tasks\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of specific correspondence points to the need for correspondence between the general effect of the strength work and the specific character of the motor regime of the given sport,\" achieved by \"systematically organising means so that the resulting cumulative effect fully establishes the special strength fitness required at the current stage of the sports training process\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The body \"selectively reacts to the dominant motor regime and movement pattern,\" which \"causes local, functional improvement in the fitness qualities of the kinematic chain's motor system (specialisation of systems) and develops qualities which are appropriate to the motor skills of the sport (specialisation of ability).\" Fig 5.17 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297) shows this as two branches from external and internal factors: primary motor regime, then motor specifics of the sports exercise, then \"specialisation according to ability\" (absolute strength, acceleration strength, starting strength, reactive ability, speed of movement, strength-endurance); and pattern of the movement, then external mechanical influences, then \"specialisation according to physiological system,\" then key muscle groups, then the functional muscle complex. The four tasks (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296):\n1. \"Timeous development of the required level of special strength fitness\"\n2. \"Elimination, where necessary, of heterochronological stages which were initially suitable for the intense functional specialisation of the key muscle groups\"\n3. \"Elimination of any lag in the development of strength fitness of individual muscle groups\"\n4. \"Intensifying the development of the essential strength abilities during the stage of advanced sports mastery.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the selective-adaptation account: the body specialises both locally (systems, key muscle groups) and by ability, so strength work must feed both branches in the right order. They refer back to 2.2.4 for the specialisation account and give no further mechanism here.\n\n**When to apply** — Reviewing whether a strength programme matches the sport at the current stage: list the abilities in the left branch of Fig 5.17 that the sport needs, check the key muscle groups in the right branch, and look for any muscle group lagging behind the rest (task 3). Eric's baseline: \"The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76, Explicit, interview Q21). Inference: DSI is one way to spot the kind of lag task 3 describes, though the baseline flags DSI's cut-offs as unvalidated (Tension 17), and the authors do not name a test.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme and the four tasks are stated, not tested; no study is cited on these pages. Task 2 refers to \"heterochronological stages\" without defining them here; see the heterochronicity entry in Related. The ability list in Fig 5.17 is the authors' own taxonomy of strength qualities; it is not exhaustive (it omits, for example, flexibility-strength, which they list elsewhere). This entry overlaps the 4.4 functional-specialisation flowchart ([[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]]); the new material here is the system-vs-ability split and the four tasks.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. Inference: for a long jumper, the left branch would stress reactive ability and starting strength, the right branch the hip and knee extensors and plantarflexors as key muscle groups, and task 3 would target whichever of those lagged on testing.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]] (4.4), [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[framework--dynamometric-and-functional-profile-for-weak-link-identification]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":7,"newStart":23,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Reviewing whether a strength programme matches the sport at the current stage: list the abilities in the left branch of Fig 5.17 that the sport needs, check the key muscle groups in the right branch, and look for any muscle group lagging behind the rest (task 3). Eric's baseline: \"The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76, Explicit, interview Q21). Inference: DSI is one way to spot the kind of lag task 3 describes, though the baseline flags DSI's cut-offs as unvalidated (Tension 17), and the authors do not name a test."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The scheme and the four tasks are stated, not tested; no study is cited on these pages. Task 2 refers to \"heterochronological stages\" without defining them here; see the heterochronicity entry in Related. The ability list in Fig 5.17 is the authors' own taxonomy of strength qualities; it is not exhaustive (it omits, for example, flexibility-strength, which they list elsewhere). This entry overlaps the 4.4 functional-specialisation flowchart ([[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]]); the new material here is the system-vs-ability split and the four tasks.","+**When NOT to apply** — The scheme and the four tasks are stated, not tested; no study is cited on these pages. Task 2 refers to \"heterochronological stages\" without defining them here; see the heterochronicity entry in Related. The ability list in Fig 5.17 is the authors' own taxonomy of strength qualities; it is not exhaustive (it omits, for example, \"flexibility-strength,\" which they list in 5.6, PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312). This entry overlaps the 4.4 functional-specialisation flowchart ([[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]]); the new material here is the system-vs-ability split and the four tasks."," "," **Example** — The authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. Inference: for a long jumper, the left branch would stress reactive ability and starting strength, the right branch the hip and knee extensors and plantarflexors as key muscle groups, and task 3 would target whichever of those lagged on testing."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7f354664-e00c-4e38-a5c3-bded4fd13fdd","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1a327ae9-dcb2-405b-b9b4-8c6dfebce7ee","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaT5u2971fh9gxNggqt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FYzb82mKRiwf5SFRkMYhRf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.4\"\ntopic: maintaining the training effect\ncategory: [exercise-selection, progression, plyometrics, jumpers, multi-year-planning]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Maintain the Training Effect by Replacing Means Before They Go Stale: The Jumper's Sequence and the Multi-Year Cycle\n\n**What it is** — \"Preservation of the training effect means that the load should always produce a training effect by the systematic, timely introduction of more effective training means based on logical continuity,\" to avoid \"the states of stagnation and deterioration described in Chapter 1 (see Fig 1.32).\" In practice, \"as the training effect of some means decreases, so more effective, newer means are introduced which, in turn, are replaced by even more effective means.\" This needs \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.18 (verified against the page image) gives the authors' example for \"developing explosive leg strength in the preparation period of a jumper\" (Verkhoshansky 1969, 1970): jumping exercises, then barbell exercises, then jumps with weights, then depth jumping, each curve rising and flattening, with a dashed line marking each switch to the next, stronger means. Over years, \"The long term use of the conjugate sequence method involves implementing a repetitive, cyclical system of sequentially applied means, each of increasing intensity,\" revising the complex by \"successively replacing those which played an earlier progressive role ... (means A and B) with newer, more effective means (C and D), and finally with still other means (E)\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.19 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298; based on Verkhoshansky 1977) shows year 1 as A, B, C; year 2 as B, C, D; year 3 as C, D, E, each year starting from a higher training-effect level.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the sequential-means premise: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). They add, for qualified athletes, that \"the long-term use of the same means, even if the volume is increased, not only will not increase one's level of special fitness but will also decrease the existing level of speed-strength and especially, maximum strength\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). No physiological mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — Plan the next means before the current one plateaus; each year, drop the weakest means from the bottom of the sequence and add a stronger one at the top. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107). Inference: the authors' rule works on a different variable: Prilepin's chart sets the dose within a means, while this heuristic says when to change the means themselves.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Figs 5.18 and 5.19 are schematics without units or data, so they do not say how long each means lasts. The authors make the method depend on \"experimental assessment of the training effect\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297); without some measure of when a means has stopped working, the switch points are guesses. The sequence is for a jumper's explosive leg strength in the preparation period; the order for other qualities or sports is not given here. The plan assumes the athlete is still improving; the authors do not address maintenance-only periods such as an in-season phase.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a jumper's preparation period moving from jumping exercises to barbell exercises to jumps with weights to depth jumping, switching each time the previous means' curve flattens (Fig 5.18, PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the experimental case: groups that stayed on one means stalled), [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]] (Fig 4.1, which 5.4.4 cites), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1939,"cache_read_input_tokens":298702,"output_tokens":2049,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":138},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1939},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2049,"cache_read_input_tokens":298702,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1939,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1939,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FYzb82mKRiwf5SFRkMYhRf":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.4\"\ntopic: maintaining the training effect\ncategory: [exercise-selection, progression, plyometrics, jumpers, multi-year-planning]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Maintain the Training Effect by Replacing Means Before They Go Stale: The Jumper's Sequence and the Multi-Year Cycle\n\n**What it is** — \"Preservation of the training effect means that the load should always produce a training effect by the systematic, timely introduction of more effective training means based on logical continuity,\" to avoid \"the states of stagnation and deterioration described in Chapter 1 (see Fig 1.32).\" In practice, \"as the training effect of some means decreases, so more effective, newer means are introduced which, in turn, are replaced by even more effective means.\" This needs \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.18 (verified against the page image) gives the authors' example for \"developing explosive leg strength in the preparation period of a jumper\" (Verkhoshansky 1969, 1970): jumping exercises, then barbell exercises, then jumps with weights, then depth jumping, each curve rising and flattening, with a dashed line marking each switch to the next, stronger means. Over years, \"The long term use of the conjugate sequence method involves implementing a repetitive, cyclical system of sequentially applied means, each of increasing intensity,\" revising the complex by \"successively replacing those which played an earlier progressive role ... (means A and B) with newer, more effective means (C and D), and finally with still other means (E)\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.19 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298; based on Verkhoshansky 1977) shows year 1 as A, B, C; year 2 as B, C, D; year 3 as C, D, E, each year starting from a higher training-effect level.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the sequential-means premise: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). They add, for qualified athletes, that \"the long-term use of the same means, even if the volume is increased, not only will not increase one's level of special fitness but will also decrease the existing level of speed-strength and especially, maximum strength\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). No physiological mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — Plan the next means before the current one plateaus; each year, drop the weakest means from the bottom of the sequence and add a stronger one at the top. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107). Inference: the authors' rule works on a different variable: Prilepin's chart sets the dose within a means, while this heuristic says when to change the means themselves.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Figs 5.18 and 5.19 are schematics without units or data, so they do not say how long each means lasts. The authors make the method depend on \"experimental assessment of the training effect\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297); without some measure of when a means has stopped working, the switch points are guesses. The sequence is for a jumper's explosive leg strength in the preparation period; the order for other qualities or sports is not given here. The plan assumes the athlete is still improving; the authors do not address maintenance-only periods such as an in-season phase.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a jumper's preparation period moving from jumping exercises to barbell exercises to jumps with weights to depth jumping, switching each time the previous means' curve flattens (Fig 5.18, PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the experimental case: groups that stayed on one means stalled), [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]] (Fig 4.1, which 5.4.4 cites), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaSoYGGh5YqtDFhSZX9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"23ca11b6-6953-4ea3-936b-bc0ef658f710","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:55.976Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"23ca11b6-6953-4ea3-936b-bc0ef658f710","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FYzb82mKRiwf5SFRkMYhRf","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"4cc511f7-972e-486e-be0e-deea5b1291b4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:56:56.099Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.4\"\ntopic: maintaining the training effect\ncategory: [exercise-selection, progression, plyometrics, jumpers, multi-year-planning]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Maintain the Training Effect by Replacing Means Before They Go Stale: The Jumper's Sequence and the Multi-Year Cycle\n\n**What it is** — \"Preservation of the training effect means that the load should always produce a training effect by the systematic, timely introduction of more effective training means based on logical continuity,\" to avoid \"the states of stagnation and deterioration described in Chapter 1 (see Fig 1.32).\" In practice, \"as the training effect of some means decreases, so more effective, newer means are introduced which, in turn, are replaced by even more effective means.\" This needs \"experimental assessment of the training effect and categorisation of the specific complex of means\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.18 (verified against the page image) gives the authors' example for \"developing explosive leg strength in the preparation period of a jumper\" (Verkhoshansky 1969, 1970): jumping exercises, then barbell exercises, then jumps with weights, then depth jumping, each curve rising and flattening, with a dashed line marking each switch to the next, stronger means. Over years, \"The long term use of the conjugate sequence method involves implementing a repetitive, cyclical system of sequentially applied means, each of increasing intensity,\" revising the complex by \"successively replacing those which played an earlier progressive role ... (means A and B) with newer, more effective means (C and D), and finally with still other means (E)\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297). Fig 5.19 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298; based on Verkhoshansky 1977) shows year 1 as A, B, C; year 2 as B, C, D; year 3 as C, D, E, each year starting from a higher training-effect level.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the sequential-means premise: \"the training effect of any means diminishes as the body adapts to its influence\" (PDF p. 298 / printed p. 287). They add, for qualified athletes, that \"the long-term use of the same means, even if the volume is increased, not only will not increase one's level of special fitness but will also decrease the existing level of speed-strength and especially, maximum strength\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). No physiological mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — Plan the next means before the current one plateaus; each year, drop the weakest means from the bottom of the sequence and add a stronger one at the top. Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107). Inference: the authors' rule works on a different variable: Prilepin's chart sets the dose within a means, while this heuristic says when to change the means themselves.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Figs 5.18 and 5.19 are schematics without units or data, so they do not say how long each means lasts. The authors make the method depend on \"experimental assessment of the training effect\" (PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297); without some measure of when a means has stopped working, the switch points are guesses. The sequence is for a jumper's explosive leg strength in the preparation period; the order for other qualities or sports is not given here. The plan assumes the athlete is still improving; the authors do not address maintenance-only periods such as an in-season phase.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a jumper's preparation period moving from jumping exercises to barbell exercises to jumps with weights to depth jumping, switching each time the previous means' curve flattens (Fig 5.18, PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]] (the experimental case: groups that stayed on one means stalled), [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]] (Fig 4.1, which 5.4.4 cites), [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"23ca11b6-6953-4ea3-936b-bc0ef658f710","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
