{"parentUuid":"c6c4cfc0-053f-4157-8a77-7807b9103105","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaXsqrzdyCEpjFKrj3F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CyvoK232CtEpEUAZZ7CeZi","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md","old_string":"[[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]]\n","new_string":"[[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]] (Myszka chooses donor sports by shared movements and affordances, a skill-acquisition angle on the same selection question)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":635,"cache_read_input_tokens":309549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":635,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":45,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01CyvoK232CtEpEUAZZ7CeZi":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md","old_string":"[[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]]\n","new_string":"[[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]] (Myszka chooses donor sports by shared movements and affordances, a skill-acquisition angle on the same selection question)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaXbydQhkmgyt5ABUDf","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"58a013b7-b758-4899-abe1-1f04d32717a8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:47.041Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"58a013b7-b758-4899-abe1-1f04d32717a8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CyvoK232CtEpEUAZZ7CeZi","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d3f3245b-3074-4c98-a21d-571eecc276c0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:57:47.156Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label.md","oldString":"[[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]]\n","newString":"[[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]] (Myszka chooses donor sports by shared movements and affordances, a skill-acquisition angle on the same selection question)\n","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.5\"\ntopic: selecting supplementary sports\ncategory: [cross-training, periodization, gpp, exercise-selection]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Choose Supplementary Sports by the Fitness Qualities They Build and the Phase They Serve, Not by the Sport's Label\n\n**What it is** — The authors define Western \"cross training\" as \"using relevant secondary sports to augment the training effect produced in an athlete's primary sport,\" and tie it to the two ways of combining training from 5.3: supplementary activities can be prescribed \"concurrently with the athlete's main sport on the same day or during the same training phase or in a definite sequence where one supplementary activity gives way to another and finally to the main sport over a given period\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298). In Russia and Eastern Europe, they write, the choice \"does not simply focus primarily on the type of sport used, but rather on the relationship between the motor and fitness qualities in the supplementary activities and the primary sport,\" with \"qualitative and quantitative modelling of each sport involved so as to ensure that the supplementary sports do not impair performance in the primary sport.\" Their example of the wrong approach: \"It is inadequate, for example, to state that track-and-field athletics, rugby or American football require the development of strength, so that heavy weight training must constitute appropriate cross training ... this simplistic approach can offer little more than short term progress\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298). Siff (the passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person) adds that one must understand \"the different models of periodisation devised by Matveyev, Vorobyev, Verkhoshansky, Bogdanov, Kuzwecov and others\" and \"precisely which fitness qualities (static strength, dynamic strength, power, dynamic flexibility, muscle endurance and so forth) are developed by the primary and the secondary supplementary sports\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299, checked against the page image). The section closes with four questions: \"Will this activity be directly beneficial to my sport?, Will this activity be indirectly beneficial to my sport?, When will other activities be beneficial or detrimental to my sport? and Would the time be better spent training with sport specific exercises?\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is specificity plus phase: \"sporting prowess involves specialisation by physiological system and specialisation by ability or skill (see 2.2.3 and 5.4.3). Therefore, any cross training programme must ensure that it enhances and does not compromise either of these processes\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). Designing it needs \"the interaction between activities of different primary emphasis, the influence of short- and long-term after-effects of training, supercompensation, the specificity of training, periodisation and restoration\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298).\n\n**When to apply** — Whenever a supplementary sport or activity is proposed for an athlete: name the qualities it trains, the phase it is for, whether it enhances or aids recovery, and answer the four questions. The authors also flag the psychological side: \"learning to push against maximal loads during the late off-season and early season can enhance the overall motivation of a rugby or football player in coping with high levels of physical and mental stress\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 32, Explicit, interview Q3). Inference: Eric states that rule for tests and technology; the authors' four questions apply the same filter to supplementary activities.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The section is argued from Russian and Eastern European practice with no data or citations beyond Enoka (1988) for the limb effect below. The authors' claim that Western cross training \"has not advanced scientifically much beyond the way in which it was used several decades ago\" is opinion. They also note a terminology problem: physiologists use \"cross training\" for the effect whereby \"training one limb can produce a training effect in the opposite limb\" (Enoka 1988), useful \"where an individual cannot use one limb due to injury,\" and they would prefer \"combinational training or complementary training\" for multi-sport conditioning (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). Check which meaning a source uses before applying it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Olympic weightlifters and field athletes in Russia and Eastern Europe \"have included volleyball or basketball in their overall training regimens,\" and some supplementary sports are used off-season, some in early preparation, some in season, \"and still others as a means of recuperation or enhancing certain qualities such as fine motor control or explosiveness\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (specialisation by system and by ability), [[claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance]] (the contralateral \"cross-training\" effect), [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (the machine limits the authors repeat for cross training, PDF p. 311), [[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":4,"newStart":23,"newLines":4,"lines":[" "," **Example** — The authors' own: Olympic weightlifters and field athletes in Russia and Eastern Europe \"have included volleyball or basketball in their overall training regimens,\" and some supplementary sports are used off-season, some in early preparation, some in season, \"and still others as a means of recuperation or enhancing certain qualities such as fine motor control or explosiveness\" (PDF p. 309 / printed p. 298)."," ","-**Related** — [[heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (specialisation by system and by ability), [[claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance]] (the contralateral \"cross-training\" effect), [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (the machine limits the authors repeat for cross training, PDF p. 311), [[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]]","+**Related** — [[heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (specialisation by system and by ability), [[claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance]] (the contralateral \"cross-training\" effect), [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (the machine limits the authors repeat for cross training, PDF p. 311), [[skill-acquisition/enhancing-skill--myszka/framework--choosing-a-donor-sport]] (Myszka chooses donor sports by shared movements and affordances, a skill-acquisition angle on the same selection question)"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"58a013b7-b758-4899-abe1-1f04d32717a8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d3f3245b-3074-4c98-a21d-571eecc276c0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffaXsqrzdyCEpjFKrj3F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VVYvCLGDBRFcyucJiYhbbg","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.5\"\ntopic: phase-dependent cross training\ncategory: [cross-training, concurrent-training, aerobic, periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# No Supplementary Sport Is Simply Good or Bad: Distance Running Can Suit a Football Off-Season, Not Its Competitive Season\n\n**What it is** — The authors reject fixed lists: \"Some authors have been swift to categorise certain sports as beneficial and others as detrimental as 'cross training' activities for specific sports. This restricted view, however, fails to take into account the fact that every sports training programme consists of distinct overlapping phases, each concentrating on developing and integrating different fitness qualities. For example, it is erroneous to label cross-country running as totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players. Although prolonged aerobic activity generally is inadvisable for use during the competitive season, it can have a place during the early off-season period, since it can improve peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise. Similarly, weight training should not be dismissed from the training schedule of a distance runner, when it may be useful in strengthening certain muscles or enhancing local muscle endurance\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299, checked against the page image). The same page carries the counterweight: \"combinations of predominantly cardiovascular activities such as regular distance running, cycling or swimming with weightlifting, rugby, or other strength sports may be counterproductive, since muscular endurance and cardiovascular fitness may be enhanced at the expense of strength, muscle mass and power,\" and \"inappropriate weight training using largely bodybuilding techniques might be detrimental to the marathoner or endurance athlete, especially if it fails to increase functional relative strength.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the phase argument above and, for aerobic work in the early off-season, the stated benefit of \"peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). For the interference side they give no new mechanism here (\"As discussed previously\"); the Ch1 entry in Related carries their earlier fibre-type argument.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether to keep or drop an activity: ask which phase it is for, not whether it \"fits\" the sport. Watch for overtraining with new variety: \"Variety usually adds interest, ensures novelty of neuromuscular stimulation and can reduce the likelihood of stagnation, but the enthusiasm it engenders can cause the recent convert to raise the intensity or volume of training to inappropriately high levels and produce overtraining. ... The maxim of regular variation in training has to be applied with intelligent circumspection\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123, Inferred, transcript), and \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" (line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' early-off-season case for aerobic work rests on recuperation, which agrees with line 123; line 124 shows the department already caps that work by intensity rather than by banning it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Prolonged aerobic activity\" is, on the authors' own statement, \"generally ... inadvisable for use during the competitive season\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). The peripheral-vascularisation benefit is asserted without a citation. The authors do not give a dose (duration, frequency) for off-season aerobic work in football or rugby players. The same section says cardiovascular activities combined with strength sports \"may be counterproductive,\" so the permission is narrow: a phase, not a standing addition.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: cross-country running is not \"totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players\"; it \"can have a place during the early off-season period\" but generally not in the competitive season (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1, the authors' earlier interference argument), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]] (4.4, weight training for distance athletes; contested)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":635,"cache_read_input_tokens":309549,"output_tokens":2142,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":635},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2142,"cache_read_input_tokens":309549,"cache_creation_input_tokens":635,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":635,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VVYvCLGDBRFcyucJiYhbbg":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.5\"\ntopic: phase-dependent cross training\ncategory: [cross-training, concurrent-training, aerobic, periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# No Supplementary Sport Is Simply Good or Bad: Distance Running Can Suit a Football Off-Season, Not Its Competitive Season\n\n**What it is** — The authors reject fixed lists: \"Some authors have been swift to categorise certain sports as beneficial and others as detrimental as 'cross training' activities for specific sports. This restricted view, however, fails to take into account the fact that every sports training programme consists of distinct overlapping phases, each concentrating on developing and integrating different fitness qualities. For example, it is erroneous to label cross-country running as totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players. Although prolonged aerobic activity generally is inadvisable for use during the competitive season, it can have a place during the early off-season period, since it can improve peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise. Similarly, weight training should not be dismissed from the training schedule of a distance runner, when it may be useful in strengthening certain muscles or enhancing local muscle endurance\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299, checked against the page image). The same page carries the counterweight: \"combinations of predominantly cardiovascular activities such as regular distance running, cycling or swimming with weightlifting, rugby, or other strength sports may be counterproductive, since muscular endurance and cardiovascular fitness may be enhanced at the expense of strength, muscle mass and power,\" and \"inappropriate weight training using largely bodybuilding techniques might be detrimental to the marathoner or endurance athlete, especially if it fails to increase functional relative strength.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the phase argument above and, for aerobic work in the early off-season, the stated benefit of \"peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). For the interference side they give no new mechanism here (\"As discussed previously\"); the Ch1 entry in Related carries their earlier fibre-type argument.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether to keep or drop an activity: ask which phase it is for, not whether it \"fits\" the sport. Watch for overtraining with new variety: \"Variety usually adds interest, ensures novelty of neuromuscular stimulation and can reduce the likelihood of stagnation, but the enthusiasm it engenders can cause the recent convert to raise the intensity or volume of training to inappropriately high levels and produce overtraining. ... The maxim of regular variation in training has to be applied with intelligent circumspection\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123, Inferred, transcript), and \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" (line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' early-off-season case for aerobic work rests on recuperation, which agrees with line 123; line 124 shows the department already caps that work by intensity rather than by banning it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Prolonged aerobic activity\" is, on the authors' own statement, \"generally ... inadvisable for use during the competitive season\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). The peripheral-vascularisation benefit is asserted without a citation. The authors do not give a dose (duration, frequency) for off-season aerobic work in football or rugby players. The same section says cardiovascular activities combined with strength sports \"may be counterproductive,\" so the permission is narrow: a phase, not a standing addition.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: cross-country running is not \"totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players\"; it \"can have a place during the early off-season period\" but generally not in the competitive season (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1, the authors' earlier interference argument), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]] (4.4, weight training for distance athletes; contested)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaXbydQhkmgyt5ABUDf","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"44b36222-218f-487d-ad48-19bba2f96a6f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:01.374Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"44b36222-218f-487d-ad48-19bba2f96a6f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VVYvCLGDBRFcyucJiYhbbg","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"86e5f1d7-85a1-4d1a-9e45-bb3744e13996","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:58:01.502Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.5\"\ntopic: phase-dependent cross training\ncategory: [cross-training, concurrent-training, aerobic, periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# No Supplementary Sport Is Simply Good or Bad: Distance Running Can Suit a Football Off-Season, Not Its Competitive Season\n\n**What it is** — The authors reject fixed lists: \"Some authors have been swift to categorise certain sports as beneficial and others as detrimental as 'cross training' activities for specific sports. This restricted view, however, fails to take into account the fact that every sports training programme consists of distinct overlapping phases, each concentrating on developing and integrating different fitness qualities. For example, it is erroneous to label cross-country running as totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players. Although prolonged aerobic activity generally is inadvisable for use during the competitive season, it can have a place during the early off-season period, since it can improve peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise. Similarly, weight training should not be dismissed from the training schedule of a distance runner, when it may be useful in strengthening certain muscles or enhancing local muscle endurance\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299, checked against the page image). The same page carries the counterweight: \"combinations of predominantly cardiovascular activities such as regular distance running, cycling or swimming with weightlifting, rugby, or other strength sports may be counterproductive, since muscular endurance and cardiovascular fitness may be enhanced at the expense of strength, muscle mass and power,\" and \"inappropriate weight training using largely bodybuilding techniques might be detrimental to the marathoner or endurance athlete, especially if it fails to increase functional relative strength.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are the phase argument above and, for aerobic work in the early off-season, the stated benefit of \"peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). For the interference side they give no new mechanism here (\"As discussed previously\"); the Ch1 entry in Related carries their earlier fibre-type argument.\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding whether to keep or drop an activity: ask which phase it is for, not whether it \"fits\" the sport. Watch for overtraining with new variety: \"Variety usually adds interest, ensures novelty of neuromuscular stimulation and can reduce the likelihood of stagnation, but the enthusiasm it engenders can cause the recent convert to raise the intensity or volume of training to inappropriately high levels and produce overtraining. ... The maxim of regular variation in training has to be applied with intelligent circumspection\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts), not distance capacity\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 123, Inferred, transcript), and \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" (line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' early-off-season case for aerobic work rests on recuperation, which agrees with line 123; line 124 shows the department already caps that work by intensity rather than by banning it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Prolonged aerobic activity\" is, on the authors' own statement, \"generally ... inadvisable for use during the competitive season\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299). The peripheral-vascularisation benefit is asserted without a citation. The authors do not give a dose (duration, frequency) for off-season aerobic work in football or rugby players. The same section says cardiovascular activities combined with strength sports \"may be counterproductive,\" so the permission is narrow: a phase, not a standing addition.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: cross-country running is not \"totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players\"; it \"can have a place during the early off-season period\" but generally not in the competitive season (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1, the authors' earlier interference argument), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[claim--strength-training-raises-haemoglobin-myoglobin-and-aids-distance-sport]] (4.4, weight training for distance athletes; contested)\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"44b36222-218f-487d-ad48-19bba2f96a6f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
