ually the only significant benefits of the average commercial circuit are a modest development of local muscular endurance, moderate decrease in percentage bodyfat and a mild improvement in cardiovascular fitness.\" \"Alternation of conventional weight training on one day and endurance training on another day produces superior results to traditional circuit training in terms of improvements in strength, and cardiovascular stamina\" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301, checked against the page image). Circuit training is dated to \"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the rest interval. Circuit training \"operates on the assumption that it can simultaneously condition you aerobically and anaerobically,\" and since \"any rest intervals will tend to prevent the body from operating in the aerobic conditioning zone,\" it allows no rest beyond moving between stations (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301). \"Paradoxically, its major limitations are imposed by its very attempt to introduce aerobic conditioning into conventional weight training by eliminating rest intervals. The absence of rest periods prevents one from using heavy weights and developing strength, from holding static or resistance-assisted stretches to enhance flexibility, and from performing intensive explosive movements to improve speed\" (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image). Underneath is specificity: \"if one wishes to enhance strength, one has to do high intensity strength training with a minimum of prolonged aerobic training, since the latter tends to decrease strength\" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301).\n\n**When to apply** — When a circuit is proposed as an all-in-one strength and conditioning session, expect modest gains in each quality; for athletes who need real strength and real aerobic fitness, train them on separate days. Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once — priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit). Inference: the authors' rejection of \"simultaneous maximum conditioning\" (PDF p. 312) is the same priority logic applied to strength and endurance together.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors cite no study for any finding in this passage (\"It has been shown,\" \"A literature review reveals\"), so `supported` is a ceiling. The verdict is on \"traditional\" or \"average commercial\" circuits; the authors go on to argue that interval circuits with rest restored are far more useful (see Related), and they say \"This is not to state that circuit training is of little value\" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301). One figure on PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302 is unclear: conventional training rest of 2-4 minutes is said to be common \"for few repetitions (1-3) with loads close to one's 1RM or a fairly large number of repetitions (10-15) with over 80% of one's 1RM.\" The image confirms the wording, but 10-15 repetitions at over 80% of 1RM is not possible for most lifters; treat that number as unclear. The note that \"heavier individuals require longer recovery periods than their lighter colleagues\" has no citation.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: CWT combining gym apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines did not match the cardiovascular conditioning of running or cycling, and alternating weight-training days with endurance days beat it for both strength and stamina (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1, the authors' earlier CCT vs ICT verdict and fibre-type argument), [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"98641181-3640-45e2-bea8-7713549a42a2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"543ef00d-7b51-4bb4-86a2-d289ab51f6c9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffabWdGqZ28UKkLyYXMA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HZuMn33VaHifvj9Q6qK1kN","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit training classification\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, conditioning, terminology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuit Training Classified: Continuous, Interval and Mixed, Each Unresisted, Resisted or Combined\n\n**What it is** — Having argued that traditional circuits fail because they remove rest, the authors extend the concept: \"if rest intervals are re-introduced into the system, CT can be extended enormously to produce a far more versatile training modality.\" They call the new form Interval Circuit Training (ICT), \"familiar to all runners in the form of interval training and fartlek.\" A circuit is \"really an extended form of 'supersetting'\"; \"the term 'super circuit' should then be reserved for the type of ICT system which supersets a series of resistance exercises\" (PDF pp. 311, 313 / printed pp. 300, 302). The classification (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image):\n- **A. Continuous Circuit Training (CCT):** Unresisted (UNRECCT), Resisted (RECCT), Combined (CCCT).\n- **B. Interval Circuit Training (ICT):** Unresisted (UNRICT), Resisted (RICT), Combined (CICT).\n- **C. Mixed Circuit Training (MCT):** \"combinations of the preceding forms.\"\nAdd-ons the authors suggest: aerobic work \"by including a 15 minute period of cycling, jogging or skipping, preferably at the end of the session\"; stretching stations in an ICT course or a first warm-up round; \"A special 10-15 minute warming-up and flexibility circuit\"; and \"A 5-10 minute type of cooling-down circuit\" (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302). From these classes they list circuit types including cardiovascular, power, strength, plyometric, speed, agility, restoration, rehabilitation, muscle endurance, muscle bulking, flexibility, isometric, upper-body and lower-body circuits (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' argument is that rest intervals are what make heavy, explosive and stretching work possible, so restoring them lets one station layout serve many purposes: \"A single set of stations may be used to offer many of the above circuits, if the instructor understands how to vary the way in which each circuit must be executed\" (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). They also state that \"the rest periods and heavier loading characteristic of interval circuit training (ICT) causes one to employ a more technically correct style of execution,\" while CCT's work against the clock produces \"fatigue, carelessness and deterioration of motor skills\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).\n\n**When to apply** — Naming and designing a circuit: first decide continuous, interval or mixed, then unresisted, resisted or combined, then the purpose. Useful for group sessions, rehab groups or off-season general preparation where one facility must serve many goals. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122, Explicit). Inference: the CCT/ICT split is one way to label which energy system a circuit loads, which that line asks staff to do.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is a naming scheme the authors propose, not a tested model, hence `untested`. The authors limit all circuit forms to \"the average non-athlete or competitive athlete during the early preparatory phase of training\" (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]]). The ICT rest periods make it a different session from the original Morgan and Adamson circuit, so evidence about traditional circuits does not transfer to ICT, and vice versa.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Table 5.4 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311) gives two circuits with \"essentially the same exercises.\" Circuit A, a continuous circuit for \"cardiovascular fitness and muscle endurance,\" has 14 stations, starting with a 12-minute fixed cycle and inserting fixed cycle and jogging stations between resistance exercises. Circuit B, an interval circuit \"relying on higher resistance with longer rest intervals between stations to emphasize production of strength or muscle endurance,\" has 11 stations, a 4-minute fixed cycle and then resistance exercises only (PDF p. 321 / printed p. 310).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1), [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8204,"cache_read_input_tokens":313428,"output_tokens":4016,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":235},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8204},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4016,"cache_read_input_tokens":313428,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8204,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8204,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01HZuMn33VaHifvj9Q6qK1kN":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit training classification\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, conditioning, terminology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuit Training Classified: Continuous, Interval and Mixed, Each Unresisted, Resisted or Combined\n\n**What it is** — Having argued that traditional circuits fail because they remove rest, the authors extend the concept: \"if rest intervals are re-introduced into the system, CT can be extended enormously to produce a far more versatile training modality.\" They call the new form Interval Circuit Training (ICT), \"familiar to all runners in the form of interval training and fartlek.\" A circuit is \"really an extended form of 'supersetting'\"; \"the term 'super circuit' should then be reserved for the type of ICT system which supersets a series of resistance exercises\" (PDF pp. 311, 313 / printed pp. 300, 302). The classification (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image):\n- **A. Continuous Circuit Training (CCT):** Unresisted (UNRECCT), Resisted (RECCT), Combined (CCCT).\n- **B. Interval Circuit Training (ICT):** Unresisted (UNRICT), Resisted (RICT), Combined (CICT).\n- **C. Mixed Circuit Training (MCT):** \"combinations of the preceding forms.\"\nAdd-ons the authors suggest: aerobic work \"by including a 15 minute period of cycling, jogging or skipping, preferably at the end of the session\"; stretching stations in an ICT course or a first warm-up round; \"A special 10-15 minute warming-up and flexibility circuit\"; and \"A 5-10 minute type of cooling-down circuit\" (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302). From these classes they list circuit types including cardiovascular, power, strength, plyometric, speed, agility, restoration, rehabilitation, muscle endurance, muscle bulking, flexibility, isometric, upper-body and lower-body circuits (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' argument is that rest intervals are what make heavy, explosive and stretching work possible, so restoring them lets one station layout serve many purposes: \"A single set of stations may be used to offer many of the above circuits, if the instructor understands how to vary the way in which each circuit must be executed\" (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). They also state that \"the rest periods and heavier loading characteristic of interval circuit training (ICT) causes one to employ a more technically correct style of execution,\" while CCT's work against the clock produces \"fatigue, carelessness and deterioration of motor skills\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).\n\n**When to apply** — Naming and designing a circuit: first decide continuous, interval or mixed, then unresisted, resisted or combined, then the purpose. Useful for group sessions, rehab groups or off-season general preparation where one facility must serve many goals. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122, Explicit). Inference: the CCT/ICT split is one way to label which energy system a circuit loads, which that line asks staff to do.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is a naming scheme the authors propose, not a tested model, hence `untested`. The authors limit all circuit forms to \"the average non-athlete or competitive athlete during the early preparatory phase of training\" (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]]). The ICT rest periods make it a different session from the original Morgan and Adamson circuit, so evidence about traditional circuits does not transfer to ICT, and vice versa.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Table 5.4 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311) gives two circuits with \"essentially the same exercises.\" Circuit A, a continuous circuit for \"cardiovascular fitness and muscle endurance,\" has 14 stations, starting with a 12-minute fixed cycle and inserting fixed cycle and jogging stations between resistance exercises. Circuit B, an interval circuit \"relying on higher resistance with longer rest intervals between stations to emphasize production of strength or muscle endurance,\" has 11 stations, a 4-minute fixed cycle and then resistance exercises only (PDF p. 321 / printed p. 310).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1), [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffabDar98YUWZS4zuSpR","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"97bf693b-d5db-414f-a3a5-5cdcd80c5783","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:06.294Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"97bf693b-d5db-414f-a3a5-5cdcd80c5783","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HZuMn33VaHifvj9Q6qK1kN","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"05df7775-5747-427d-9f6b-4a987972ecda","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:06.393Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit training classification\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, conditioning, terminology]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuit Training Classified: Continuous, Interval and Mixed, Each Unresisted, Resisted or Combined\n\n**What it is** — Having argued that traditional circuits fail because they remove rest, the authors extend the concept: \"if rest intervals are re-introduced into the system, CT can be extended enormously to produce a far more versatile training modality.\" They call the new form Interval Circuit Training (ICT), \"familiar to all runners in the form of interval training and fartlek.\" A circuit is \"really an extended form of 'supersetting'\"; \"the term 'super circuit' should then be reserved for the type of ICT system which supersets a series of resistance exercises\" (PDF pp. 311, 313 / printed pp. 300, 302). The classification (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image):\n- **A. Continuous Circuit Training (CCT):** Unresisted (UNRECCT), Resisted (RECCT), Combined (CCCT).\n- **B. Interval Circuit Training (ICT):** Unresisted (UNRICT), Resisted (RICT), Combined (CICT).\n- **C. Mixed Circuit Training (MCT):** \"combinations of the preceding forms.\"\nAdd-ons the authors suggest: aerobic work \"by including a 15 minute period of cycling, jogging or skipping, preferably at the end of the session\"; stretching stations in an ICT course or a first warm-up round; \"A special 10-15 minute warming-up and flexibility circuit\"; and \"A 5-10 minute type of cooling-down circuit\" (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302). From these classes they list circuit types including cardiovascular, power, strength, plyometric, speed, agility, restoration, rehabilitation, muscle endurance, muscle bulking, flexibility, isometric, upper-body and lower-body circuits (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' argument is that rest intervals are what make heavy, explosive and stretching work possible, so restoring them lets one station layout serve many purposes: \"A single set of stations may be used to offer many of the above circuits, if the instructor understands how to vary the way in which each circuit must be executed\" (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). They also state that \"the rest periods and heavier loading characteristic of interval circuit training (ICT) causes one to employ a more technically correct style of execution,\" while CCT's work against the clock produces \"fatigue, carelessness and deterioration of motor skills\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).\n\n**When to apply** — Naming and designing a circuit: first decide continuous, interval or mixed, then unresisted, resisted or combined, then the purpose. Useful for group sessions, rehab groups or off-season general preparation where one facility must serve many goals. Eric's baseline: \"Everything we do is some form of conditioning\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 122, Explicit). Inference: the CCT/ICT split is one way to label which energy system a circuit loads, which that line asks staff to do.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is a naming scheme the authors propose, not a tested model, hence `untested`. The authors limit all circuit forms to \"the average non-athlete or competitive athlete during the early preparatory phase of training\" (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]]). The ICT rest periods make it a different session from the original Morgan and Adamson circuit, so evidence about traditional circuits does not transfer to ICT, and vice versa.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Table 5.4 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311) gives two circuits with \"essentially the same exercises.\" Circuit A, a continuous circuit for \"cardiovascular fitness and muscle endurance,\" has 14 stations, starting with a 12-minute fixed cycle and inserting fixed cycle and jogging stations between resistance exercises. Circuit B, an interval circuit \"relying on higher resistance with longer rest intervals between stations to emphasize production of strength or muscle endurance,\" has 11 stations, a 4-minute fixed cycle and then resistance exercises only (PDF p. 321 / printed p. 310).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1), [[claim--exercise-economy-improves-with-skill-and-interval-style-costs-more]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"97bf693b-d5db-414f-a3a5-5cdcd80c5783","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranc