{"parentUuid":"46d0f437-1391-4eb0-a293-88aca1d3d5e3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[],"toolInputCopies":[{"id":"toolu_0131dWZLiLo9CDWuCfFuoBRy","copy":"wire"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fc0d74e0-b98d-4afe-a325-441f9aeb0509","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:31.403Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fc0d74e0-b98d-4afe-a325-441f9aeb0509","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffafYUWS74fcuwyUpVij","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":863,"cache_read_input_tokens":325925,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":863,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":2152,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffaeoZ6fbFsVUTSckVs2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"27191d0c-9d66-4b8e-9730-19a87b960277","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:31.402Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"27191d0c-9d66-4b8e-9730-19a87b960277","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffafYUWS74fcuwyUpVij","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NSWUdeGZ1JURoKUqGJiDnC","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit design parameters\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, load-prescription, conditioning]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Aerobic vs Anaerobic Circuits: Rough Guidelines for Reps, Load, Rest and Laps\n\n**What it is** — The authors give \"rough guidelines,\" warning that \"a considerable degree of individuality is involved and no precise figures can be quoted to be universally appropriate for everyone\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304, checked against the page image). Cardiovascular circuits \"all fall under the category of CCT,\" and \"A true aerobic circuit would involve the predominant use of very low resistance devices or prolonged rhythmic movements\"; anaerobic circuits \"may be either CCT (continuous) or ICT (interval), with the former resulting largely in muscle endurance, and variations of the latter developing strength, power, muscle endurance, speed or flexibility.\"\n\n| | Cardiovascular (aerobic) circuits | Anaerobic circuits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rest | \"Continuous activity or very brief intervals\" between stations | \"Rest intervals between stations to permit some recovery (1-4 minutes)\" |\n| Station mix | aerobic activity alternating \"between resistance stations\" | \"Fewer aerobic stations and more resistance stations\" |\n| Reps per resistance station | \"20-40\" | \"3-8\" |\n| Load | \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" | \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" |\n| Laps (\"excursions\") | \"5-10\" | \"3-5\" |\n\nSub-cases (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304): for dynamic muscle endurance, \"resistance should be nearer 50% of 1RM, repetitions should be nearer 50, number of excursions should be near 4 and rest intervals should be shorter\"; for strength and power, \"resistance should exceed 75% of 1RM, repetitions should not exceed 5, rest intervals should be nearer 2 minutes and there should be no more than 3 excursions\"; \"Development of power generally requires the smallest number of repetitions\"; flexibility circuits \"may require static stretches to be held for 20-30 seconds.\" Agility circuits \"necessitate many repetitions to imprint the correct movement patterns,\" and restoration circuits must be \"of low intensity, moderate duration and low complexity,\" using patterns \"such as those used in physiotherapeutic PNF or Tai Chi\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). A second load scheme, \"applicable to the average programme designed to moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance,\" uses \"40-50% of 10RM for beginners, 40-50% of 5RM for intermediate trainees, 40-50% of 1RM for advanced trainees,\" and \"For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie the split to rest: continuous work keeps the circuit aerobic, while rest allows heavier loads and fewer reps for strength, power and speed (PDF pp. 312-313, 315 / printed pp. 301-302, 304). They give no further mechanism and cite no study; they refer readers to Table 1.1 \"for further information\" (PDF p. 315).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting first-draft numbers for a group circuit, then adjusting for the individual. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' cardiovascular circuit has no work:rest rule at all, so for football the baseline's work:rest and speed caps would be the binding limits.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (three load figures for muscle-endurance circuits).** The anaerobic column says 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM; the muscle-endurance sub-case of the same anaerobic category says about 50% of 1RM for about 50 reps (the image confirms \"50\"); and PDF p. 317 gives 40-50% of 10RM, 5RM or 1RM for programmes aimed at muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance. Fifty reps at 50% of 1RM also falls outside both columns of the table. The text gives no way to reconcile them. All percentage figures assume a tested max (1RM, 5RM or 10RM). The authors' own conclusion limits circuits to non-athletes and the early preparatory phase (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312), which sits uneasily with \"strength circuits\" at over 75% of 1RM; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own strength-and-power circuit: over 75% of 1RM, no more than 5 reps, rest nearer 2 minutes, no more than 3 laps (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":863,"cache_read_input_tokens":325925,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":863,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01NSWUdeGZ1JURoKUqGJiDnC":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit design parameters\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, load-prescription, conditioning]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Aerobic vs Anaerobic Circuits: Rough Guidelines for Reps, Load, Rest and Laps\n\n**What it is** — The authors give \"rough guidelines,\" warning that \"a considerable degree of individuality is involved and no precise figures can be quoted to be universally appropriate for everyone\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304, checked against the page image). Cardiovascular circuits \"all fall under the category of CCT,\" and \"A true aerobic circuit would involve the predominant use of very low resistance devices or prolonged rhythmic movements\"; anaerobic circuits \"may be either CCT (continuous) or ICT (interval), with the former resulting largely in muscle endurance, and variations of the latter developing strength, power, muscle endurance, speed or flexibility.\"\n\n| | Cardiovascular (aerobic) circuits | Anaerobic circuits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rest | \"Continuous activity or very brief intervals\" between stations | \"Rest intervals between stations to permit some recovery (1-4 minutes)\" |\n| Station mix | aerobic activity alternating \"between resistance stations\" | \"Fewer aerobic stations and more resistance stations\" |\n| Reps per resistance station | \"20-40\" | \"3-8\" |\n| Load | \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" | \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" |\n| Laps (\"excursions\") | \"5-10\" | \"3-5\" |\n\nSub-cases (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304): for dynamic muscle endurance, \"resistance should be nearer 50% of 1RM, repetitions should be nearer 50, number of excursions should be near 4 and rest intervals should be shorter\"; for strength and power, \"resistance should exceed 75% of 1RM, repetitions should not exceed 5, rest intervals should be nearer 2 minutes and there should be no more than 3 excursions\"; \"Development of power generally requires the smallest number of repetitions\"; flexibility circuits \"may require static stretches to be held for 20-30 seconds.\" Agility circuits \"necessitate many repetitions to imprint the correct movement patterns,\" and restoration circuits must be \"of low intensity, moderate duration and low complexity,\" using patterns \"such as those used in physiotherapeutic PNF or Tai Chi\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). A second load scheme, \"applicable to the average programme designed to moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance,\" uses \"40-50% of 10RM for beginners, 40-50% of 5RM for intermediate trainees, 40-50% of 1RM for advanced trainees,\" and \"For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie the split to rest: continuous work keeps the circuit aerobic, while rest allows heavier loads and fewer reps for strength, power and speed (PDF pp. 312-313, 315 / printed pp. 301-302, 304). They give no further mechanism and cite no study; they refer readers to Table 1.1 \"for further information\" (PDF p. 315).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting first-draft numbers for a group circuit, then adjusting for the individual. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' cardiovascular circuit has no work:rest rule at all, so for football the baseline's work:rest and speed caps would be the binding limits.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (three load figures for muscle-endurance circuits).** The anaerobic column says 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM; the muscle-endurance sub-case of the same anaerobic category says about 50% of 1RM for about 50 reps (the image confirms \"50\"); and PDF p. 317 gives 40-50% of 10RM, 5RM or 1RM for programmes aimed at muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance. Fifty reps at 50% of 1RM also falls outside both columns of the table. The text gives no way to reconcile them. All percentage figures assume a tested max (1RM, 5RM or 10RM). The authors' own conclusion limits circuits to non-athletes and the early preparatory phase (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312), which sits uneasily with \"strength circuits\" at over 75% of 1RM; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own strength-and-power circuit: over 75% of 1RM, no more than 5 reps, rest nearer 2 minutes, no more than 3 laps (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffaeoZ6fbFsVUTSckVs2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"56bfccb1-eac4-4b9b-a1ae-ce0ddb72626c","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:47.700Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"56bfccb1-eac4-4b9b-a1ae-ce0ddb72626c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NSWUdeGZ1JURoKUqGJiDnC","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"d70cd5bb-cc56-4903-84f6-3bf41703da4e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T15:59:47.787Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit design parameters\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, load-prescription, conditioning]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Aerobic vs Anaerobic Circuits: Rough Guidelines for Reps, Load, Rest and Laps\n\n**What it is** — The authors give \"rough guidelines,\" warning that \"a considerable degree of individuality is involved and no precise figures can be quoted to be universally appropriate for everyone\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304, checked against the page image). Cardiovascular circuits \"all fall under the category of CCT,\" and \"A true aerobic circuit would involve the predominant use of very low resistance devices or prolonged rhythmic movements\"; anaerobic circuits \"may be either CCT (continuous) or ICT (interval), with the former resulting largely in muscle endurance, and variations of the latter developing strength, power, muscle endurance, speed or flexibility.\"\n\n| | Cardiovascular (aerobic) circuits | Anaerobic circuits |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rest | \"Continuous activity or very brief intervals\" between stations | \"Rest intervals between stations to permit some recovery (1-4 minutes)\" |\n| Station mix | aerobic activity alternating \"between resistance stations\" | \"Fewer aerobic stations and more resistance stations\" |\n| Reps per resistance station | \"20-40\" | \"3-8\" |\n| Load | \"usually not more than 30% of 1RM\" | \"usually more than 70% of 1RM\" |\n| Laps (\"excursions\") | \"5-10\" | \"3-5\" |\n\nSub-cases (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304): for dynamic muscle endurance, \"resistance should be nearer 50% of 1RM, repetitions should be nearer 50, number of excursions should be near 4 and rest intervals should be shorter\"; for strength and power, \"resistance should exceed 75% of 1RM, repetitions should not exceed 5, rest intervals should be nearer 2 minutes and there should be no more than 3 excursions\"; \"Development of power generally requires the smallest number of repetitions\"; flexibility circuits \"may require static stretches to be held for 20-30 seconds.\" Agility circuits \"necessitate many repetitions to imprint the correct movement patterns,\" and restoration circuits must be \"of low intensity, moderate duration and low complexity,\" using patterns \"such as those used in physiotherapeutic PNF or Tai Chi\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). A second load scheme, \"applicable to the average programme designed to moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance,\" uses \"40-50% of 10RM for beginners, 40-50% of 5RM for intermediate trainees, 40-50% of 1RM for advanced trainees,\" and \"For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors tie the split to rest: continuous work keeps the circuit aerobic, while rest allows heavier loads and fewer reps for strength, power and speed (PDF pp. 312-313, 315 / printed pp. 301-302, 304). They give no further mechanism and cite no study; they refer readers to Table 1.1 \"for further information\" (PDF p. 315).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting first-draft numbers for a group circuit, then adjusting for the individual. Eric's baseline: \"Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' cardiovascular circuit has no work:rest rule at all, so for football the baseline's work:rest and speed caps would be the binding limits.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (three load figures for muscle-endurance circuits).** The anaerobic column says 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM; the muscle-endurance sub-case of the same anaerobic category says about 50% of 1RM for about 50 reps (the image confirms \"50\"); and PDF p. 317 gives 40-50% of 10RM, 5RM or 1RM for programmes aimed at muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance. Fifty reps at 50% of 1RM also falls outside both columns of the table. The text gives no way to reconcile them. All percentage figures assume a tested max (1RM, 5RM or 10RM). The authors' own conclusion limits circuits to non-athletes and the early preparatory phase (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312), which sits uneasily with \"strength circuits\" at over 75% of 1RM; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]].\n\n**Example** — The authors' own strength-and-power circuit: over 75% of 1RM, no more than 5 reps, rest nearer 2 minutes, no more than 3 laps (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"56bfccb1-eac4-4b9b-a1ae-ce0ddb72626c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d70cd5bb-cc56-4903-84f6-3bf41703da4e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffafYUWS74fcuwyUpVij","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013BPvtB8dcxDVDHLu1gcFKh","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit progression and testing\ncategory: [circuit-training, progression, load-prescription, novices, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Progress Circuits From Simple to Complex, and Give Novices About Three Weeks of Light Loading Before Any RM Test\n\n**What it is** — \"there is no such entity as the best circuit, only the most appropriate and effective circuit for achieving specific fitness aims in a particular person at a particular stage of training.\" Each circuit should be run at beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, and \"Circuits should always progress from simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They prefer the Bulgarian kilogram method as \"more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" while noting both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306).\n\n**When to apply** — Group circuits, first-year athletes, and rehab or return groups where a max test would be premature. Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 61, Explicit, interview Q10). Inference: the authors' \"add 5 kg, 10 kg to the starting load\" method needs no max at all, which fits that practice better than the percentage system. On heart rate, Eric's baseline: \"The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method\" (line 119, Explicit, interview Q20). Inference: the authors' circuit targets use the same 220 - age estimate, but as a percentage of maximum heart rate, not of threshold.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The three-week and three-month figures are given without a source. The heart-rate bands are percentages of an age-predicted maximum (220 - age), which carries large individual error; the authors call it \"a popular method,\" not a validated one. Inference: 40-50% of age-predicted maximum is a very low intensity for any trained athlete; these bands are written for fitness-centre clients. The Russian and Bulgarian labels are the authors' names for the two methods; no source is given for the attribution.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.20 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), \"A basic multi-level circuit\" in which \"the total beginner can work twice through the circuit from A to B; after a week or two, he may go 3 times ... and finally ... as many as 6\"; intermediates add stations and work \"3-6 times through the circuit A to C\"; advanced athletes do \"3-5 excursions through circuit A to D.\"\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":863,"cache_read_input_tokens":325925,"output_tokens":4303,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":291},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":863},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4303,"cache_read_input_tokens":325925,"cache_creation_input_tokens":863,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":863,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013BPvtB8dcxDVDHLu1gcFKh":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit progression and testing\ncategory: [circuit-training, progression, load-prescription, novices, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Progress Circuits From Simple to Complex, and Give Novices About Three Weeks of Light Loading Before Any RM Test\n\n**What it is** — \"there is no such entity as the best circuit, only the most appropriate and effective circuit for achieving specific fitness aims in a particular person at a particular stage of training.\" Each circuit should be run at beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, and \"Circuits should always progress from simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They prefer the Bulgarian kilogram method as \"more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" while noting both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306).\n\n**When to apply** — Group circuits, first-year athletes, and rehab or return groups where a max test would be premature. Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 61, Explicit, interview Q10). Inference: the authors' \"add 5 kg, 10 kg to the starting load\" method needs no max at all, which fits that practice better than the percentage system. On heart rate, Eric's baseline: \"The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method\" (line 119, Explicit, interview Q20). Inference: the authors' circuit targets use the same 220 - age estimate, but as a percentage of maximum heart rate, not of threshold.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The three-week and three-month figures are given without a source. The heart-rate bands are percentages of an age-predicted maximum (220 - age), which carries large individual error; the authors call it \"a popular method,\" not a validated one. Inference: 40-50% of age-predicted maximum is a very low intensity for any trained athlete; these bands are written for fitness-centre clients. The Russian and Bulgarian labels are the authors' names for the two methods; no source is given for the attribution.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.20 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), \"A basic multi-level circuit\" in which \"the total beginner can work twice through the circuit from A to B; after a week or two, he may go 3 times ... and finally ... as many as 6\"; intermediates add stations and work \"3-6 times through the circuit A to C\"; advanced athletes do \"3-5 excursions through circuit A to D.\"\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffaeoZ6fbFsVUTSckVs2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2ba366c2-a850-413b-ab44-63590b719461","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:02.690Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
