{"parentUuid":"eb6b0dc1-fb7a-427a-a9ca-199cbac8e9bb","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffaip3y4mCCquYZN3cFN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012n9E3juVGtv9ARzjGfcs7z","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md","old_string":"for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\"","new_string":"for ICT strength circuits, \"increases in resistance necessitate decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals between successive stations.\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4577,"cache_read_input_tokens":326788,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4577,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_012n9E3juVGtv9ARzjGfcs7z":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md","old_string":"for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\"","new_string":"for ICT strength circuits, \"increases in resistance necessitate decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals between successive stations.\""}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffai91iJSZjSxdyU3UWu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"49381cd2-6bca-4682-8cdc-69499dd20ccb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:16.567Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"49381cd2-6bca-4682-8cdc-69499dd20ccb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012n9E3juVGtv9ARzjGfcs7z","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"aedb1424-3a7a-4cf0-baf5-cf8a558a5d21","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:16.643Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing.md","oldString":"for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\"","newString":"for ICT strength circuits, \"increases in resistance necessitate decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals between successive stations.\"","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit progression and testing\ncategory: [circuit-training, progression, load-prescription, novices, testing]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Progress Circuits From Simple to Complex, and Give Novices About Three Weeks of Light Loading Before Any RM Test\n\n**What it is** — \"there is no such entity as the best circuit, only the most appropriate and effective circuit for achieving specific fitness aims in a particular person at a particular stage of training.\" Each circuit should be run at beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, and \"Circuits should always progress from simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They prefer the Bulgarian kilogram method as \"more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" while noting both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306).\n\n**When to apply** — Group circuits, first-year athletes, and rehab or return groups where a max test would be premature. Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 61, Explicit, interview Q10). Inference: the authors' \"add 5 kg, 10 kg to the starting load\" method needs no max at all, which fits that practice better than the percentage system. On heart rate, Eric's baseline: \"The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method\" (line 119, Explicit, interview Q20). Inference: the authors' circuit targets use the same 220 - age estimate, but as a percentage of maximum heart rate, not of threshold.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The three-week and three-month figures are given without a source. The heart-rate bands are percentages of an age-predicted maximum (220 - age), which carries large individual error; the authors call it \"a popular method,\" not a validated one. Inference: 40-50% of age-predicted maximum is a very low intensity for any trained athlete; these bands are written for fitness-centre clients. The Russian and Bulgarian labels are the authors' names for the two methods; no source is given for the attribution.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.20 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), \"A basic multi-level circuit\" in which \"the total beginner can work twice through the circuit from A to B; after a week or two, he may go 3 times ... and finally ... as many as 6\"; intermediates add stations and work \"3-6 times through the circuit A to C\"; advanced athletes do \"3-5 excursions through circuit A to D.\"\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":7,"newStart":13,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," # Progress Circuits From Simple to Complex, and Give Novices About Three Weeks of Light Loading Before Any RM Test"," ","-**What it is** — \"there is no such entity as the best circuit, only the most appropriate and effective circuit for achieving specific fitness aims in a particular person at a particular stage of training.\" Each circuit should be run at beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, and \"Circuits should always progress from simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, more resistance \"necessitate[s] decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).","+**What it is** — \"there is no such entity as the best circuit, only the most appropriate and effective circuit for achieving specific fitness aims in a particular person at a particular stage of training.\" Each circuit should be run at beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, and \"Circuits should always progress from simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, \"increases in resistance necessitate decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals between successive stations.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image)."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They prefer the Bulgarian kilogram method as \"more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" while noting both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"49381cd2-6bca-4682-8cdc-69499dd20ccb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
