simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, \"increases in resistance necessitate decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals between successive stations.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They prefer the Bulgarian kilogram method as \"more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" while noting both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306).\n\n**When to apply** — Group circuits, first-year athletes, and rehab or return groups where a max test would be premature. Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 61, Explicit, interview Q10). Inference: the authors' \"add 5 kg, 10 kg to the starting load\" method needs no max at all, which fits that practice better than the percentage system. On heart rate, Eric's baseline: \"The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method\" (line 119, Explicit, interview Q20). Inference: the authors' circuit targets use the same 220 - age estimate, but as a percentage of maximum heart rate, not of threshold.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The three-week and three-month figures are given without a source. The heart-rate bands are percentages of an age-predicted maximum (220 - age), which carries large individual error; the authors call it \"a popular method,\" not a validated one. Inference: 40-50% of age-predicted maximum is a very low intensity for any trained athlete; these bands are written for fitness-centre clients. The Russian and Bulgarian labels are the authors' names for the two methods; no source is given for the attribution.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.20 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), \"A basic multi-level circuit\" in which \"the total beginner can work twice through the circuit from A to B; after a week or two, he may go 3 times ... and finally ... as many as 6\"; intermediates add stations and work \"3-6 times through the circuit A to C\"; advanced athletes do \"3-5 excursions through circuit A to D.\"\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--program-variable-complexity-scales-with-training-age]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — \"there is no such entity as the best circuit, only the most appropriate and effective circuit for achieving specific fitness aims in a particular person at a particular stage of training.\" Each circuit should be run at beginner, intermediate and advanced levels, and \"Circuits should always progress from simple to complex, light to strenuous, short to long, and low resistance to high resistance\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). The progression levers: more stations; more resistance (\"especially in strength circuits\"); more reps per station; more laps; hybrid exercises at some stations; harder stations replacing easier ones; changing contraction type (concentric, isometric, eccentric); and higher working rate \"assessed by heart rate or Rating of Perceived Exertion.\" Limits: \"up to a maximum of about 1 hour for CCT aerobic circuits\"; for ICT strength circuits, \"increases in resistance necessitate decreases in the number of repetitions and increases in duration of the rest intervals between successive stations.\" Loads can be set as \"a certain percentage of one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Russian system)\" or \"a certain number of kilograms below one's 1RM, 6RM or 10RM (Bulgarian system).\" Testing: \"Maximal stress testing ... is not generally advisable for the novice. There should be a pre-testing phase ... It is preferable to start with very light loads ... and to prescribe programmes in terms of adding 5kg, 10kg and so forth to one's starting load. After completion of about three weeks of this pre-testing phase, the trainee may then attempt a 6RM or 10RM. After some three months, it should be safe for the novice to attempt a 1RM, if necessary\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). Aerobic circuit heart-rate targets: \"40-50% of maximal recommended heart rate (i.e.: 220 - Age) for beginner circuits,\" \"50-65%\" for intermediate and \"65-80%\" for advanced (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They prefer the Bulgarian kilogram method as \"more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" while noting both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306).","+**Why it works** — The authors' reasons for pre-testing are skill and tolerance: the novice must learn \"the necessary skills of using the test apparatus and of adapting to the specific nature of the new physical stresses in his life\" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305). They say \"Both systems have their respective merits and deficiencies,\" that the kilogram (Bulgarian) method \"would appear to be more intuitive and generally easier to calculate mentally,\" and they note both methods share \"the disadvantage of being based on testing the trainee with a maximal effort.\" They add: \"individual variations should always be taken into account, and after-exercise condition should be monitored regularly (in terms of tiredness, muscle soreness and other factors discussed in 8.4)\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306)."," "," **When to apply** — Group circuits, first-year athletes, and rehab or return groups where a max test would be premature. Eric's baseline: \"No sport performs true 1RM testing. Maxes are projected from observation plus the athlete's history.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 61, Explicit, interview Q10). Inference: the authors' \"add 5 kg, 10 kg to the starting load\" method needs no max at all, which fits that practice better than the percentage system. On heart rate, Eric's baseline: \"The anaerobic-threshold heart rate in use is the age-based method\" (line 119, Explicit, interview Q20). Inference: the authors' circuit targets use the same 220 - age estimate, but as a percentage of maximum heart rate, not of threshold."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"644428d2-b7d6-42d7-9ef3-152fee31ead9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7de8791d-8025-4f54-9d33-7fcd028e3b45","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffaip3y4mCCquYZN3cFN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TbRJuiX3uS8MPATw2HqacM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: station order by active rest\ncategory: [circuit-training, exercise-order, recovery, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Order Circuit Stations by the Setchinov Phenomenon: Alternate Upper and Lower, Front and Back, Agonist and Antagonist\n\n**What it is** — \"The effectiveness of any circuit may be enhanced by taking cognizance of the Setchinov phenomenon, which encourages restoration by means of diverting activity performed between exercises. For example, this may entail exercise of upper body muscles alternating with exercise of unfatigued muscles of the legs. In applying this principle to circuit training, upper body stations should then alternately exercise upper and lower body muscles, back and front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page images, PDF pp. 319-320 / printed pp. 308-309): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference: in a football off-season circuit, a lower-body station (split squat) followed by an upper pull (row), then a lower-body hinge, then an upper push, follows the authors' rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Alternation helps local recovery, not whole-body fatigue: a circuit that alternates regions but has no rest still limits load and speed, which is the authors' main criticism of traditional circuits (see Related). For maximal strength or power work the authors want rest intervals, not just alternation (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). The authors give no data for how much the Setchinov effect improves a circuit.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.25, aerobic stations such as fixed cycles or skipping alternating with resistance stations, \"with the latter alternating between upper and lower body movements\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (order of exercises changes the outcome), [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4577,"cache_read_input_tokens":326788,"output_tokens":2122,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":162},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4577},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2122,"cache_read_input_tokens":326788,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4577,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4577,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TbRJuiX3uS8MPATw2HqacM":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: station order by active rest\ncategory: [circuit-training, exercise-order, recovery, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Order Circuit Stations by the Setchinov Phenomenon: Alternate Upper and Lower, Front and Back, Agonist and Antagonist\n\n**What it is** — \"The effectiveness of any circuit may be enhanced by taking cognizance of the Setchinov phenomenon, which encourages restoration by means of diverting activity performed between exercises. For example, this may entail exercise of upper body muscles alternating with exercise of unfatigued muscles of the legs. In applying this principle to circuit training, upper body stations should then alternately exercise upper and lower body muscles, back and front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page images, PDF pp. 319-320 / printed pp. 308-309): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference: in a football off-season circuit, a lower-body station (split squat) followed by an upper pull (row), then a lower-body hinge, then an upper push, follows the authors' rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Alternation helps local recovery, not whole-body fatigue: a circuit that alternates regions but has no rest still limits load and speed, which is the authors' main criticism of traditional circuits (see Related). For maximal strength or power work the authors want rest intervals, not just alternation (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). The authors give no data for how much the Setchinov effect improves a circuit.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.25, aerobic stations such as fixed cycles or skipping alternating with resistance stations, \"with the latter alternating between upper and lower body movements\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (order of exercises changes the outcome), [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011Cffai91iJSZjSxdyU3UWu","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"692425a3-b430-4ce3-b19b-8b99062473e8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:31.148Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
