d front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page images, PDF pp. 319-320 / printed pp. 308-309): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference: in a football off-season circuit, a lower-body station (split squat) followed by an upper pull (row), then a lower-body hinge, then an upper push, follows the authors' rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Alternation helps local recovery, not whole-body fatigue: a circuit that alternates regions but has no rest still limits load and speed, which is the authors' main criticism of traditional circuits (see Related). For maximal strength or power work the authors want rest intervals, not just alternation (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). The authors give no data for how much the Setchinov effect improves a circuit.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.25, aerobic stations such as fixed cycles or skipping alternating with resistance stations, \"with the latter alternating between upper and lower body movements\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (order of exercises changes the outcome), [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":7,"newStart":13,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," # Order Circuit Stations by the Setchinov Phenomenon: Alternate Upper and Lower, Front and Back, Agonist and Antagonist"," ","-**What it is** — \"The effectiveness of any circuit may be enhanced by taking cognizance of the Setchinov phenomenon, which encourages restoration by means of diverting activity performed between exercises. For example, this may entail exercise of upper body muscles alternating with exercise of unfatigued muscles of the legs. In applying this principle to circuit training, upper body stations should then alternately exercise upper and lower body muscles, back and front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page images, PDF pp. 319-320 / printed pp. 308-309): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\"","+**What it is** — \"The effectiveness of any circuit may be enhanced by taking cognizance of the Setchinov phenomenon, which encourages restoration by means of diverting activity performed between exercises. For example, this may entail exercise of upper body muscles alternating with exercise of unfatigued muscles of the legs. In applying this principle to circuit training, upper body stations should then alternately exercise upper and lower body muscles, back and front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page image, PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308; described in the text on PDF pp. 319-320): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\""," "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"953a043c-dd33-4cce-b31c-6c99675a4181","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2c0de414-f0f6-4fd4-a804-9a9ba92b16ae","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffakXmF3fLrtyZ7Ncktp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019StuPcBeur1335jcNQDw2q","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit equipment choice\ncategory: [circuit-training, equipment, machines, free-weights, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Equip Circuits With Free Weights and Pulleys, Not Multi-Station Machines; Use a Spotter When Machines Start You in the Weakest Position\n\n**What it is** — For circuits, the authors rank equipment by cost and function. Where cost matters, \"specialised variable and non-variable multi-purpose machines have to be regarded as the lowest priority,\" and \"a few inexpensive 'hi-lo' pulley machines would be a more than adequate substitute. In fact, the latter offer a level of three-dimensional variety that other machines cannot emulate.\" \"A well chosen collection of free weights, benches, racks and pulley machines, wedded with a skipping surface, padded aerobics area, bouncing boards and a few basic fixed cycles, offers virtually everything that the most impressive multi-station machines offer at a fraction of the price.\" \"Free weights are generally far more effective than any variable and non-variable 'isotonic' and isokinetic machines in developing strength and power\" (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). Against machines: they \"constrain each limb to move in set two-dimensional patterns\"; \"it would require at least seven different machines to train the muscles involved in the basic movement of cleaning a barbell from the ground to the shoulders. It requires at least three machines to offer the training benefits of the basic squat\"; and \"many of these machines compel one to begin the required movement in the most vulnerable and biomechanically weakest joint position with little possibility for pre-stretching,\" naming \"most bench press, seated press, seated leg press, leg extension and 'pec deck' machines\" (PDF pp. 314-315 / printed pp. 303-304, PDF p. 315 checked against the page image). The one machine advantage: \"the load can be changed more rapidly than on a barbell,\" valuable when a mixed-strength group moves \"against the clock,\" and pulley machines share it (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are those above: free weights and pulleys allow full-range, three-dimensional movement that conditions \"the proprioceptive mechanisms,\" and machines start many movements where the joint is weakest with no pre-stretch (PDF pp. 314-315). On safety and space they call the difference \"minimal\" and \"inconsequential\" (PDF p. 314).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a circuit room or choosing stations on a budget. When machines are used in a timed circuit, the authors advise that \"a training partner hand you the grips of the machine in your strongest position or stabilise you before you begin to move,\" and \"Contrary to what manufacturers may state, one needs a 'spotter' or training partner to guide you safely and efficiently through any circuit\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred). Inference: the authors' seven-machines-for-a-clean point is a cost-and-transfer argument for the same free-weight core.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The machine counts (seven for a clean, three for a squat) are the authors' estimates, not measurements. The claim that free weights are \"far more effective\" for strength and power is stated without a study. Machines keep a role the authors name elsewhere: limited, specific use \"during the earlier stages of general physical preparation or rehabilitation\" (said of isokinetic machines, PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300). Spotting every station of a circuit is often impractical in a large group; the authors do not say how to staff it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a small school or club can build \"entire dumbbell circuits or pulley machine circuits\" with \"a few benches, racks and floor mats,\" set up \"indoors or outdoors, on land or in water\" (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (4.2.7.1, the general machine critique), [[heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use]], [[claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined]], [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2530,"cache_read_input_tokens":331365,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2530,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019StuPcBeur1335jcNQDw2q":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit equipment choice\ncategory: [circuit-training, equipment, machines, free-weights, safety]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Equip Circuits With Free Weights and Pulleys, Not Multi-Station Machines; Use a Spotter When Machines Start You in the Weakest Position\n\n**What it is** — For circuits, the authors rank equipment by cost and function. Where cost matters, \"specialised variable and non-variable multi-purpose machines have to be regarded as the lowest priority,\" and \"a few inexpensive 'hi-lo' pulley machines would be a more than adequate substitute. In fact, the latter offer a level of three-dimensional variety that other machines cannot emulate.\" \"A well chosen collection of free weights, benches, racks and pulley machines, wedded with a skipping surface, padded aerobics area, bouncing boards and a few basic fixed cycles, offers virtually everything that the most impressive multi-station machines offer at a fraction of the price.\" \"Free weights are generally far more effective than any variable and non-variable 'isotonic' and isokinetic machines in developing strength and power\" (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). Against machines: they \"constrain each limb to move in set two-dimensional patterns\"; \"it would require at least seven different machines to train the muscles involved in the basic movement of cleaning a barbell from the ground to the shoulders. It requires at least three machines to offer the training benefits of the basic squat\"; and \"many of these machines compel one to begin the required movement in the most vulnerable and biomechanically weakest joint position with little possibility for pre-stretching,\" naming \"most bench press, seated press, seated leg press, leg extension and 'pec deck' machines\" (PDF pp. 314-315 / printed pp. 303-304, PDF p. 315 checked against the page image). The one machine advantage: \"the load can be changed more rapidly than on a barbell,\" valuable when a mixed-strength group moves \"against the clock,\" and pulley machines share it (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are those above: free weights and pulleys allow full-range, three-dimensional movement that conditions \"the proprioceptive mechanisms,\" and machines start many movements where the joint is weakest with no pre-stretch (PDF pp. 314-315). On safety and space they call the difference \"minimal\" and \"inconsequential\" (PDF p. 314).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a circuit room or choosing stations on a budget. When machines are used in a timed circuit, the authors advise that \"a training partner hand you the grips of the machine in your strongest position or stabilise you before you begin to move,\" and \"Contrary to what manufacturers may state, one needs a 'spotter' or training partner to guide you safely and efficiently through any circuit\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred). Inference: the authors' seven-machines-for-a-clean point is a cost-and-transfer argument for the same free-weight core.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The machine counts (seven for a clean, three for a squat) are the authors' estimates, not measurements. The claim that free weights are \"far more effective\" for strength and power is stated without a study. Machines keep a role the authors name elsewhere: limited, specific use \"during the earlier stages of general physical preparation or rehabilitation\" (said of isokinetic machines, PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300). Spotting every station of a circuit is often impractical in a large group; the authors do not say how to staff it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a small school or club can build \"entire dumbbell circuits or pulley machine circuits\" with \"a few benches, racks and floor mats,\" set up \"indoors or outdoors, on land or in water\" (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (4.2.7.1, the general machine critique), [[heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use]], [[claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined]], [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffakEqm9YgA7Lno2Wcop","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"18b00cc0-a141-4009-b9c8-55ddec5c7713","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:00:54.732Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
