achines offer at a fraction of the price.\" \"Free weights are generally far more effective than any variable and non-variable 'isotonic' and isokinetic machines in developing strength and power\" (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). Against machines: they \"constrain each limb to move in set two-dimensional patterns\"; \"it would require at least seven different machines to train the muscles involved in the basic movement of cleaning a barbell from the ground to the shoulders. It requires at least three machines to offer the training benefits of the basic squat\"; and \"many of these machines compel one to begin the required movement in the most vulnerable and biomechanically weakest joint position with little possibility for pre-stretching,\" naming \"most bench press, seated press, seated leg press, leg extension and 'pec deck' machines\" (PDF pp. 314-315 / printed pp. 303-304, PDF p. 315 checked against the page image). The one machine advantage: \"the load can be changed more rapidly than on a barbell,\" valuable when a mixed-strength group moves \"against the clock,\" and pulley machines share it (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasons are those above: free weights and pulleys allow full-range, three-dimensional movement that conditions \"the proprioceptive mechanisms,\" and machines start many movements where the joint is weakest with no pre-stretch (PDF pp. 314-315). On safety and space they call the difference \"minimal\" and \"inconsequential\" (PDF p. 314).\n\n**When to apply** — Setting up a circuit room or choosing stations on a budget. When machines are used in a timed circuit, the authors advise that \"a training partner hand you the grips of the machine in your strongest position or stabilise you before you begin to move,\" and \"Contrary to what manufacturers may state, one needs a 'spotter' or training partner to guide you safely and efficiently through any circuit\" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred). Inference: the authors' seven-machines-for-a-clean point is a cost-and-transfer argument for the same free-weight core.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The machine counts (seven for a clean, three for a squat) are the authors' estimates, not measurements. The claim that free weights are \"far more effective\" for strength and power is stated without a study. Machines keep a role the authors name elsewhere: limited, specific use \"during the earlier stages of general physical preparation or rehabilitation\" (said of isokinetic machines, PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300). Spotting every station of a circuit is often impractical in a large group; the authors do not say how to staff it.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: a small school or club can build \"entire dumbbell circuits or pulley machine circuits\" with \"a few benches, racks and floor mats,\" set up \"indoors or outdoors, on land or in water\" (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (4.2.7.1, the general machine critique), [[heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use]], [[claim--nfr-machine-marketing-claims-examined]], [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"18b00cc0-a141-4009-b9c8-55ddec5c7713","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
