{"parentUuid":"165b48b6-ff51-43c3-9bb1-80bc84034b07","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffanL6KqDr55NhDCcB6L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6313,"cache_read_input_tokens":333895,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6313,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":1083,"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffan2fptrY64Vg49Knc2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"18fdbdab-2659-432c-9951-82d1d9bfcb0a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:02.463Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"18fdbdab-2659-432c-9951-82d1d9bfcb0a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffanL6KqDr55NhDCcB6L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01M3NpJ2m2ZCvvm1cWS9QPTz","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit layout variants\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, group-training, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuit Layout Variants: A Two-Station Circuit Is a Superset, and Circuits Can Backtrack, Stagger, Detour, Pyramid or Strip\n\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a systems approach adapted from applied mathematics\" to circuits. \"The traditional approach requires one to proceed from starting station to terminal station for a certain number of sets, without backtracking, omitting stations or diverting to extra equipment\"; \"The more generalised approach includes the possibility of backtracking, omitting stations, altering the sequence of stations, diverting to other equipment, using different speeds of movement or time periods on each station, changing the exercise pattern at each station, and changing the types of muscle contraction at each station\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309, checked against the page image). The variants, each shown in a figure checked against the page images (PDF pp. 320-322 / printed pp. 309-311):\n- **Superset = two-station circuit.** \"a circuit with two stations is exactly the same as a superset with two exercises (Fig 5.29).\"\n- **Retrophase circuit.** \"backtrack by one station for every two stations completed\" (Fig 5.30); backtracking more \"may cause confusion if there is a large group.\"\n- **Dual-set retrophase and sequenced supersets.** \"complete two sets at each station before moving to the next station, then backtrack to complete the remaining two sets\" (Fig 5.31b); or superset A and B \"before going on to superset exercises C and D\" (Fig 5.31a).\n- **Staggered or zig-zag circuit.** Lets clients \"progress through portions of a circuit easily without obstructing others\"; some do the inner circuit, some the outer, advanced clients \"zigzag through the entire circuit,\" with aerobic stations in the middle of the floor (Fig 5.32).\n- **Detour circuit.** Clients \"exit at a particular point to perform a sub-circuit\" of free weights, aerobic, skill, flexibility, running, plyometric or water exercises elsewhere (Fig 5.33).\n- **Other variants** (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311): repetitions to failure at each station; \"pyramid circuits\" in which load rises \"from 40-80% of 1RM\" and reps fall \"e.g. from 10 to 4\" with each lap; \"stripping circuits\" that pyramid up to \"a 6RM or 10RM on each station, then progressively strip the load down,\" generally \"about 10kg each time\"; and variable-repetition or variable-interval sets. Aqualand circuits mix land and water stations (Figs 5.21-5.23, PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated purposes: more variety \"thereby diminishing the likelihood of stagnation and boredom\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309), less crowding at stations (staggered circuits), and access to equipment outside the circuit (detours). For water stations they give a physical reason: \"the resistance offered by water increases with the velocity of movement (actually, the square of the velocity)\" and \"is also directly proportional to the area of the limb pushing against the water\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**When to apply** — Running a large group through limited equipment, or adding variety to a circuit block without changing its exercises. The superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes: a two-exercise superset can be planned and progressed with the same rules as a circuit. Inference: a staggered layout suits a team lift where position groups share racks, since an inner and outer loop keep groups from queuing.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The variants are design options, not tested training methods, hence `untested`. Retrophase and staggered layouts add complexity; the authors themselves warn that backtracking \"may cause confusion\" with large groups. Stripping and failure-based circuits raise fatigue; the authors say working to failure \"makes it highly advisable that you have a 'spotting' partner nearby\" (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311). For aqualand circuits they note it is \"inadvisable to have water dripping from wet athletes on the floor and onto indoor apparatus\" and that pool water must be changed regularly (PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own stripping set: pyramid \"from 10 repetitions with 60kg up to 6 repetitions with 100kg,\" then the partner strips the bar to 90 kg, 80 kg and 70 kg, with sets to failure at each load (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (the same velocity-squared drag relation, for parachutes), [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6313,"cache_read_input_tokens":333895,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6313,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01M3NpJ2m2ZCvvm1cWS9QPTz":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit layout variants\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, group-training, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuit Layout Variants: A Two-Station Circuit Is a Superset, and Circuits Can Backtrack, Stagger, Detour, Pyramid or Strip\n\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a systems approach adapted from applied mathematics\" to circuits. \"The traditional approach requires one to proceed from starting station to terminal station for a certain number of sets, without backtracking, omitting stations or diverting to extra equipment\"; \"The more generalised approach includes the possibility of backtracking, omitting stations, altering the sequence of stations, diverting to other equipment, using different speeds of movement or time periods on each station, changing the exercise pattern at each station, and changing the types of muscle contraction at each station\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309, checked against the page image). The variants, each shown in a figure checked against the page images (PDF pp. 320-322 / printed pp. 309-311):\n- **Superset = two-station circuit.** \"a circuit with two stations is exactly the same as a superset with two exercises (Fig 5.29).\"\n- **Retrophase circuit.** \"backtrack by one station for every two stations completed\" (Fig 5.30); backtracking more \"may cause confusion if there is a large group.\"\n- **Dual-set retrophase and sequenced supersets.** \"complete two sets at each station before moving to the next station, then backtrack to complete the remaining two sets\" (Fig 5.31b); or superset A and B \"before going on to superset exercises C and D\" (Fig 5.31a).\n- **Staggered or zig-zag circuit.** Lets clients \"progress through portions of a circuit easily without obstructing others\"; some do the inner circuit, some the outer, advanced clients \"zigzag through the entire circuit,\" with aerobic stations in the middle of the floor (Fig 5.32).\n- **Detour circuit.** Clients \"exit at a particular point to perform a sub-circuit\" of free weights, aerobic, skill, flexibility, running, plyometric or water exercises elsewhere (Fig 5.33).\n- **Other variants** (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311): repetitions to failure at each station; \"pyramid circuits\" in which load rises \"from 40-80% of 1RM\" and reps fall \"e.g. from 10 to 4\" with each lap; \"stripping circuits\" that pyramid up to \"a 6RM or 10RM on each station, then progressively strip the load down,\" generally \"about 10kg each time\"; and variable-repetition or variable-interval sets. Aqualand circuits mix land and water stations (Figs 5.21-5.23, PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated purposes: more variety \"thereby diminishing the likelihood of stagnation and boredom\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309), less crowding at stations (staggered circuits), and access to equipment outside the circuit (detours). For water stations they give a physical reason: \"the resistance offered by water increases with the velocity of movement (actually, the square of the velocity)\" and \"is also directly proportional to the area of the limb pushing against the water\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**When to apply** — Running a large group through limited equipment, or adding variety to a circuit block without changing its exercises. The superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes: a two-exercise superset can be planned and progressed with the same rules as a circuit. Inference: a staggered layout suits a team lift where position groups share racks, since an inner and outer loop keep groups from queuing.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The variants are design options, not tested training methods, hence `untested`. Retrophase and staggered layouts add complexity; the authors themselves warn that backtracking \"may cause confusion\" with large groups. Stripping and failure-based circuits raise fatigue; the authors say working to failure \"makes it highly advisable that you have a 'spotting' partner nearby\" (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311). For aqualand circuits they note it is \"inadvisable to have water dripping from wet athletes on the floor and onto indoor apparatus\" and that pool water must be changed regularly (PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own stripping set: pyramid \"from 10 repetitions with 60kg up to 6 repetitions with 100kg,\" then the partner strips the bar to 90 kg, 80 kg and 70 kg, with sets to failure at each load (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (the same velocity-squared drag relation, for parachutes), [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffan2fptrY64Vg49Knc2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9404e338-ce90-45f9-b7db-65059706595f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:18.411Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9404e338-ce90-45f9-b7db-65059706595f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M3NpJ2m2ZCvvm1cWS9QPTz","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"177de8e6-5812-47a1-a280-805364e0b976","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:18.526Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit layout variants\ncategory: [circuit-training, programming, group-training, variation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuit Layout Variants: A Two-Station Circuit Is a Superset, and Circuits Can Backtrack, Stagger, Detour, Pyramid or Strip\n\n**What it is** — The authors propose \"a systems approach adapted from applied mathematics\" to circuits. \"The traditional approach requires one to proceed from starting station to terminal station for a certain number of sets, without backtracking, omitting stations or diverting to extra equipment\"; \"The more generalised approach includes the possibility of backtracking, omitting stations, altering the sequence of stations, diverting to other equipment, using different speeds of movement or time periods on each station, changing the exercise pattern at each station, and changing the types of muscle contraction at each station\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309, checked against the page image). The variants, each shown in a figure checked against the page images (PDF pp. 320-322 / printed pp. 309-311):\n- **Superset = two-station circuit.** \"a circuit with two stations is exactly the same as a superset with two exercises (Fig 5.29).\"\n- **Retrophase circuit.** \"backtrack by one station for every two stations completed\" (Fig 5.30); backtracking more \"may cause confusion if there is a large group.\"\n- **Dual-set retrophase and sequenced supersets.** \"complete two sets at each station before moving to the next station, then backtrack to complete the remaining two sets\" (Fig 5.31b); or superset A and B \"before going on to superset exercises C and D\" (Fig 5.31a).\n- **Staggered or zig-zag circuit.** Lets clients \"progress through portions of a circuit easily without obstructing others\"; some do the inner circuit, some the outer, advanced clients \"zigzag through the entire circuit,\" with aerobic stations in the middle of the floor (Fig 5.32).\n- **Detour circuit.** Clients \"exit at a particular point to perform a sub-circuit\" of free weights, aerobic, skill, flexibility, running, plyometric or water exercises elsewhere (Fig 5.33).\n- **Other variants** (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311): repetitions to failure at each station; \"pyramid circuits\" in which load rises \"from 40-80% of 1RM\" and reps fall \"e.g. from 10 to 4\" with each lap; \"stripping circuits\" that pyramid up to \"a 6RM or 10RM on each station, then progressively strip the load down,\" generally \"about 10kg each time\"; and variable-repetition or variable-interval sets. Aqualand circuits mix land and water stations (Figs 5.21-5.23, PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated purposes: more variety \"thereby diminishing the likelihood of stagnation and boredom\" (PDF p. 320 / printed p. 309), less crowding at stations (staggered circuits), and access to equipment outside the circuit (detours). For water stations they give a physical reason: \"the resistance offered by water increases with the velocity of movement (actually, the square of the velocity)\" and \"is also directly proportional to the area of the limb pushing against the water\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).\n\n**When to apply** — Running a large group through limited equipment, or adding variety to a circuit block without changing its exercises. The superset equivalence is useful when writing programmes: a two-exercise superset can be planned and progressed with the same rules as a circuit. Inference: a staggered layout suits a team lift where position groups share racks, since an inner and outer loop keep groups from queuing.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The variants are design options, not tested training methods, hence `untested`. Retrophase and staggered layouts add complexity; the authors themselves warn that backtracking \"may cause confusion\" with large groups. Stripping and failure-based circuits raise fatigue; the authors say working to failure \"makes it highly advisable that you have a 'spotting' partner nearby\" (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311). For aqualand circuits they note it is \"inadvisable to have water dripping from wet athletes on the floor and onto indoor apparatus\" and that pool water must be changed regularly (PDF pp. 317-318).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own stripping set: pyramid \"from 10 repetitions with 60kg up to 6 repetitions with 100kg,\" then the partner strips the bar to 90 kg, 80 kg and 70 kg, with sets to failure at each load (PDF p. 322 / printed p. 311, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release]] (the same velocity-squared drag relation, for parachutes), [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9404e338-ce90-45f9-b7db-65059706595f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"177de8e6-5812-47a1-a280-805364e0b976","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffanL6KqDr55NhDCcB6L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015EFMn4jzAr8KqEDLPbbj8k","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit training limits for athletes\ncategory: [circuit-training, special-strength, periodization, gpp]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuits Suit Non-Athletes and the Early Preparatory Phase; They Cannot Build Sport-Specific Strength, and Bigger Circuits Do Less\n\n**What it is** — The authors' closing judgment, italicised in the original: \"all forms of circuit training are largely suited to the average non-athlete or competitive athlete during the early preparatory phase of training.\" \"The constant progression in a circuit from one exercise to another without completing all sets with one exercise to a prescribed maximum number of repetitions before moving to the next exercise does not permit one to adequately develop the different types of sport specific strength. Even with interval circuit training on machines, it is not possible to train with the medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading which is necessary to develop qualities such as muscle hypertrophy, speed-strength, strength-speed, static strength, flexibility-strength, explosive strength and acceleration strength. The length of the interval between successive sets of the same exercise depends on the number of stations in the circuit, so the larger the circuit, the less its ability to significantly develop any of the major sport-specific strength-related qualities\" (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312, checked against the page image). The same page adds a planning rule, also italicised: \"The design of any circuit training programme is incomplete if attention is paid to the single session alone,\" with the long-term plan changing \"type, duration, intensity and complexity of each circuit,\" applying \"the principles of supercompensation and periodisation,\" and ensuring \"that light and heavy circuits alternate, with provision being made for adequate restoration.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is station count: the rest between two sets of the same exercise is set by how many other stations come between them, so a large circuit spaces sets of any one exercise far apart, and the loading pattern needed for each strength quality cannot be met (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312).\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding where circuits sit in an athlete's year: general preparation early in the off-season, group conditioning for non-athletes, or rehab groups, but not the main means for hypertrophy, maximal or explosive strength. If a circuit is used, keep it small so sets of each exercise recur often. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred). Inference: those lifts need the heavy and explosive loading the authors say circuits cannot give, so for Baylor athletes circuits would supplement, not replace, the core lifts.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The same section gives strength-and-power circuits at over 75% of 1RM with no more than 5 reps and about 2 minutes' rest (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304), says \"For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), and lists power, plyometric, speed and muscle-bulking circuits (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). That sits uneasily with \"not possible to train with the medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading,\" unless the qualifier \"on machines\" carries the weight; the text does not say. The authors' Ch1 statement agrees with the limit: interval circuit training \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently to meet the specific needs of strength and speed sports, except possibly during the early off-season\" (PDF p. 91 / printed p. 80, as quoted in [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]]). No study is cited for the station-count argument.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 12-station circuit with one squat station gives about 11 stations of work between squat sets, so the squat cannot be loaded or rested like a strength set; a 2-station superset of squat and row can. The authors give the rule, not this case.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1), [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6313,"cache_read_input_tokens":333895,"output_tokens":3821,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":142},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6313},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3821,"cache_read_input_tokens":333895,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6313,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6313,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015EFMn4jzAr8KqEDLPbbj8k":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit training limits for athletes\ncategory: [circuit-training, special-strength, periodization, gpp]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuits Suit Non-Athletes and the Early Preparatory Phase; They Cannot Build Sport-Specific Strength, and Bigger Circuits Do Less\n\n**What it is** — The authors' closing judgment, italicised in the original: \"all forms of circuit training are largely suited to the average non-athlete or competitive athlete during the early preparatory phase of training.\" \"The constant progression in a circuit from one exercise to another without completing all sets with one exercise to a prescribed maximum number of repetitions before moving to the next exercise does not permit one to adequately develop the different types of sport specific strength. Even with interval circuit training on machines, it is not possible to train with the medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading which is necessary to develop qualities such as muscle hypertrophy, speed-strength, strength-speed, static strength, flexibility-strength, explosive strength and acceleration strength. The length of the interval between successive sets of the same exercise depends on the number of stations in the circuit, so the larger the circuit, the less its ability to significantly develop any of the major sport-specific strength-related qualities\" (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312, checked against the page image). The same page adds a planning rule, also italicised: \"The design of any circuit training programme is incomplete if attention is paid to the single session alone,\" with the long-term plan changing \"type, duration, intensity and complexity of each circuit,\" applying \"the principles of supercompensation and periodisation,\" and ensuring \"that light and heavy circuits alternate, with provision being made for adequate restoration.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is station count: the rest between two sets of the same exercise is set by how many other stations come between them, so a large circuit spaces sets of any one exercise far apart, and the loading pattern needed for each strength quality cannot be met (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312).\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding where circuits sit in an athlete's year: general preparation early in the off-season, group conditioning for non-athletes, or rehab groups, but not the main means for hypertrophy, maximal or explosive strength. If a circuit is used, keep it small so sets of each exercise recur often. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred). Inference: those lifts need the heavy and explosive loading the authors say circuits cannot give, so for Baylor athletes circuits would supplement, not replace, the core lifts.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The same section gives strength-and-power circuits at over 75% of 1RM with no more than 5 reps and about 2 minutes' rest (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304), says \"For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), and lists power, plyometric, speed and muscle-bulking circuits (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). That sits uneasily with \"not possible to train with the medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading,\" unless the qualifier \"on machines\" carries the weight; the text does not say. The authors' Ch1 statement agrees with the limit: interval circuit training \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently to meet the specific needs of strength and speed sports, except possibly during the early off-season\" (PDF p. 91 / printed p. 80, as quoted in [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]]). No study is cited for the station-count argument.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 12-station circuit with one squat station gives about 11 stations of work between squat sets, so the squat cannot be loaded or rested like a strength set; a 2-station superset of squat and row can. The authors give the rule, not this case.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1), [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]]\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffan2fptrY64Vg49Knc2","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5e1f77ee-b32f-48c3-958d-ee91863f2949","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:32.847Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5e1f77ee-b32f-48c3-958d-ee91863f2949","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015EFMn4jzAr8KqEDLPbbj8k","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"598bd135-bc11-4c81-87d8-4f0de6fd83bc","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:01:32.944Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: circuit training limits for athletes\ncategory: [circuit-training, special-strength, periodization, gpp]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Circuits Suit Non-Athletes and the Early Preparatory Phase; They Cannot Build Sport-Specific Strength, and Bigger Circuits Do Less\n\n**What it is** — The authors' closing judgment, italicised in the original: \"all forms of circuit training are largely suited to the average non-athlete or competitive athlete during the early preparatory phase of training.\" \"The constant progression in a circuit from one exercise to another without completing all sets with one exercise to a prescribed maximum number of repetitions before moving to the next exercise does not permit one to adequately develop the different types of sport specific strength. Even with interval circuit training on machines, it is not possible to train with the medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading which is necessary to develop qualities such as muscle hypertrophy, speed-strength, strength-speed, static strength, flexibility-strength, explosive strength and acceleration strength. The length of the interval between successive sets of the same exercise depends on the number of stations in the circuit, so the larger the circuit, the less its ability to significantly develop any of the major sport-specific strength-related qualities\" (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312, checked against the page image). The same page adds a planning rule, also italicised: \"The design of any circuit training programme is incomplete if attention is paid to the single session alone,\" with the long-term plan changing \"type, duration, intensity and complexity of each circuit,\" applying \"the principles of supercompensation and periodisation,\" and ensuring \"that light and heavy circuits alternate, with provision being made for adequate restoration.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is station count: the rest between two sets of the same exercise is set by how many other stations come between them, so a large circuit spaces sets of any one exercise far apart, and the loading pattern needed for each strength quality cannot be met (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312).\n\n**When to apply** — Deciding where circuits sit in an athlete's year: general preparation early in the off-season, group conditioning for non-athletes, or rehab groups, but not the main means for hypertrophy, maximal or explosive strength. If a circuit is used, keep it small so sets of each exercise recur often. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred). Inference: those lifts need the heavy and explosive loading the authors say circuits cannot give, so for Baylor athletes circuits would supplement, not replace, the core lifts.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book.** The same section gives strength-and-power circuits at over 75% of 1RM with no more than 5 reps and about 2 minutes' rest (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304), says \"For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used\" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306), and lists power, plyometric, speed and muscle-bulking circuits (PDF p. 314 / printed p. 303). That sits uneasily with \"not possible to train with the medium heavy, near maximal or explosive loading,\" unless the qualifier \"on machines\" carries the weight; the text does not say. The authors' Ch1 statement agrees with the limit: interval circuit training \"does not enhance strength and power sufficiently to meet the specific needs of strength and speed sports, except possibly during the early off-season\" (PDF p. 91 / printed p. 80, as quoted in [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]]). No study is cited for the station-count argument.\n\n**Example** — Inference: a 12-station circuit with one squat station gives about 11 stations of work between squat sets, so the squat cannot be loaded or rested like a strength set; a 2-station superset of squat and row can. The authors give the rule, not this case.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1), [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[framework--strength-speed-continuum-five-zones]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5e1f77ee-b32f-48c3-958d-ee91863f2949","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
