requestId":"req_011Cffaskyv4BeN9SCCdL97d","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f0535497-3e84-4762-9bb4-4d8b706aa5b5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:22.801Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f0535497-3e84-4762-9bb4-4d8b706aa5b5","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"624a8cec-33fa-4c0a-a445-c72ce718e6ab","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FkYdQgKgSBoNXc4GDPHdgF","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"64ad019a-cacf-45f5-8240-b288a87f9dcd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:22.944Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks.md","oldString":"**Example** — The authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. Inference: for a long jumper,","newString":"**Example** — Inference: the authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. For a long jumper,","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.4.3\"\ntopic: specific correspondence of training effect\ncategory: [specificity, functional-specialisation, special-strength, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specific Correspondence of the Training Effect: Specialisation by System and by Ability, and Four Programming Tasks\n\n**What it is** — \"The principle of specific correspondence points to the need for correspondence between the general effect of the strength work and the specific character of the motor regime of the given sport,\" achieved by \"systematically organising means so that the resulting cumulative effect fully establishes the special strength fitness required at the current stage of the sports training process\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296). The body \"selectively reacts to the dominant motor regime and movement pattern,\" which \"causes local, functional improvement in the fitness qualities of the kinematic chain's motor system (specialisation of systems) and develops qualities which are appropriate to the motor skills of the sport (specialisation of ability).\" Fig 5.17 (verified against the page image, PDF p. 308 / printed p. 297) shows this as two branches from external and internal factors: primary motor regime, then motor specifics of the sports exercise, then \"specialisation according to ability\" (absolute strength, acceleration strength, starting strength, reactive ability, speed of movement, strength-endurance); and pattern of the movement, then external mechanical influences, then \"specialisation according to physiological system,\" then key muscle groups, then the functional muscle complex. The four tasks (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296):\n1. \"Timeous development of the required level of special strength fitness\"\n2. \"Elimination, where necessary, of heterochronological stages which were initially suitable for the intense functional specialisation of the key muscle groups\"\n3. \"Elimination of any lag in the development of strength fitness of individual muscle groups\"\n4. \"Intensifying the development of the essential strength abilities during the stage of advanced sports mastery.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is the selective-adaptation account: the body specialises both locally (systems, key muscle groups) and by ability, so strength work must feed both branches in the right order. They refer back to 2.2.4 for the specialisation account and give no further mechanism here.\n\n**When to apply** — Reviewing whether a strength programme matches the sport at the current stage: list the abilities in the left branch of Fig 5.17 that the sport needs, check the key muscle groups in the right branch, and look for any muscle group lagging behind the rest (task 3). Eric's baseline: \"The dynamic strength index (DSI: CMJ against isometric mid-thigh pull peak force) chooses between plyometric and max-strength emphasis.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 76, Explicit, interview Q21). Inference: DSI is one way to spot the kind of lag task 3 describes, though the baseline flags DSI's cut-offs as unvalidated (Tension 17), and the authors do not name a test.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme and the four tasks are stated, not tested; no study is cited on these pages. Task 2 refers to \"heterochronological stages\" without defining them here; see the heterochronicity entry in Related. The ability list in Fig 5.17 is the authors' own taxonomy of strength qualities; it is not exhaustive (it omits, for example, \"flexibility-strength,\" which they list in 5.6, PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312). This entry overlaps the 4.4 functional-specialisation flowchart ([[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]]); the new material here is the system-vs-ability split and the four tasks.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. Inference: for a long jumper, the left branch would stress reactive ability and starting strength, the right branch the hip and knee extensors and plantarflexors as key muscle groups, and task 3 would target whichever of those lagged on testing.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]] (4.4), [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[framework--dynamometric-and-functional-profile-for-weak-link-identification]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":6,"newStart":25,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," **When NOT to apply** — The scheme and the four tasks are stated, not tested; no study is cited on these pages. Task 2 refers to \"heterochronological stages\" without defining them here; see the heterochronicity entry in Related. The ability list in Fig 5.17 is the authors' own taxonomy of strength qualities; it is not exhaustive (it omits, for example, \"flexibility-strength,\" which they list in 5.6, PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312). This entry overlaps the 4.4 functional-specialisation flowchart ([[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]]); the new material here is the system-vs-ability split and the four tasks."," ","-**Example** — The authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. Inference: for a long jumper, the left branch would stress reactive ability and starting strength, the right branch the hip and knee extensors and plantarflexors as key muscle groups, and task 3 would target whichever of those lagged on testing.","+**Example** — Inference: the authors give no worked example in 5.4.3. For a long jumper, the left branch would stress reactive ability and starting strength, the right branch the hip and knee extensors and plantarflexors as key muscle groups, and task 3 would target whichever of those lagged on testing."," "," **Related** — [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]] (4.4), [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[framework--dynamometric-and-functional-profile-for-weak-link-identification]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]]"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f0535497-3e84-4762-9bb4-4d8b706aa5b5","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
