{"parentUuid":"7fe0f51b-01fc-474b-98b5-5bd153f2bb51","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cuz7XeFcJxLrsAtXU5yt2d","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a0bad91f-3db8-42b9-9274-5800c849ca92","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:34.631Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means.md","oldString":"Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block.","newString":"Inference: plan an unloading week between blocks, as the study did, and test after it rather than on the last day of a hard block.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3\"\ntopic: depth jumps after resistance block\ncategory: [plyometrics, sequencing, recovery, explosive-strength, weightlifting]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Deniskin (1976): A Resistance Stage, Then Two Depth-Jump Stages, With Complete Rest Between Them\n\n**What it is** — \"qualified athletes modelled different variants of weightlifting pre-competition training under laboratory conditions (Deniskin, 1976).\" The test movement was \"A leg extension movement involving the lifting of a 1RM ... starting with an initial knee angle of 90°.\" Stage 1 was \"intensive work with resistance, the purpose of which was to increase explosive strength.\" \"Only depth-jumps were used during the second and third stages.\" Two depth-jump stages were used because \"a preliminary investigation ... showed that the rise in functional state achieved after one stage is unstable.\" \"The fourth stage served as a control and served only to test performance.\" \"The subjects were given a complete rest between stages to enhance supercompensation\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Fig 5.14 (verified against the page image) lays the stages out by week: resistance weeks 1-7, rest week 8, depth jumps weeks 9-15, rest week 16, depth jumps weeks 17-21, rest week 22, testing weeks 23-24. It plots Fmax (kgf), tmax (s, time to Fmax), starting strength (Ss) and explosive strength (Se). Read by eye from the image: Fmax rises from about 103 to about 110 kgf in stage 1 and reaches about 125 kgf by stage 4; tmax falls from about 0.42 s to about 0.27-0.32 s; Ss and Se both rise through stages 1-3, and Ss falls back from its stage-3 high at the stage-4 test.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' conclusion: \"the plyometric method produces an additional, significant increase in the level of explosive strength that was first attained with resistance training,\" which shows \"the use of these means in two appropriate stages: the first stage, which improves explosive strength, and the second stage, which stabilises the level of special fitness.\" On rest: \"There was a significant increase in all the control indicators after a period of complete rest following the first and second stages. This emphasizes that rest may be used as a training means. This is not a paradox, because growth and maintenance processes take place predominantly during sleep and other forms of passive or active rest\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). That sentence is the authors' mechanism; they cite nothing for it.\n\n**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript): Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first. Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after 7 weeks of resistance work, a rest week, then 7 and 5 weeks of depth jumps with a rest week after each, Fmax in the leg extension had risen from about 103 to about 125 kgf at the stage-4 test (Fig 5.14, PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290; values read from the image).\n\n**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (ES, plyometrics and heavy resistance, same research group), [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[framework--reactivity-coefficient-and-depth-jump-test]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' conclusion: \"the plyometric method produces an additional, significant increase in the level of explosive strength that was first attained with resistance training,\" which shows \"the use of these means in two appropriate stages: the first stage, which improves explosive strength, and the second stage, which stabilises the level of special fitness.\" On rest: \"There was a significant increase in all the control indicators after a period of complete rest following the first and second stages. This emphasizes that rest may be used as a training means. This is not a paradox, because growth and maintenance processes take place predominantly during sleep and other forms of passive or active rest\" (PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). That sentence is the authors' mechanism; they cite nothing for it."," ","-**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Plan an unloading week between blocks and test after it, not on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript): Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first. Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one.","+**When to apply** — Planning a heavy-resistance block followed by a plyometric block, as in the authors' second sequencing rule (\"Strong-acting plyometric methods should be done after strength exercises, but not before\"; PDF p. 301 / printed p. 290). Inference: plan an unloading week between blocks, as the study did, and test after it rather than on the last day of a hard block. Eric's baseline (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 151, Inferred, transcript): Recovery capacity must be \"earned\" by training hard enough first. Inference: the planned rest weeks here are the opposite case to the baseline's warning against under-training: rest comes after a hard block, not in place of one."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The subjects were \"qualified athletes\" in a laboratory model of weightlifting preparation; no sample size, statistics or control group are given, and the \"control\" stage 4 is a test period, not a control group. The test was a 1RM leg extension from 90° of knee flexion, so the results are for that movement. \"complete rest\" between stages is not defined (passive or active, how long beyond what Fig 5.14 shows as one week). The next paragraph on the same page, about electrostimulation combined with plyometrics and heavy resistance, is a separate finding already atomized in [[heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities]] (4.2.1.6). The figure values above are approximate readings from a printed chart."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7fe0f51b-01fc-474b-98b5-5bd153f2bb51","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a0bad91f-3db8-42b9-9274-5800c849ca92","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffatqJu8zB568NaYGBNw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QM6gwCZ2JeBScK1v5gXkSE","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md","old_string":"When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small. Cap a heavy-strength emphasis well before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, and change the means.","new_string":"When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small, as the authors advise. Inference: change the means of a heavy-strength emphasis before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, rather than waiting for the plateau."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":610,"cache_read_input_tokens":347654,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":610,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01QM6gwCZ2JeBScK1v5gXkSE":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md","old_string":"When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small. Cap a heavy-strength emphasis well before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, and change the means.","new_string":"When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small, as the authors advise. Inference: change the means of a heavy-strength emphasis before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, rather than waiting for the plateau."}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffatTxkq3hGkBVkBZwhG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fc76e2a7-cf07-4d3c-bcba-0a8b765290ab","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:36.686Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fc76e2a7-cf07-4d3c-bcba-0a8b765290ab","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QM6gwCZ2JeBScK1v5gXkSE","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ac1362d4-39be-4c67-b67b-37ba4e512332","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:36.802Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md","oldString":"When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small. Cap a heavy-strength emphasis well before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, and change the means.","newString":"When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small, as the authors advise. Inference: change the means of a heavy-strength emphasis before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, rather than waiting for the plateau.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.1\"\ntopic: heavy strength volume interference\ncategory: [interference, speed-strength, technique, volume, special-strength]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks\n\n**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"prolonged, heavy workloads slow the contraction speed of trained muscles\"; Filinov, \"excessively heavy strength loads diminish the force and speed of boxers' punches\"; Deniskin (1976), \"prolonged use of high volume strength loads diminishes the power and speed of weightlifters, high jumpers and triple jumpers.\" On dose: \"speed-strength and strength-speed cease to increase after a certain volume of heavy strength training has been executed. Deniskin observed this phenomenon after 18 weeks (54 workouts) of strength training. Other studies have also noted the same phenomenon after 50-60 training sessions or approximately 12 weeks of training.\" On technique, Siff writes (the passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person): \"research by Verkhoshansky and colleagues has established that excessive maximum strength training can impair speed-strength and technical skill in boxers (Verkhoshansky, 1977). It can also lead to a deterioration of several months' duration in the technique of weightlifters and javelin throwers\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation for the technique loss: \"The negative influence of heavy strength training on sports technique may be due substantially to deterioration of the speed-strength qualities of the neuromuscular system,\" and \"heavy training loads reduce one's ability to control movements, primarily of their most complex technical phases\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). For the plateau they give a dose argument only: \"there are certain volumes and durations of strength training that are essential for achieving an optimum training effect. Exceeding these optimal values renders any further increase in training volume not only unproductive, but detrimental.\" No cellular mechanism is given.\n\n**When to apply** — When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small. Cap a heavy-strength emphasis well before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, and change the means. The authors also warn against prescribing heavy physical and technical work together for highly qualified athletes: a \"literal interpretation of this concept of unified training by the concurrent prescription of physical and technical training leads to serious error,\" because it \"contradicts the well-known need for the athlete to practise technical skills when he is fresh\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred). Inference: the authors' warning is about volume, not intensity, which fits that rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 30% figure is \"as much as,\" with no study named for it. The Kotz and Filinov reports carry no dates or data here. The 12-18 week figures are for \"heavy strength training\" in speed-strength athletes; the authors do not say the same plateau applies to novices or to lower-volume strength work. The section does not argue against heavy strength work in general: it calls it necessary for absolute strength and general conditioning, and criticises only extensive use during specialised phases. The authors' wider complaint (PDF p. 302) that training means are applied in a \"rubber-stamp way\" in which \"Virtually the only factors that change are the load and overall volume\" is an opinion, not a finding.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Deniskin saw speed-strength and strength-speed stop rising after 18 weeks (54 workouts) of strength training; other studies saw it after 50-60 sessions, about 12 weeks (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).\n\n**Related** — [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]] (swimmers' explosive strength fell under concurrent endurance and strength work; agreement on interference, different cause), [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]] (stiffness and loss of explosive strength after heavy blocks), [[claim--strength-detraining-and-retention-timelines]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]] (technical skill needs a rested athlete)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' explanation for the technique loss: \"The negative influence of heavy strength training on sports technique may be due substantially to deterioration of the speed-strength qualities of the neuromuscular system,\" and \"heavy training loads reduce one's ability to control movements, primarily of their most complex technical phases\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). For the plateau they give a dose argument only: \"there are certain volumes and durations of strength training that are essential for achieving an optimum training effect. Exceeding these optimal values renders any further increase in training volume not only unproductive, but detrimental.\" No cellular mechanism is given."," ","-**When to apply** — When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small. Cap a heavy-strength emphasis well before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, and change the means. The authors also warn against prescribing heavy physical and technical work together for highly qualified athletes: a \"literal interpretation of this concept of unified training by the concurrent prescription of physical and technical training leads to serious error,\" because it \"contradicts the well-known need for the athlete to practise technical skills when he is fresh\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred). Inference: the authors' warning is about volume, not intensity, which fits that rule.","+**When to apply** — When a specialised speed or jump phase is running, keep high-volume heavy strength work out of it or small, as the authors advise. Inference: change the means of a heavy-strength emphasis before the 12-18 week mark the authors report, rather than waiting for the plateau. The authors also warn against prescribing heavy physical and technical work together for highly qualified athletes: a \"literal interpretation of this concept of unified training by the concurrent prescription of physical and technical training leads to serious error,\" because it \"contradicts the well-known need for the athlete to practise technical skills when he is fresh\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is \"maintain intensity, reduce volume\" rather than trying to build strength while managing a full competition schedule.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred). Inference: the authors' warning is about volume, not intensity, which fits that rule."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The 30% figure is \"as much as,\" with no study named for it. The Kotz and Filinov reports carry no dates or data here. The 12-18 week figures are for \"heavy strength training\" in speed-strength athletes; the authors do not say the same plateau applies to novices or to lower-volume strength work. The section does not argue against heavy strength work in general: it calls it necessary for absolute strength and general conditioning, and criticises only extensive use during specialised phases. The authors' wider complaint (PDF p. 302) that training means are applied in a \"rubber-stamp way\" in which \"Virtually the only factors that change are the load and overall volume\" is an opinion, not a finding."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fc76e2a7-cf07-4d3c-bcba-0a8b765290ab","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ac1362d4-39be-4c67-b67b-37ba4e512332","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffatqJu8zB568NaYGBNw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011EYtPbmMGQ7nUg8p9fBSys","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","old_string":"After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading.","new_string":"After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":610,"cache_read_input_tokens":347654,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":610,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011EYtPbmMGQ7nUg8p9fBSys":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","old_string":"After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading.","new_string":"After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading."}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CffatTxkq3hGkBVkBZwhG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"daddca0c-a1d4-4fbb-9b36-91bacfc2b9c7","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:38.733Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"daddca0c-a1d4-4fbb-9b36-91bacfc2b9c7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011EYtPbmMGQ7nUg8p9fBSys","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ecea9adb-d99c-4d01-af8a-902440011a3e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:38.830Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","oldString":"After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading.","newString":"After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.3.3\"\ntopic: restoration after concentrated loading\ncategory: [concentrated-loading, delayed-training-effect, recovery, block-periodization, overtraining]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# After a Concentrated Block, Unload and Wait: Gains Commonly Appear After a 1-3 Week Latency\n\n**What it is** — The authors warn: \"The system is not without its dangers, since concentrated loading is very stressful and, besides increasing the potential for overtraining, it can decrease motor qualities such as speed-strength. After a phase of emphasis on heavy strength training, there may be an increase in muscle stiffness and a loss of explosive strength, sporting skill or speed.\" Their rule, italicised in the original: \"a diminished loading and restorative period has to be observed after a given concentrated loading phase to allow the body to supercompensate to the desired level of preparedness.\" The timing: \"The gains elicited, for instance, by 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training, may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks, so it is essential for the athlete not to lose patience and hasten into the next concentrated phase without adequate restoration. Not only does premature imposition of concentrated loading suppress the delayed training effect of the previous stage, but it also lowers the effectiveness of future training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the conjugate sequence system is based on exploitation of the delayed training effect\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), so the next block must start once that effect has appeared, not before. No physiological mechanism is given in this section. Inference: the authors frame the restorative period as supercompensation; the two-factor (fitness-fatigue) reading in this library would describe the same pattern as fatigue clearing to reveal fitness, and the book does not choose between them here.\n\n**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]] (4.1.2, the delayed training effect), [[case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means]] (rest weeks between stages), [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' reason: \"the conjugate sequence system is based on exploitation of the delayed training effect\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294), so the next block must start once that effect has appeared, not before. No physiological mechanism is given in this section. Inference: the authors frame the restorative period as supercompensation; the two-factor (fitness-fatigue) reading in this library would describe the same pattern as fatigue clearing to reveal fitness, and the book does not choose between them here."," ","-**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block, and test or judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block.","+**When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block."," "," **When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"daddca0c-a1d4-4fbb-9b36-91bacfc2b9c7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ecea9adb-d99c-4d01-af8a-902440011a3e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffatqJu8zB568NaYGBNw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0133dK7ypSQfyq7zeXUfV5hu","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md","old_string":"Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference:","new_string":"Ordering any circuit so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Inference: the same rule can pair exercises in a superset within a strength session. Inference:"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":610,"cache_read_input_tokens":347654,"output_tokens":1561,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":378},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":610},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1561,"cache_read_input_tokens":347654,"cache_creation_input_tokens":610,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":610,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0133dK7ypSQfyq7zeXUfV5hu":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md","old_string":"Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference:","new_string":"Ordering any circuit so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Inference: the same rule can pair exercises in a superset within a strength session. Inference:"}},"apiBlockIndex":5,"requestId":"req_011CffatTxkq3hGkBVkBZwhG","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8cdec34d-042c-47f1-8e5a-5874f2a51609","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:40.859Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8cdec34d-042c-47f1-8e5a-5874f2a51609","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d50c13ce-05db-402d-a20c-8f3393d035f7","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0133dK7ypSQfyq7zeXUfV5hu","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"4a057bc6-4dbb-4c60-99d5-8d073b2240d4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:02:40.972Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing.md","oldString":"Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference:","newString":"Ordering any circuit so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Inference: the same rule can pair exercises in a superset within a strength session. Inference:","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"5.6\"\ntopic: station order by active rest\ncategory: [circuit-training, exercise-order, recovery, programming]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Order Circuit Stations by the Setchinov Phenomenon: Alternate Upper and Lower, Front and Back, Agonist and Antagonist\n\n**What it is** — \"The effectiveness of any circuit may be enhanced by taking cognizance of the Setchinov phenomenon, which encourages restoration by means of diverting activity performed between exercises. For example, this may entail exercise of upper body muscles alternating with exercise of unfatigued muscles of the legs. In applying this principle to circuit training, upper body stations should then alternately exercise upper and lower body muscles, back and front muscles, or agonistic and antagonistic muscles\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308, checked against the page image). The authors' layouts (Figs 5.25-5.28, verified against the page image, PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308; described in the text on PDF pp. 319-320): aerobic stations (AS) alternating one-for-one with resistance stations (RS) that alternate upper and lower body (Fig 5.25); one aerobic station followed by two resistance stations, \"again alternately exercising the upper and lower (or front/back, left/right, etc.) parts of the body\" (Fig 5.27); and one aerobic station followed by three resistance stations (Fig 5.28). Circuits aimed \"more on muscle endurance or strength, can begin with an aerobics station, then progress through a succession of resistance stations, also preferably sequenced in accordance with the Setchinov principle.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry.\n\n**When to apply** — Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference: in a football off-season circuit, a lower-body station (split squat) followed by an upper pull (row), then a lower-body hinge, then an upper push, follows the authors' rule.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Alternation helps local recovery, not whole-body fatigue: a circuit that alternates regions but has no rest still limits load and speed, which is the authors' main criticism of traditional circuits (see Related). For maximal strength or power work the authors want rest intervals, not just alternation (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). The authors give no data for how much the Setchinov effect improves a circuit.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 5.25, aerobic stations such as fixed cycles or skipping alternating with resistance stations, \"with the latter alternating between upper and lower body movements\" (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters]], [[framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[framework--non-commutative-non-associative-training-order]] (order of exercises changes the outcome), [[block-periodization--issurin/heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":7,"newStart":17,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the one in the name: diverting activity between exercises \"encourages restoration\" of the muscles just worked (PDF p. 319 / printed p. 308). They do not explain how. Inference: the Setchinov (Sechenov) active-rest effect is a classical observation that working other muscles during a rest period speeds recovery of the fatigued ones; the book cites no study for it here, and its size in modern studies is not checked in this entry."," ","-**When to apply** — Ordering any circuit or superset sequence so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Also usable for pairing exercises in a strength session with short rest. Inference: in a football off-season circuit, a lower-body station (split squat) followed by an upper pull (row), then a lower-body hinge, then an upper push, follows the authors' rule.","+**When to apply** — Ordering any circuit so that consecutive stations load different regions, which lets the athlete keep moving while each region recovers. Inference: the same rule can pair exercises in a superset within a strength session. Inference: in a football off-season circuit, a lower-body station (split squat) followed by an upper pull (row), then a lower-body hinge, then an upper push, follows the authors' rule."," "," **When NOT to apply** — Alternation helps local recovery, not whole-body fatigue: a circuit that alternates regions but has no rest still limits load and speed, which is the authors' main criticism of traditional circuits (see Related). For maximal strength or power work the authors want rest intervals, not just alternation (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304). The authors give no data for how much the Setchinov effect improves a circuit."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8cdec34d-042c-47f1-8e5a-5874f2a51609","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
