{"parentUuid":"cb2ee9ef-e94d-412d-af7a-4873ed3e50af","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbGjPZo4DGdNe3YEnK5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RMbmx1UURcwv4EBFvKDX4b","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\ndef sub(f, old, new):\n    t=open(f).read()\n    assert t.count(old)==1, (f, old[:50], t.count(old))\n    open(f,'w').write(t.replace(old,new))\nsub('claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md',\n '# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks',\n '# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Speed-Strength Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks')\nsub('claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md',\n 'explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).',\n 'explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image).')\nsub('claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits.md',\n '\"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300).',\n '\"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300, checked against the page image).')\nsub('heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md',\n 'The first rule is about order over a period (stages), not within one session;',\n 'Inference: the first rule reads as order over a period (stages), not within one session, since the authors introduce the rules as \"for the sequential use of training means over a given period,\" though the same list mixes session-level and stage-level rankings;')\nsub('framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md',\n '**Example** — The authors\\' own illustrations are in the four section entries:',\n '**Example** — Inference: the authors give no single example of all four principles. Their speed-strength sequence in 5.3.3 (general work, concentrated strength loading, technique on the strength block\\'s delayed effect, technique under competitive conditions; PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293) shows principles 2 and 4 in one plan. Their own illustrations of each principle are in the four section entries:')\nsub('heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md',\n 'Use the onset figure for when to expect a result',\n 'The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect\\'s duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result')\nsub('heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md',\n 'Distance Running Can Suit a Football Off-Season,',\n 'Distance Running Can Suit a Football Early Off-Season,')\nsub('principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md',\n 'presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).',\n 'presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296, checked against the page image).')\nprint('ok')\nEOF\ngrep -c \"\" /dev/null; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Apply six fixes and re-run lint"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11575,"cache_read_input_tokens":355712,"output_tokens":1965,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":442},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11575},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1965,"cache_read_input_tokens":355712,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11575,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RMbmx1UURcwv4EBFvKDX4b":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\ndef sub(f, old, new):\n    t=open(f).read()\n    assert t.count(old)==1, (f, old[:50], t.count(old))\n    open(f,'w').write(t.replace(old,new))\nsub('claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md',\n '# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks',\n '# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Speed-Strength Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks')\nsub('claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md',\n 'explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).',\n 'explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image).')\nsub('claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits.md',\n '\"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300).',\n '\"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300, checked against the page image).')\nsub('heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md',\n 'The first rule is about order over a period (stages), not within one session;',\n 'Inference: the first rule reads as order over a period (stages), not within one session, since the authors introduce the rules as \"for the sequential use of training means over a given period,\" though the same list mixes session-level and stage-level rankings;')\nsub('framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md',\n '**Example** — The authors\\' own illustrations are in the four section entries:',\n '**Example** — Inference: the authors give no single example of all four principles. Their speed-strength sequence in 5.3.3 (general work, concentrated strength loading, technique on the strength block\\'s delayed effect, technique under competitive conditions; PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293) shows principles 2 and 4 in one plan. Their own illustrations of each principle are in the four section entries:')\nsub('heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md',\n 'Use the onset figure for when to expect a result',\n 'The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect\\'s duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result')\nsub('heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md',\n 'Distance Running Can Suit a Football Off-Season,',\n 'Distance Running Can Suit a Football Early Off-Season,')\nsub('principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md',\n 'presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296).',\n 'presented to the body in a specific sequence during the STP (Sports Training Process) in order to accelerate adaptation\" (PDF p. 307 / printed p. 296, checked against the page image).')\nprint('ok')\nEOF\ngrep -c \"\" /dev/null; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Apply six fixes and re-run lint"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffbGPzWRwNBj5qr8ye3d","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f47e3ba8-a6c1-4942-bba7-f8c6c07e97b8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:07:41.325Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f47e3ba8-a6c1-4942-bba7-f8c6c07e97b8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"13b2e94d-1b2a-4672-944b-f2e4cd21284b","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RMbmx1UURcwv4EBFvKDX4b","type":"tool_result","content":"ok\n0\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"873718a1-fe0b-4fe5-9a69-992953802764","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:07:42.158Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"ok\n0\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":9,"newStart":11,"newLines":9,"lines":[" created: 2026-10-03"," ---"," ","-# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks","+# Too Much Heavy Strength Work Lowers Speed, Explosiveness and Technique; Speed-Strength Gains Stop After About 12-18 Weeks"," ","-**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"prolonged, heavy workloads slow the contraction speed of trained muscles\"; Filinov, \"excessively heavy strength loads diminish the force and speed of boxers' punches\"; Deniskin (1976), \"prolonged use of high volume strength loads diminishes the power and speed of weightlifters, high jumpers and triple jumpers.\" On dose: \"speed-strength and strength-speed cease to increase after a certain volume of heavy strength training has been executed. Deniskin observed this phenomenon after 18 weeks (54 workouts) of strength training. Other studies have also noted the same phenomenon after 50-60 training sessions or approximately 12 weeks of training.\" On technique, Siff writes (the passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person): \"research by Verkhoshansky and colleagues has established that excessive maximum strength training can impair speed-strength and technical skill in boxers (Verkhoshansky, 1977). It can also lead to a deterioration of several months' duration in the technique of weightlifters and javelin throwers\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291).","+**What it is** — The authors grant that general heavy resistance exercises \"enable the athlete to reach the necessary level of absolute strength, to provide general physical conditioning and to train the neuromuscular system non-specifically,\" but \"it is undesirable to use them extensively during specialised training phases, since they can diminish speed and explosiveness. High volume strength loads used after a phase of specialised jump training can substantially reduce the existing level of explosive strength (by as much as 30%)\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291; all figures in this entry checked against the page image). Supporting reports, all cited without dates except Deniskin: Kotz, \"prolonged, heavy workloads slow the contraction speed of trained muscles\"; Filinov, \"excessively heavy strength loads diminish the force and speed of boxers' punches\"; Deniskin (1976), \"prolonged use of high volume strength loads diminishes the power and speed of weightlifters, high jumpers and triple jumpers.\" On dose: \"speed-strength and strength-speed cease to increase after a certain volume of heavy strength training has been executed. Deniskin observed this phenomenon after 18 weeks (54 workouts) of strength training. Other studies have also noted the same phenomenon after 50-60 training sessions or approximately 12 weeks of training.\" On technique, Siff writes (the passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person): \"research by Verkhoshansky and colleagues has established that excessive maximum strength training can impair speed-strength and technical skill in boxers (Verkhoshansky, 1977). It can also lead to a deterioration of several months' duration in the technique of weightlifters and javelin throwers\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291)."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' explanation for the technique loss: \"The negative influence of heavy strength training on sports technique may be due substantially to deterioration of the speed-strength qualities of the neuromuscular system,\" and \"heavy training loads reduce one's ability to control movements, primarily of their most complex technical phases\" (PDF p. 302 / printed p. 291). For the plateau they give a dose argument only: \"there are certain volumes and durations of strength training that are essential for achieving an optimum training effect. Exceeding these optimal values renders any further increase in training volume not only unproductive, but detrimental.\" No cellular mechanism is given."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":7,"newStart":13,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," # Traditional Circuit Weight Training Gives Only Modest Mixed Benefits, Because Removing Rest Removes Heavy, Explosive and Stretching Work"," ","-**What it is** — The authors' verdict on the \"average commercial circuit\": \"only a moderate compromise of the reputed cardiovascular and strength conditioning benefits can be achieved by traditional circuit training\" (PDF pp. 311-312 / printed pp. 300-301). Specifically: circuit weight training (CWT) \"using combinations of gymnasium apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines cannot produce cardiovascular conditioning which rivals that of running, cycling and other traditional forms\"; \"because CWT generally relies on the use of light weights and high repetitions, increase in strength and power is also very limited\"; and \"Virtually the only significant benefits of the average commercial circuit are a modest development of local muscular endurance, moderate decrease in percentage bodyfat and a mild improvement in cardiovascular fitness.\" \"Alternation of conventional weight training on one day and endurance training on another day produces superior results to traditional circuit training in terms of improvements in strength, and cardiovascular stamina\" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301, checked against the page image). Circuit training is dated to \"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300).","+**What it is** — The authors' verdict on the \"average commercial circuit\": \"only a moderate compromise of the reputed cardiovascular and strength conditioning benefits can be achieved by traditional circuit training\" (PDF pp. 311-312 / printed pp. 300-301). Specifically: circuit weight training (CWT) \"using combinations of gymnasium apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines cannot produce cardiovascular conditioning which rivals that of running, cycling and other traditional forms\"; \"because CWT generally relies on the use of light weights and high repetitions, increase in strength and power is also very limited\"; and \"Virtually the only significant benefits of the average commercial circuit are a modest development of local muscular endurance, moderate decrease in percentage bodyfat and a mild improvement in cardiovascular fitness.\" \"Alternation of conventional weight training on one day and endurance training on another day produces superior results to traditional circuit training in terms of improvements in strength, and cardiovascular stamina\" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301, checked against the page image). Circuit training is dated to \"R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds\" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300, checked against the page image)."," "," **Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the rest interval. Circuit training \"operates on the assumption that it can simultaneously condition you aerobically and anaerobically,\" and since \"any rest intervals will tend to prevent the body from operating in the aerobic conditioning zone,\" it allows no rest beyond moving between stations (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301). \"Paradoxically, its major limitations are imposed by its very attempt to introduce aerobic conditioning into conventional weight training by eliminating rest intervals. The absence of rest periods prevents one from using heavy weights and developing strength, from holding static or resistance-assisted stretches to enhance flexibility, and from performing intensive explosive movements to improve speed\" (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image). Underneath is specificity: \"if one wishes to enhance strength, one has to do high intensity strength training with a minimum of prolonged aerobic training, since the latter tends to decrease strength\" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301)."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":6,"newStart":25,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," **When NOT to apply** — The premise is the authors' summary; the selective-adaptation idea is already in this library with its sources ([[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], 2.3.1). For novices, the authors' own premise says general adaptation dominates, so these principles, built for \"the high levels of sports proficiency,\" apply less. The principles are stated as a framework, not tested as a set. The term \"acceleration of specific adaptation\" carries two definitions in the chapter (see [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]])."," ","-**Example** — The authors' own illustrations are in the four section entries: Fig 5.16 (convergence), the children's sport-school stages (early specialisation by motor regime), Fig 5.17 (functional specialisation) and Figs 5.18-5.19 (replacing means). See Related.","+**Example** — Inference: the authors give no single example of all four principles. Their speed-strength sequence in 5.3.3 (general work, concentrated strength loading, technique on the strength block's delayed effect, technique under competitive conditions; PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293) shows principles 2 and 4 in one plan. Their own illustrations of each principle are in the four section entries: Fig 5.16 (convergence), the children's sport-school stages (early specialisation by motor regime), Fig 5.17 (functional specialisation) and Figs 5.18-5.19 (replacing means). See Related."," "," **Related** — [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]], [[framework--five-criteria-of-dynamic-correspondence]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — After any 4-8 week block that concentrates on one quality, plan a lower-load period before the next concentrated block. Inference: judge the block's result after the 1-3 week latency, not on the last day of loading. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 183, Inferred). Inference: the 1-3 week latency is a reason to leave slack between blocks in that roadmap, so a delayed gain is not cut short by the next block."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop.","+**When NOT to apply** — The 1-3 week figure is \"most commonly,\" with no study named. It is a latency to the onset of gains. Chapter 6 gives a different number for a different quantity: the long-term delayed training effect \"was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation,\" its duration \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage,\" and its appearance \"individualised\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image). **Contested across the library:** Bompa's entry [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]] attributes to Siff and Verkhoshansky that \"gains may appear 4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends.\" That matches neither Supertraining sentence: 5.3.3 says gains appear after 1-3 weeks; Chapter 6 says the effect lasts 4-12 weeks. The other 4-12 week figures in the book are also durations: Chapter 6 calls unidirectional training \"for prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" very effective (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer), and the Addendum says \"the tendency has been for the stages to last anywhere from 4-12 weeks,\" with the delayed effect's duration \"equal to the duration of the stage\" (PDF p. 536 / printed p. 525, text layer). Use the onset figure for when to expect a result and the duration figure for how long it may hold. Chapter 6 also qualifies the warning: during the emergence of the delayed effect \"it is relatively easy and painless for the body to change to intensive loading, but it will react negatively to extensive work\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364), so the restorative period is about volume, not a full stop."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: after a 4-8 week concentrated strength block, the gains \"may occur most commonly after a latency period of 1-3 weeks\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). Inference: an off-season strength block ending in late March would be judged in April, and the speed-emphasis block that follows would open at lower volume rather than at full load."," "]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":7,"newStart":11,"newLines":7,"lines":[" created: 2026-10-03"," ---"," ","-# No Supplementary Sport Is Simply Good or Bad: Distance Running Can Suit a Football Off-Season, Not Its Competitive Season","+# No Supplementary Sport Is Simply Good or Bad: Distance Running Can Suit a Football Early Off-Season, Not Its Competitive Season"," "," **What it is** — The authors reject fixed lists: \"Some authors have been swift to categorise certain sports as beneficial and others as detrimental as 'cross training' activities for specific sports. This restricted view, however, fails to take into account the fact that every sports training programme consists of distinct overlapping phases, each concentrating on developing and integrating different fitness qualities. For example, it is erroneous to label cross-country running as totally unsuitable for rugby or American football players. Although prolonged aerobic activity generally is inadvisable for use during the competitive season, it can have a place during the early off-season period, since it can improve peripheral vascularisation and recuperation after intense exercise. Similarly, weight training should not be dismissed from the training schedule of a distance runner, when it may be useful in strengthening certain muscles or enhancing local muscle endurance\" (PDF p. 310 / printed p. 299, checked against the page image). The same page carries the counterweight: \"combinations of predominantly cardiovascular activities such as regular distance running, cycling or swimming with weightlifting, rugby, or other strength sports may be counterproductive, since muscular endurance and cardiovascular fitness may be enhanced at the expense of strength, muscle mass and power,\" and \"inappropriate weight training using largely bodybuilding techniques might be detrimental to the marathoner or endurance athlete, especially if it fails to increase functional relative strength.\""," "]}]}],"moreFiles":2,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--accelerating-specific-adaptation.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f47e3ba8-a6c1-4942-bba7-f8c6c07e97b8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
