-sequence-system-of-training-means.md && grep -o '\\[\\[research-papers/[^]]*\\]\\]' framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md | sort -u; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky 2>&1 | tail -2","description":"Prefix Issurin 2016 link and re-lint"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffbJ1g92aMpcTD2yhkE4","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d306ab46-803e-454d-bcdf-a2d8b351f2bf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:07:54.928Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d306ab46-803e-454d-bcdf-a2d8b351f2bf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"13b2e94d-1b2a-4672-944b-f2e4cd21284b","agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PFeyHpE9b4kxuPLFY67mtE","type":"tool_result","content":"[[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"56bee798-5601-4506-8b20-684dec46fd6d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:07:55.624Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"[[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":8,"newStart":21,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Advanced athletes \"who are at a high level of special strength fitness,\" for whom the authors recommend \"primarily a complex system of means for: producing the specific training effect of the complex of means; preserving the magnitude of the training effect,\" the latter \"realised by the sequential replacement of one complex combination of means with another\" (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293). Eric's baseline: \"Training-compatibility is an explicit constraint on programming ... \"you can't ride two horses with one saddle.\" Compatible qualities are trained together; the rest are trained at a retention dose.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 182, Inferred). Inference: the speed-strength sequence (general work, concentrated strength, technique on the strength block's delayed effect) is the closest of the two to a football off-season, but the authors give it for \"speed-strength and complex-technique types of sport,\" not team sports."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Not for lower-level athletes on the authors' own account: research supporting the concurrent system was done on \"athletes of lower qualification,\" in whom \"the negative influence of the complex system is not apparent\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). **Contested by Issurin.** The library's summary of Issurin 2016 (*Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11) records that Issurin judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation (the reviewed studies improved some fitness measures but did not report team practice or competition outcomes), and presented the model as suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see [[issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors that concentration is for high-level athletes ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], PDF 44 / printed 33), so the dispute is over which sports the unidirectional version suits, not over concentration as such. Note also a naming overlap in the text: \"complex system\" is used for both the concurrent system the authors reject for elites (PDF p. 303) and the \"complex system of means\" they recommend for qualified athletes (PDF p. 304). The second means a complex of means with one primary emphasis, replaced as a whole over time.","+**When NOT to apply** — Not for lower-level athletes on the authors' own account: research supporting the concurrent system was done on \"athletes of lower qualification,\" in whom \"the negative influence of the complex system is not apparent\" (PDF p. 303 / printed p. 292). **Contested by Issurin.** The library's summary of Issurin 2016 (*Sports Medicine*, pp. 6-7 and 11) records that Issurin judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation (the reviewed studies improved some fitness measures but did not report team practice or competition outcomes), and presented the model as suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality and restricted where many interacting abilities decide performance (see [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Issurin 2008 agrees with the authors that concentration is for high-level athletes ([[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], PDF 44 / printed 33), so the dispute is over which sports the unidirectional version suits, not over concentration as such. Note also a naming overlap in the text: \"complex system\" is used for both the concurrent system the authors reject for elites (PDF p. 303) and the \"complex system of means\" they recommend for qualified athletes (PDF p. 304). The second means a complex of means with one primary emphasis, replaced as a whole over time."," "," **Example** — The authors' own speed-strength sequence: general development work, then concentrated strength loading, then in-depth technique work \"against a background of the delayed training effect of the strength block,\" then technique under competitive conditions (PDF p. 304 / printed p. 293)."," ","-**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin's multi-targeted alternative), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]","+**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[principle--sequentially-introduce-means-of-greater-training-effect]], [[heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]], [[framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin's multi-targeted alternative), [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d306ab46-803e-454d-bcdf-a2d8b351f2bf","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
