{"parentUuid":"1cf7c7e3-45b1-4c5e-92b9-d33389acf2a5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a07e06bc199bf998d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffbKfYiPfpPiu6jvRyox","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I covered all of batch 5c: 26 new entries for 5.3 to 5.6 (PDF 298 to 323). I stopped at the end of Chapter 5 and did not edit `_book.md` or any existing entry. The lint shows no issues in any of my 26 files.\n\n## Entries\n- **By kind:** 10 heuristic, 8 framework, 3 case-study, 3 principle, 2 claim.\n- **By confidence:** 14 supported, 9 contested, 3 untested, 0 proven.\n- **Untested:** the two circuit taxonomies, and early specialisation by motor regime, which rests only on Verkhoshansky 1963/1972 with no data.\n- **Scope file:** `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5c.txt` lists all 26.\n\n## Rows for `_book.md`\n| Section | PDF idx | Printed | Entries |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 5.3 lead-in | 298-301 | 287-290 | 5 |\n| 5.3.1 | 302 | 291 | 1 |\n| 5.3.2 | 302-303 | 291-292 | 1 |\n| 5.3.3 | 303-305 | 292-294 | 3 |\n| 5.4 lead-in | 305 | 294 | 1 |\n| 5.4.1 | 305-307 | 294-296 | 2 |\n| 5.4.2 | 307 | 296 | 1 |\n| 5.4.3 | 307-308 | 296-297 | 1 |\n| 5.4.4 | 308-309 | 297-298 | 1 |\n| 5.5 | 309-311 | 298-300 | 2 |\n| 5.6 | 311-323 | 300-312 | 8 |\n\nScope notes:\n- The two paragraphs at the top of PDF 298 are the end of 5.2.5. I left them for the 5.2.4-5.2.5 atomizer.\n- Two passages are already covered elsewhere, so I linked them instead of writing new entries:\n  - The ES-and-plyometrics finding on PDF 301 is already in the 4.2.1.6 ES heuristic.\n  - The opening of 5.4 (adaptation becomes selective as athletes improve) is already in the 2.3.1 transfer claim.\n\n## Contradictions and unclear spots (each recorded in its entry with both pages)\n- **Sequencing results, PDF 299 vs 300:** the experiments found no significant difference between strength-then-speed-strength and the reverse within one workout, and a larger effect for mixed sessions. The summary list a page later ranks mixed sessions second and strength-then-speed-strength in one session last.\n- **Fig 5.12 vs its text:** the text says combining short and long jumps summed their effects. The bars show the short-jump group beating the combined group at 30 m from a low start. Against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is about equal at two distances, above it at one and below it at one.\n- **\"Acceleration of specific adaptation\" has two definitions:** a macrocycle volume cut of up to 20% (PDF 305) and bringing forward the stage of peak proficiency (PDF 307).\n- **The 4-12 week figures:**\n  - 5.3.3 says gains from a concentrated block appear after a 1-3 week latency (PDF 305).\n  - Chapter 6 (PDF 375 and 381) and the Addendum (PDF 536) give 4-12 weeks as a duration, not an onset.\n  - The vault's Bompa entry says Siff and Verkhoshansky put gains at \"4 to 12 weeks after the concentrated block ends\", which matches none of these. That attribution needs checking.\n- **Rest intervals, PDF 313:** \"10-15 reps with over 80% of one's 1RM\". The page image confirms the wording, but the number is implausible, so I marked it unclear.\n- **Muscle-endurance circuit loads, PDF 315 and 317:** three figures that don't agree: 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM, about 50 reps at 50% of 1RM, and 40-50% of a 10RM, 5RM or 1RM.\n- **Circuits and heavy loading:** PDF 323 says circuits can't use near-maximal or explosive loading, but PDF 315 and 317 prescribe strength circuits at over 75-80% of 1RM.\n- **Noticed but not filed:**\n  - Fig 5.15 labels stage 1 \"Aerobic glycolysis\" while the text says \"Aerobic\".\n  - The PDF 298 sentence that anaerobic work \"creates favourable conditions for the training effect of aerobic work\" reads backwards against the sequence it follows.\n\n**Contested by another library source:** the conjugate sequence and concentrated-loading entries are set `contested` because the library's summary of Issurin 2016 judges the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for team, combat and swimming preparation. Issurin's 2008 book agrees with the authors that concentrated loading is for high-level athletes.\n\n**Voice:** two passages name Verkhoshansky in the third person and are attributed to Siff: PDF 302 (\"research by Verkhoshansky and colleagues\") and PDF 310 (the list of periodisation authors).\n\n## Pages checked against images\nEvery page I opened as an image: 298-302, 304-313, 315-317, 319-323 and 375. Every \"verified against the page image\" claim rests on one of them.\n- Images resolved several OCR misreads: \"amply\" for \"simply\", the garbled researcher names, \"I moderate\", and the interleaved sidebar on PDF 317.\n- I rendered 303, 314 and 318 but did not open them. Quotes from those pages come from the text layer, which reads cleanly there, and none of those quotes claims image verification. The PDF 381 and 536 cross-references are also text layer only.\n\nThere is nothing I couldn't read.\n\n## Reciprocal-link suggestions\nEach row names an existing file and the new entries it should link to.\n\n| Existing file | Should link to |\n|---|---|\n| Ch1 `claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals` | `claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits`, `heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities`, `heuristic--no-supplementary-sport-is-categorically-good-or-bad` |\n| 4.1.2 `framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy` | `framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means`, `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next` |\n| 4.1.2 `heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination` | `heuristic--replace-means-as-their-training-effect-fades` |\n| 4.2.1.6 `heuristic--electrostimulation-sequencing-dosage-and-safety-practicalities` | `case-study--deniskin-depth-jumps-after-resistance-and-rest-as-a-training-means` |\n| 2.3.1 `claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases` | `framework--four-principles-of-special-strength-training-organisation` |\n| 4.4 `framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness` | `framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks` |\n| 4.2.7.1 `framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines` | `heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines` |\n| 3.4.3 `claim--irradiation-effect-raises-prime-mover-force-and-endurance` | `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` |\n| `framework--post-activation-potentiation-load-timing-sequencing` | `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments` |\n| `heuristic--parachute-resistance-sizing-progression-and-contrast-release` | `framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour` |\n| Vault: Issurin `principle--concentration-of-training-workloads` | `framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means`, `framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading` |\n| Vault: Issurin `heuristic--exercise-sequencing-by-fatigue-state` | `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments` |\n| Vault: Issurin `case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay` | `claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique` |\n| Vault: Issurin `framework--long-term-preparation-four-stage-model-and-directions` | `heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport` |\n| Vault: Bompa `principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching` | `heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next`, plus a locator check on its 4-12 week attribution |\n| Vault: Bompa `framework--ltad-sequential-model` | `heuristic--early-specialisation-by-motor-regime-not-by-sport` |\n| Vault: Bondarchuk `claim--sprint-strength-exercise-transfer-ceiling` | `principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means` |\n| Vault: Myszka `framework--choosing-a-donor-sport` | `heuristic--choose-cross-training-by-fitness-qualities-and-phase-not-sport-label` |\n| Vault: `research-papers/issurin-2016-…` | `framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means` |\n\n## Lint output\n```\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe one flag is in `_book.md`, which the coordinator owns, and I didn't touch that file. Extra checks on my 26 files all pass:\n- each file has exactly the six section headers;\n- the only em dashes are in those headers, none in titles or body;\n- no `[[wikilink]]` wraps across lines;\n- every link resolves."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":730,"cache_read_input_tokens":370489,"output_tokens":3551,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":730},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3551,"cache_read_input_tokens":370489,"cache_creation_input_tokens":730,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":730,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffbK1Au6P3XJeuNXVFhY","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"be8be5f2-2a51-4ce8-a26a-efc401cdf2eb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T16:08:32.739Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","ver